window.WEBCARDS=[
{"l":"m10b","src":"https://download.boekhuis.nl/9789401814720_fragm-docb.pdf","card":{"h":"Official exam specification","p":["Source: PeopleCert ITIL Foundation (Version 5) Syllabus, Version 5.0 (February 2026). The format is confirmed on the PeopleCert exam page."],"li":["40 questions, each worth 1 mark. There is no negative marking, so answer every question.","Pass mark: 26 marks (65%).","60 minutes. Candidates taking the exam in a language that is not their native or working language may be awarded 25% extra time, that is 75 minutes in total.","Closed book. The Official Book is for study only and is NOT permitted in the exam.","Bloom's levels 1 and 2 only. Level 1 = recall (criteria worded Define, List, Know). Level 2 = understanding (Explain, Describe, Understand, Distinguish).","All questions are multiple choice with four options.","Exam languages: English, Chinese, French, German, Italian, Japanese, Polish, Portuguese (Brazil), Spanish."],"n":"The PeopleCert booking form asks for your native language. Extra time 'may be awarded', so confirm the exam duration (60 or 75 minutes) before exam day."},"guide":1},
{"l":"m10b","src":"https://download.boekhuis.nl/9789401814720_fragm-docb.pdf","card":{"h":"Question styles you will meet","p":["All questions are Objective Test Questions with four options. Distractors are plausible answers that a candidate with incomplete knowledge would be likely to choose."],"li":["Standard: a question stem and four answer options.","Missing word(s): a sentence with one or more words missing. Choose the missing word(s) from four options.","List: four statements, two of them correct. Choose the option with the correct pair, for example '1 and 3'. List questions are never negative.","Negative: a standard question worded negatively, for example 'Which is NOT...?'. Used only as an exception, where the learning outcome is to know that something is not done or should not occur."],"n":"Questions starting 'Identify the missing word(s)' in this app practise the missing-word style."},"guide":1},
{"l":"m10b","src":"https://download.boekhuis.nl/9789401814720_fragm-docb.pdf","card":{"h":"Exam weighting by syllabus category","tb":{"head":["Syllabus category","Weight","About this many of 40","Study in this app"],"rows":[["1. Key ITIL terms and definitions","30%","12","Modules 1 and 2, plus the continual improvement definition (Module 8)"],["2. The ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management","10%","4","Module 9"],["3. The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle","10%","4","Lifecycle in Modules 1 and 6, purpose of each activity in Module 6+"],["4. The ITIL Value System","40%","16","Modules 3, 4, 5 and 8, practices and operating models in Module 6, key terms and metrics in Module 6+"],["5. Value stream identification, mapping, and management","5%","2","Module 7, complexity thinking in Module 9"],["6. ITIL and AI","2.5%","1","Module 9 (AI)"],["7. ITIL and other frameworks","2.5%","1","Module 10"]]},"n":"The weightings are official (syllabus section 5). The question counts are 40 x weighting, calculated here: PeopleCert does not publish exact counts per category. Categories 1 and 4 together are 70% of the exam."},"guide":1},
{"l":"m10b","src":"https://itil.com/Itil-News-and-Announcements/itil-version-5-explained","card":{"h":"After you pass: renewal and next certifications","li":["ITIL (Version 5) certificates carry a three-year renewal date.","Renew by logging 20 CPD points per year for three consecutive years (PeopleCert Plus), by passing another certification from the ITIL product suite, or by retaking the same certification.","Practice Manager = Foundation + one practice module (Monitor, Support and Fulfil, or Plan, Implement and Control, or Collaborate, Assure and Improve) + ITIL Transformation.","Managing Professional = Foundation + ITIL Product, ITIL Service, ITIL Experience + ITIL Transformation.","Strategic Leader = Foundation + ITIL Strategy + ITIL Transformation.","ITIL Master = all three designations. ITIL Transformation is taken once and counts for every designation.","ITIL AI Governance is an extension module with no prerequisite and is not linked to any designation.","PeopleCert currently plans to sunset all ITIL 4 modules on 31 December 2027."]},"guide":1},
{"l":"m10b","src":"https://peoplecert.org/Partners/digital-services/Take2","card":{"h":"Take2 re-sit rules","li":["Take2 can be purchased up to 15 minutes before the exam. It is not available for purchase after a failed exam.","It becomes usable only if the linked exam result is a fail.","The re-sit must be scheduled and taken within 6 months of the first exam, through Online Proctoring.","It is exam-specific, non-transferable and non-refundable. Missing the re-sit appointment without rescheduling forfeits it."]},"guide":1},
{"l":"m10b","src":"https://itil.com/Itil-News-and-Announcements/itil-version-5-foundation-whats-new-guide","card":{"h":"What changed from ITIL 4 (official PeopleCert view)","p":["PeopleCert's ITIL architects describe ITIL (Version 5) as 'the result of evolution rather than revolution'. It keeps the guiding principles and the value system from ITIL 4."],"li":["New: the eight-activity ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model (discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support). Products and services are 'two sides of the same' solution.","The value chain is simplified. The six ITIL 4 value chain activities (plan, improve, engage, design and transition, obtain/build, deliver and support) are not part of ITIL (Version 5).","Two overriding principles at Foundation level: AI-native and complexity-native.","The service journey model, previously in ITIL 4 Drive Stakeholder Value, is now taught at Foundation level.","A refined continual improvement model.","The 34 management practices are organized into two groups: product and service management practices, and general management practices."],"n":"Practice tests that use ITIL 4 terms (service value chain, 'Did we get there?', technical practices) are not aligned with the Version 5 exam."},"guide":1},
{"l":"m2a","src":"https://download.boekhuis.nl/9789401814720_fragm-docb.pdf","card":{"h":"Terminology: 'digital product vendor'","p":["The syllabus calls the vendor role 'digital product vendor'. The course slides often say 'product vendor'. Both mean the organization responsible for the creation and continual improvement of digital products and related service offerings."]}},
{"l":"m2c","src":"https://itil.com/Itil-News-and-Announcements/itil-version-5-foundation-whats-new-guide","card":{"h":"Exam trap: the last journey step is 'Reflect'","p":["PeopleCert moved the service journey model from ITIL 4 Drive Stakeholder Value to Foundation level. In ITIL 4 the last step was 'Realize'. In ITIL (Version 5) it is 'Reflect'."],"steps":["Explore","Engage","Offer","Agree","Onboard","Co-create","Reflect"],"n":"Offboard is a branch at the Onboard stage (Figure 3.5), not one of the seven steps."}},
{"l":"m6c","src":"https://itil.com/Itil-News-and-Announcements/itil-version-5-explained","card":{"h":"Exam trap: no 'technical' practice group","p":["ITIL 4 grouped its 34 practices into three groups: general (14), service (17) and technical (3). ITIL (Version 5) uses two groups: general management practices (12) and product and service management practices (22)."],"li":["Deployment management, infrastructure and platform management, and software development and management were ITIL 4 technical practices. They are now product and service management practices.","Information security management and service financial management were ITIL 4 general practices. They are now product and service management practices."]}},
{"l":"m8a","src":"https://itil.com/Itil-News-and-Announcements/itil-version-5-foundation-whats-new-guide","card":{"h":"Exam trap: ITIL 4 step names","p":["ITIL (Version 5) uses a refined continual improvement model. Steps 6 and 7 are worded differently from ITIL 4, and some third-party 'Version 5' practice sites still use the old wording."],"tb":{"head":["Step","ITIL (Version 5): use this","ITIL 4: old wording"],"rows":[["6","Are we getting there?","Did we get there?"],["7","How do we keep the improvements relevant?","How do we keep the momentum going?"]]},"n":"Steps 1-5 are unchanged: What is the vision? -> Where are we now? -> Where do we want to be? -> How do we get there? -> Take action."}}];
window.QB=window.QB||[];
QB.push(
{"m":"m8","l":"m8a","w":1,"q":"In ITIL (Version 5), what is step 6 of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model?","o":["Are we getting there?","Did we get there?","How do we get there?","Where do we want to be?"],"e":"Step 6 is 'Are we getting there?' (course slides, Figure 5.4). 'Did we get there?' is the ITIL 4 wording that some third-party 'Version 5' sites still use. PeopleCert notes that Version 5 has a refined continual improvement model (itil.com, 'ITIL Foundation (Version 5), what's new?').","x":["ITIL 4 wording, not ITIL (Version 5).","This is step 4.","This is step 3."]},
{"m":"m8","l":"m8a","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nThe final step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model is 'How do we keep the improvements [ ? ]?'","o":["relevant","going","visible","measured"],"e":"Step 7 is 'How do we keep the improvements relevant?' (course slides). ITIL 4 used 'How do we keep the momentum going?', which is why 'going' can look familiar. Missing-word style per the PeopleCert syllabus v5.0.","x":["Echoes the ITIL 4 step 'How do we keep the momentum going?'.","Visibility supports improvement but is not part of the step name.","Measuring progress belongs to step 6, 'Are we getting there?'."]},
{"m":"m2","l":"m2c","w":1,"q":"What is the LAST step of the ITIL Service Journey Model in ITIL (Version 5)?","o":["Reflect","Realize","Co-create","Offboard"],"e":"The seven steps are Explore, Engage, Offer, Agree, Onboard, Co-create, Reflect (course slides, Figure 3.5). 'Realize' was the last step in ITIL 4 Drive Stakeholder Value, the module the journey model came from (itil.com, 'ITIL Foundation (Version 5), what's new?').","x":["ITIL 4 Drive Stakeholder Value wording.","Step 6, not the last step.","A branch at the Onboard stage, not one of the seven steps."]},
{"m":"m6","l":"m6c","w":1,"q":"In ITIL (Version 5), which practice group includes deployment management?","o":["Product and service management","Technical management","General management","Service operations management"],"e":"ITIL (Version 5) has two groups: general management practices (12) and product and service management practices (22). Deployment management is in the second group. In ITIL 4 it was a technical management practice, a group that no longer exists (itil.com FAQ 'New ITIL Explained', course practice lists).","x":["The ITIL 4 group. ITIL (Version 5) has no technical group.","Deployment management is not one of the 12 general management practices.","Not an ITIL practice group."]},
{"m":"m6","l":"m6c","w":1,"q":"Which TWO are product and service management practices in ITIL (Version 5)?\n1. Information security management\n2. Risk management\n3. Service financial management\n4. Supplier management","o":["1 and 3","1 and 2","2 and 4","3 and 4"],"e":"Information security management and service financial management are product and service management practices in ITIL (Version 5). In ITIL 4 both were general management practices. Risk management and supplier management are general management practices (course practice lists, itil.com FAQ).","x":["Risk management is a general management practice.","Both are general management practices.","Supplier management is a general management practice."]},
{"m":"m1","l":"m1a","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA [ ? ] is a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks.","o":["service","product","service offering","digital product"],"e":"Definition of service (course glossary). A product is a configuration of an organization's resources designed to offer value for a consumer. Missing-word style per the PeopleCert syllabus v5.0.","x":["A product is a configuration of resources designed to offer value for a consumer.","A service offering is a formal description of one or more services.","A digital product is a combination of resources based on digital technology."]},
{"m":"m2","l":"m2d","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA [ ? ] is a set of metrics that define expected or achieved service quality.","o":["service level","service level agreement","service offering","service value"],"e":"Service level: 'A set of metrics that define expected or achieved service quality' (course glossary). An SLA is the documented agreement that identifies the services and the agreed level of each service.","x":["An SLA is the documented agreement, not the set of metrics.","A service offering is a formal description of services.","Service value is the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of a service."]},
{"m":"m4","l":"m4a","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\n[ ? ] is the system by which an organization is directed and controlled.","o":["Governance","Management","Continual improvement","The operating model"],"e":"Governance: 'The system by which an organization is directed and controlled' (course glossary, syllabus 4.3.1).","x":["Management works within the direction set by governance.","Continual improvement is a recurring activity to keep meeting stakeholders' expectations.","An operating model represents how an organization co-creates value and runs itself."]},
{"m":"m6x","l":"a8","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA [ ? ] is a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents.","o":["problem","known error","error","major incident"],"e":"Problem: 'A cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents.' A known error is a problem that has been analysed but has not been resolved. An error is a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents (course glossary, syllabus 4.4.8 and 4.4.10).","x":["A known error is a problem that has been analysed but not resolved.","An error is a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents.","An incident is an unplanned interruption or quality reduction, not its cause."]},
{"m":"m6x","l":"a6","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nAny change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item is called an [ ? ].","o":["event","incident","alert","escalation"],"e":"Event: 'Any change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item' (course glossary, syllabus 4.4.3). An incident is an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service.","x":["An incident is an unplanned interruption or reduction in service quality.","An alert may report an event, but it is not the defined term.","Escalation is not a change of state of a configuration item."]},
{"m":"m7","l":"m7a","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nAn [ ? ] value stream enables value for internal customers to support the organization's core value streams.","o":["enabling","internal","operational","auxiliary"],"e":"Enabling value stream: 'A value stream that enables value for internal customers to support the organization's core value streams' (course glossary, syllabus 5.1.1). A core value stream enables value for consumers in a form intended by the operating model.","x":["'Internal' describes the customers, not the type of value stream.","Not an ITIL type of value stream.","Not an ITIL type of value stream."]},
{"m":"m8","l":"m8a","w":1,"q":"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nContinual improvement is a recurring organizational activity performed at all levels of an organization to ensure that the organization continually meets stakeholders' [ ? ].","o":["expectations","contracts","service level targets","budgets"],"e":"Continual improvement: 'A recurring organizational activity performed at all levels of an organization to ensure that the organization continually meets stakeholders' expectations' (course glossary). Syllabus 1.1.4 asks you to define it as a key concept.","x":["Too narrow: contracts are only one source of expectations.","Too narrow: service level targets are one way to express expectations.","Budgets are a constraint, not what continual improvement must meet."]}
);

;
// Official ITIL Foundation (Version 5) sample papers and workbook quizzes. Copyright PeopleCert International Limited. Personal study use.
// o[0] is the correct answer; o[1..3] are the other options in their official A-D order; pos = official position (0=A..3=D) of the correct answer.
// e = official rationale of the correct option, x[i] = official rationale of o[i+1] (the "A. Correct."/"B. Incorrect." labels are omitted).
window.OFFICIAL=[
{id:"S1",t:"Official sample paper 1",qs:[
{n:1,m:"m1",l:"m1b",syl:"1.2.4",ty:"std",q:"Which concept is illustrated when an employee is granted permission to the company’s new file-sharing platform for daily use?",o:["Access to resources","Warranty","Goods","Utility"],pos:3,e:"“[…] the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions.” In this case, the employee is granted permission to use the ‘resource’ of the file sharing platform. Ref 3.1.2",x:["“Warranty is the assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements” and is unrelated to granting permission to an employee. Ref 3.2.4","Transferring goods usually entails the transfer of “[…] goods from a service provider to a service consumer. In the case of digital services, this is a rare but possible form of service interaction”. Permission to use a platform is not the transfer of goods. Ref 3.1.2","“Utility is the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need. Utility can be summarized as ‘what the service does’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for purpose’.” Ref 3.2.4"]},
{n:2,m:"m1",l:"m1b",syl:"1.2.4",ty:"std",q:"An IT support engineer assisting a user in configuring their laptop is an example of which concept?",o:["Service actions","Transfer of goods","Sustainability","Access to resources"],pos:1,e:"Service actions are actions “[…] performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and a service consumer. The fulfilment of service requests is a common form of service actions. Other examples include delivery of goods, user training, consultations, and so on.” In this case, the service provider team has taken an action by assisting the user. Ref 3.1.2",x:["“Transfer of goods: some services include transfer of goods from a service provider to a service consumer. In the case of digital services, this is a rare but possible form of service interaction. Examples include transfer of equipment or consumables.” Assisting a user to configure a laptop is an example of service action, not an example of transferring goods. Ref 3.1.2","”Sustainability is the assurance that a product or service meets and will continue to meet the requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth.” Ref 3.2.4","“Access to resources: the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions.” Ref 3.1.2"]},
{n:3,m:"m1",l:"m1d",syl:"1.2.3",ty:"std",q:"What influences whether a service is perceived as valuable by a consumer?",o:["The extent to which the positive effects of the service outweigh the negative ones","The service provider’s ability to minimize their operational costs","The number of providers delivering the service to the consumer","The amount of money the service provider invests in resources"],pos:2,e:"“[...] services can introduce new costs and risks, and in some cases, they can negatively affect some of the intended outcomes, while supporting others. Services are perceived by service consumers as valuable only when they have more positive effects than negative, […].” Ref 3.1.1",x:["Operational costs do not concern the consumer, but the services provided “[…] can introduce new costs and risks [...] Services are perceived by service consumers as valuable only when they have more positive effects than negative, […]”. Ref 3.1.1","“Service providers help their consumers to achieve outcomes, and in doing so, take on some of the associated costs and risks. Service providers are usually specialized in specific types of services” and hence, the number of providers is not significant for the consumer. Ref 3.1.1","The amount of money invested in resources by a service provider is unrelated to the consumer’s assessment of value. “The value for the service consumer(s): is defined by their own needs; is achieved through the support of intended outcomes and optimization of the service consumer’s costs and risks; is subjective and influenced by the service experience; changes over time and in different circumstances.” Ref 5.2.1.1"]},
{n:4,m:"m1",l:"m1d",syl:"1.3.6",ty:"std",q:"Which of the following is TRUE regarding services and desired outcomes?",o:["Services may have unintended and surprising outcomes, both positive and negative","Services guarantee that all intended outcomes will be achieved","Services completely eliminate risks associated with achieving outcomes","Services always result in positive effects without exceptions"],pos:3,e:"“When enabling desired outcomes for service consumers, services may also prevent or reduce other desired outcomes or introduce undesired ones. […] In addition to the intended and anticipated positive and negative effect on service consumers, services may have unintended and surprising outcomes, both positive and negative.” Ref 3.1.1.1",x:["Services do not guarantee all outcomes; they may support some while hindering others. “When enabling desired outcomes for service consumers, services may also prevent or reduce other desired outcomes or introduce undesired ones. […] In addition to the intended and anticipated positive and negative effect on service consumers, services may have unintended and surprising outcomes, both positive and negative.” Ref 3.1.1.1","Services can mitigate or reduce risks, but they cannot remove them entirely. Whereas risks may be “[…] reduced or fully removed from a consumer by the service […]. In many cases, a service may only reduce a consumer’s risks, but the consumer may decide that this reduction is sufficient to support the value proposition” and, when “enabling desired outcomes for service consumers, services may also prevent or reduce other desired outcomes or introduce undesired ones […]. In addition to the intended and anticipated positive and negative effect on service consumers, services may have unintended and surprising outcomes, both positive and negative.” Ref 3.1.1.3, 3.1.1.1","Services may lead to both “positive and negative” effects, so outcomes are not always positive. “In addition to the intended and anticipated positive and negative effect on service consumers, services may have unintended and surprising outcomes, […].” Ref 3.1.1.1"]},
{n:5,m:"m2",l:"m2d",syl:"1.4.11",ty:"std",q:"A service provider commits to using recyclable components, reducing carbon emissions, and maintaining transparent supply chains.\nWhich concept does this commitment represent?",o:["Sustainability","Utility","Warranty","User experience"],pos:3,e:"“Sustainability requirements may include responsible sourcing of materials and components, responsible work and employment practices, use of clean energy, low carbon footprint, incorporation of recycled and recyclable materials, transparent supply chains, and others. These requirements may originate from national and international regulations and standards or be defined in the organization’s sustainability strategy.” Ref 3.2.4",x:["Utility is the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need. “Utility can be summarized as ‘what the service does’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for purpose’. To have utility, a service must either support the performance of the consumer or remove constraints from the consumer.” Ref 3.2.4","“Warranty is the assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements. Warranty can be summarized as ‘how the service performs’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for use’. Warranty typically addresses such areas as the availability of the service, its capacity, levels of security, and continuity.” Ref 3.2.4","“User experience is the sum of functional and emotional interactions with a service and service provider as perceived by a service user.” Ref 3.2.3"]},
{n:6,m:"m9",l:"m9b",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Which dimension of product and service management is directly influenced by leadership commitment to psychological safety and continual learning?",o:["Organizations and people","Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes","Information and technology"],pos:3,e:"The ‘Organization and people’ dimension is most concerned with establishing a culture of trust. “Digitally enabled organizations are under pressure to continually improve their performance under changing market conditions. […]. The challenge for these organizations is to foster an effective set of shared beliefs, perceptions, and values in relation to risks. This, in turn, should create a safety culture, and result in behaviour that is beneficial for all stakeholders, including the members of the organization.” Ref 2.2.1",x:["The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension focuses on external relationships and agreements, not on the internal culture of trust. It “[…] encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services. It also incorporates contracts and other agreements between the organization and its partners or suppliers.” Ref 2.5","The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension is concerned with workflows and efficiency. It “[…] addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3","“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4"]},
{n:7,m:"m9",l:"m9e",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Which dimension of product and service management addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows?",o:["Value streams and processes","Partners and suppliers","Information and technology","Organizations and people"],pos:3,e:"“The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension address organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3",x:["“The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services. It also incorporates contracts and other agreements between the organization and its partners or suppliers.” Ref 2.5","“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4","The ‘organizations and people’ dimension ensures that an organization’s “[…] roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, are well defined and support its overall strategy and operating model”. It also ensures “[…] the way an organization is structured and managed, […]”. Ref 2.2"]},
{n:8,m:"m9",l:"m9c",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Which dimension of product and service management addresses data and technologies used in digital product and services?",o:["Information and technology","Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes","Organizations and people"],pos:3,e:"“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4",x:["“The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services. It also incorporates contracts and other agreements between the organization and its partners or suppliers.” Ref 2.5","“The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3","The ’organizations and people’ dimension ensures that “[…] the way an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, are well defined and support its overall strategy and operating model.” Ref 2.2"]},
{n:9,m:"m9",l:"m9d",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Which dimension of product and service management is concerned with relationships with other organizations that are involved in discovery, design and continual improvement of product?",o:["Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes","Information and technology","Organizations and people"],pos:2,e:"“The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services.” Ref 2.5",x:["“The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3","“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4","The ‘organizations and people’ dimension focuses on the human and cultural aspects of service management. It ensures “[…] that the way an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, are well defined and support its overall strategy and operating model.” Ref 2.2"]},
{n:10,m:"m5",l:"m5a",syl:"4.2.2",ty:"std",q:"According to the ‘focus on value’ ITIL Guiding Principle, all organizational activities should link back to what?",o:["Benefits for organization, its customers, and stakeholders","Increased revenue only","Employee satisfaction","Market share dominance"],pos:0,e:"The ‘focus on value’ principle stipulates that all “[…] activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1",x:["Focusing solely on revenue is too narrow because financial gain is only one dimension of value. ‘focus on value’ stresses that value also includes outcomes like customer satisfaction, loyalty, reduced cost, and long-term opportunities, not just monetary profits. Different stakeholders may have a different value definition. “This value may come in various forms, such as revenue, customer loyalty, lower cost, or growth opportunities.” Ref 5.2.1","While employee satisfaction is important for overall success, it is an enabler rather than the ultimate value outcome. It is a contributing factor but not the overarching focus of organizational activities. Additionally, it is the role of leadership to “[…] engage, inspire, and motivate people by behaving with integrity to generate respect and empower employees”. Ref 2.2.2","Market dominance may contribute to value but is not the universal measure of success. ‘focus on value’ encourages creating value for stakeholders through outcomes like revenue, loyalty, reduced cost, or growth opportunities. “All activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1"]},
{n:11,m:"m5",l:"m5b",syl:"4.2.3",ty:"std",q:"According to the ITIL Guiding Principle ‘start where you are’, what is the MAIN risk of starting over without considering what is already available when improving a service?",o:["Wasting time and losing valuable existing capabilities and resources","Losing opportunities for collaboration and shared understanding across teams","Overcomplicating solutions by ignoring practical, simple approaches","Failing to gather feedback needed to guide gradual improvement"],pos:3,e:"When improving products or services, starting over completely is rarely wise. The approach of completely removing what has been done “[…] can be extremely wasteful, not only in terms of time, but also in terms of the loss of existing capabilities, competencies, people, and tools that could have significant value in the improvement effort. Do not start over without first considering what is already available to be leveraged.” Ref 5.2.2",x:["The concept of collaboration is unrelated to the ‘start where you are’ principle. “Collaboration that leads to real accomplishment requires information, understanding, and trust. Work and its results should be made visible, hidden agendas should be avoided, and information should be shared to the greatest degree possible.” Ref 5.2.4","Overcomplexity of solutions is dealt with in the ‘keep it simple and practical’ principle, not in ‘start where you are’. ”To avoid the ‘build trap’ when more and more features are built, overcomplicating the products, organizations should stay focused on value and keep their products and services simple and practical.” Ref 5.2.6","This has to do with the ‘progress iteratively with feedback’ principle which states that “Seeking and using feedback before, throughout, and after each iteration will ensure that actions are focused and appropriate, even in changing circumstances.” Ref 5.2.3.1"]},
{n:12,m:"m5",l:"m5c",syl:"4.2.4",ty:"std",q:"What is the MAIN purpose of using feedback throughout improvement iterations?",o:["To ensure that improvement actions remain relevant and effective in changing circumstances","To confirm that all stakeholder opinions are in agreement","To prevent the need for further adjustments once work has started","To avoid changes in project priorities during implementation"],pos:1,e:"Faster response to customer needs is one of the benefits of embedding feedback loops. “Working in a timeboxed, iterative manner with feedback loops embedded into the workflow allows for: […] faster responses to customer and business needs.” Ref 5.2.3.1",x:["While it is not necessary for all stakeholder opinions to agree, it is significant to work “[…] effectively with others, coordinating tasks, managing conflict within a group, sharing ideas, and respecting diverse opinions”. Also, ‘feedback’ which “[…] is information about stakeholder reactions and opinions, which is used as a basis for improvement” cannot be uniform. Ref 2.2.4, 5.2.3.1","Earlier detection of issues is one of the benefits of embedding feedback loops. Working in timeboxed iterations with feedback loops enables flexibility, quicker responses to needs, early failure detection, and improved overall quality. ”Working in a timeboxed, iterative manner with feedback loops embedded into the workflow allows for: […] the ability to detect and respond to failures earlier.” Ref 5.2.3.1","Changes in priorities are common when progressing iteratively with feedback and while “[…] the iteration is being undertaken, circumstances can change, new priorities can arise, and the need for the iteration may be altered or even eliminated. Seeking and using feedback before, throughout, and after each iteration will ensure that actions are focused and appropriate, even in changing circumstances.” And it is the role of leadership to “[…] focus on value by being prepared to adjust priorities and stay aligned with the overall vision”. Ref 5.2.3.1, 2.2.2"]},
{n:13,m:"m5",l:"m5f",syl:"4.2.7",ty:"std",q:"A design team is creating a new workflow and tries to account for every possible exception.\nWhat is the risk of designing the workflow in this way?",o:["Creating unnecessary complexity","Achieving stronger overall risk control","Delivering outcomes more quickly and consistently","Ensuring employees always follow processes without question"],pos:1,e:"“Trying to provide a solution for every exception will often lead to over-complication. When creating a product or a practice, designers need to think about exceptions, but they cannot cover them all. Instead, they should design rules that can be used to handle exceptions generally.” Ref 5.2.6",x:["While it may seem that handling every exception improves risk management, ‘keep it simple and practical’ says that it leads to over-complication. “Trying to provide a solution for every exception will often lead to over-complication” and this in turn would likely lead to weaker, not stronger risk control. Ref 5.2.6","A workflow doesn’t create outcomes, it creates outputs. “Having appropriate feedback loops between the participants of an activity gives them a better understanding of where their work comes from and where their outputs go. It also helps them see how their actions and outputs affect the outcomes, which, in turn, enables them to make better decisions.” Ref 5.2.3.1","The opposite is generally true as people must be empowered through “[…] trust, autonomy, and resources: this is more effective than overcomplicated procedures and controls, especially in complex situations.“ Ref 5.2.6.2"]},
{n:14,m:"m5",l:"m5g",syl:"4.2.9",ty:"std",q:"Why should organizations consider the interaction of ITIL Guiding Principles?",o:["Because principles are interdependent and complement each other","Because one principle is always superior to the rest","Because principles must be applied in a strict order","Because one principle is usually sufficient for any situation"],pos:0,e:"“In addition to being aware of the ITIL Guiding Principles, it is important to recognize that they interact with and depend upon each other. For example, if an organization is committed to progressing iteratively with feedback, it should also think and work holistically to ensure that each iteration of an improvement includes all the elements necessary to deliver real results. Similarly, making use of appropriate feedback is key to collaboration, and focusing on what will truly be valuable to the customer makes it easier to keep things simple and practical.” Ref 5.2.8",x:["”Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other. There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8","Principles do not follow a strict order. ”Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other. There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8","”Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other. There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8"]},
{n:15,m:"m4",l:"m4a",syl:"4.3.2",ty:"std",q:"What is the role of a governing body in an organization?",o:["It is accountable for performance and compliance at the highest level","It is responsible for day-to-day operations","It manages the technical details of digital products directly","It focuses exclusively on financial reporting"],pos:0,e:"“Every organization is directed by a governing body: a person or group of people who are accountable at the highest level for the performance and compliance of the organization.” Ref 5.3.1",x:["Day-to-day operations are handled by management, not governance. The governing body provides direction, but execution is delegated. The governing body is concerned with strategic direction, oversight, compliance, and accountability. Furthermore, it is the Management practices that “enable and support the value chain activities”. Ref 5.4.1 Figure 2.1","Technical details of digital products fall under operational and technical teams, not under the governing body. The governing body is concerned with strategic direction, oversight, compliance, and accountability. “If the purpose of an internal technology organization is ‘to support the core business of the company with effective and efficient technology solutions’, and business-critical digital products and services are developed and delivered internally, then the internal technology organization is likely to take responsibility for all activities of the digital product and service management.” Ref 5.4.2","Exclusive focus on financial reporting is too narrow. While financial accountability is part of governance, it also covers compliance, risk, digital technology use, strategic investment, and stakeholder value. “The governing body is accountable for the organization’s compliance with policies and external regulations. Governance of the use of digital technology is an important part of corporate governance” Ref 5.3.1"]},
{n:16,m:"m6x",l:"a1",syl:"4.4.11",ty:"neg",q:"Which of the following is NOT a success metric of the ‘discover’ activity?",o:["Service performance against the agreed SLA targets","Strategic fit of the organization’s products and service offerings","Market relevance of the products and service offerings","Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings"],pos:2,e:"“Service performance against the agreed SLA targets” is a key metric of the ‘deliver’ activity. The key metrics of success of the ‘discover’ activity are: “Strategic fit of the organization’s products and service offerings. Market relevance of the products and service offerings. Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings. Commercial success of the products (if applicable).” Ref 4.8.2, Table 4.7, 4.2.2, Table 4.1",x:["“Strategic fit of the organization’s products and service offerings” is a success metric of ‘discover’ activity. Ref 4.2.2, Table 4.1","Market relevance is a success metric of ‘discover’ activity: “Market relevance of the products and service offerings. Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings. Commercial success of the products (if applicable)”. Ref 4.2.2. Table 4.1","“Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings” is one of the success metrics of ‘discover’ activity. Ref 4.2.2, Table 4.1"]},
{n:17,m:"m1",l:"m1a",syl:"1.1.3",ty:"std",q:"What is a digital service?",o:["A service that fully or largely relies on digital products","A combination of technology resources designed for consumers","The transfer of goods from provider to consumer","A catalogue of services for consumers"],pos:0,e:"A digital service is \"a service that fully or largely relies on digital products.\" Ref 3.1.2",x:["This resembles the digital product definition: \"Digital product is a combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers.\" Ref 3.1.2","Transfer of goods is a form of service interaction: \"examples include delivery of goods, user training, consultations, and so on. This form of service interaction is very common in several industries such as hospitality, education, transport, and many others.\" Ref 3.1.2","A catalogue or description of services corresponds more closely to a service offering, not a digital service itself. “Service offering is a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group.” Ref 3.1.2"]},
{n:18,m:"m6",l:"m6b",syl:"4.4.12",ty:"std",q:"Which term describes 'what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why'?",o:["Organization’s purpose","Value stream","Value chain","Operating model"],pos:3,e:"An organization’s purpose is “[…] what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why.” Ref 5.4.1",x:["“Value stream is a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.” Ref 2.3.1","The ‘Value chain’ enables and supports the value streams, in addition, “[…] continual improvement of value streams is achieved indirectly by improving the value chain and the management practices.” Ref 6.4","The operating model is a “[…] conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself.” Ref 5.4.1"]},
{n:19,m:"m6",l:"m6b",syl:"4.4.12",ty:"std",q:"Which statement BEST explains the relationship between an organization’s purpose and its operating model?",o:["The organization’s purpose determines why it exists, while the operating model shows how it fulfils that purpose","The operating model defines the organization's financial strategy to achieve its purpose","The operating model replaces the organization’s purpose once the value chain is defined","The purpose and operating model are unrelated because one focuses on customers and the other on internal processes"],pos:1,e:"“Operating model is a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself.” Ref 5.4.1",x:["’Financial strategy’ is part of the ‘acquire’ activity, the “[…] supplier management, service financial management, and IT asset management.” Ref 4.4.2","“Value chain is an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service” while operating model “is a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself.” Ref 2.3.1, 5.4.1","“Operating model is a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself.” Ref 5.4.1"]},
{n:20,m:"m6",l:"m6c",syl:"4.5.3",ty:"std",q:"Which of the following describes a management practice?",o:["A set of organizational resources and capabilities designed to perform work or accomplish an objective","A series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products","A visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with stakeholders","A set of interconnected activities that an organization performs to create and deliver products"],pos:1,e:"“A management practice is a set of organizational resources and capabilities designed and adopted for performing work or accomplishing an objective.” Ref 5.5",x:["This is ‘value stream’: “[…] a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.” Ref 2.3.1","This is ‘operating model’ which is “[…] a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself”. Ref 5.4.1","This is value chain: “[…] an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service.” Ref 2.3.1"]},
{n:21,m:"m6",l:"m6c",syl:"4.5.4",ty:"std",q:"Which aspect of an ITIL Official Practice Guide describes the roles, competencies, and responsibilities needed for a specific practice?",o:["Organizations and people","Capability assessment and development","General information","Partners and suppliers"],pos:2,e:"The ‘organizations and people’ aspect of an ITIL Official Practice Guide describes: “Roles, competencies, and responsibilities” as well as “organizational solutions and teams (specific to the practice)” Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4",x:["Capability assessment and development contains a “Description of the capability levels according to ITIL Maturity Model […]. Capability criteria for the practice. Recommendations for self-assessment of the practice capability level”. Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4","‘General information’ covers: “Purpose and description; Key terms and concepts; Scope; Practice success factors; Key metrics” Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4","‘Partners and suppliers’ covers: “Dependencies of the practice on third parties” and “Support from third parties”. Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4"]},
{n:22,m:"m8",l:"m8a",syl:"4.6.2",ty:"std",q:"A company plans an improvement initiative and ensures it is clearly linked to organizational goals and objectives.\nWhich continual improvement step is being performed?",o:["What is the vision?","Where are we now?","Take Action","Where do we want to be?"],pos:0,e:"“Each improvement initiative should support the organization’s goals and objectives. The first step of the continual improvement model is to define the vision of the initiative. This provides context for all subsequent decisions and links individual actions to the organization’s vision for the future.” Ref 5.6.1.1",x:["In the \"where are we now\" step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model, the organization assesses existing products and services. “Depending on the scope of improvement, this may be an assessment of existing products and services, including user perception of value received, people’s competencies and skills, involved workflows, available information, and the capabilities of the available technological solutions.” Ref 5.6.1.2","In the ‘take action’ step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model, “[…] the plan for the improvement is acted upon. Agreed actions and experiments can be conducted as a sequence, simultaneously, or independently”. Ref 5.6.1.5","In the ‘where do we want to be’, step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model, the organization defines (Point B), “[…] the target state for the next step of the journey, […]” and what it “should look like. A journey step cannot be mapped out if the destination, or at least the direction, is not clear”. Ref 5.6.1.3"]},
{n:23,m:"m7",l:"m7b",syl:"5.3.2",ty:"neg",q:"Which of the following is NOT one of the steps in value stream mapping?",o:["Value stream management","Value stream identification","Mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream","Mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream"],pos:3,e:"This is not a step in value stream mapping. “Value stream mapping includes the following steps: value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements.” An organization’s purpose is a general term describing “[…] what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why”. Ref 6.3, Glossary",x:["This is one of the steps in value stream mapping. Value stream mapping includes the following steps: “value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements”. Ref 6.3","This is one of the steps in value stream mapping. Value stream mapping includes the following steps: “value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements”. Ref 6.3","This is one of the steps in value stream mapping. Value stream mapping includes the following steps: “value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements”. Ref 6.3"]},
{n:24,m:"m1",l:"m1a",syl:"1.1.3",ty:"std",q:"What is a digital product?",o:["A combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers","A formal description of one or more services designed to address consumer needs","The access to the service provider’s resources and their use according to the agreed terms and conditions","Actions performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and consumer"],pos:2,e:"\"Digital products are a combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers.” Ref 4.5.1",x:["This is the definition of service offering: \"[…] a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group. A service offering may include goods, access to resources, and service action\". Ref 3.1.2","This is the definition of access to resources: \"[…] the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions\". Ref 3.1.2","This is the definition of service action: \"[…] an action performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and a service consumer.\" Ref 3.1.2"]},
{n:25,m:"m6x",l:"a3",syl:"4.4.11",ty:"std",q:"Which of the following is a key success metric for the ‘acquire’ activity?",o:["Quality of the resources and services sourced by suppliers","Number and impact of incidents and performance deviations","Service performance against the agreed SLA targets","Negative impact of changes on service availability and performance"],pos:1,e:"The key metrics of the ‘acquire’ activity are: “Quality of the resources and services. Acquire cycle time. Adherence to the product roadmap and other relevant guidelines. Stakeholder satisfaction with the ‘acquire’ activity, timelines, and acquired resources”. Ref 4.4.2, Table 4.3",x:["“Number and impact of incidents and performance deviations” is a key metric of success in the ‘operate’ activity. Ref 4.7.2, Table 4.6","“Service performance against the agreed SLA targets” is a key metric of success in the ‘deliver’ activity. Ref 4.8.2, Table 4.7","This metric is related to the ‘transition’ activity, where minimizing disruption during deployment is important. “What are the key metrics of success? Negative impact of transition on service availability and performance.” Ref 4.6.2, Table 4.5"]},
{n:26,m:"m1",l:"m1a",syl:"1.1.3",ty:"std",q:"Which BEST describes the relationship between digital services and digital products?",o:["Digital services are always based on digital products","Digital products are always based on digital services","Digital services and digital products exist independently","Digital products are service actions performed by providers"],pos:1,e:"\"Digital services are always based on digital products. Products provide capabilities, while service offerings describe the potential value they offer to customers.\" Ref 3.1.2",x:["The opposite is true: \"Digital services are always based on digital products. Products provide capabilities, while service offerings describe the potential value they offer to customers\". Ref 3.1.2","They are interdependent, not independent: \"Digital services are always based on digital products. Products provide capabilities, while service offerings describe the potential value they offer to customers.\" Ref 3.1.2","Service actions are a separate concept. They are actions \"[…] performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and a service consumer. The fulfilment of service requests is a common form of service actions\". Ref 3.1.2"]},
{n:27,m:"m2",l:"m2a",syl:"1.4.4",ty:"std",q:"Which role takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption?",o:["Customer","Organization","Sponsor","Service provider"],pos:2,e:"“Customer is the role that defines the requirements for products and services and takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption.\" Ref 3.2.1.1",x:["An organization is a broad entity with functions and responsibilities, but it is not specifically the role accountable for service consumption outcomes. “Organization is a person or a group of people that has its own functions with responsibilities, authorities, and relationships to achieve its objectives.” Ref 3.2.1","The sponsor \"is the role that authorizes budget for service consumption.\" Ref 3.2.1.1","The service provider is responsible for delivering and supporting services, not for the consumer’s outcomes. “Service provider is an organization responsible for the delivery and support of services.” Ref 3.2.1"]},
{n:28,m:"m2",l:"m2b",syl:"1.4.2",ty:"std",q:"Which type of service relationship typically focuses on support and efficiency through standardized services?",o:["Basic relationship","Cooperative relationship","Collaborative relationship","Partner relationship"],pos:0,e:"The 'typical focus' of the 'basic relationship' is “support and efficiency”. Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1",x:["The 'typical focus' of the 'cooperative relationship' is \"improvement and effectiveness\". Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1","The ‘typical focus‘ of the ‘collaborative relationship‘ is \"innovation and growth\". Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1","A ‘partner relationship’ is another name for 'collaborative' and its focus is “innovation and growth”, not support and efficiency. Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1"]},
{n:29,m:"m2",l:"m2c",syl:"1.4.3",ty:"std",q:"Which statement about the ‘band of visibility’ is CORRECT?",o:["It represents all aspects visible between provider and consumer organizations","It includes only the activities visible to the service provider","It applies exclusively to modern, technology-based digital service models","It defines the complete path of the customer’s service journey"],pos:2,e:"The 'band of visibility' \"[…] is the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3",x:["Visibility is not about access levels, it is about \"[…] the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3","Visibility is not exclusive to technology-based digital service models, but is \"[…] the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3","Visibility is unrelated to contract timelines. It is \"[…] the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3"]},
{n:30,m:"m6x",l:"a1",syl:"3.2.1",ty:"std",q:"What is the MAIN purpose of the 'discover' activity?",o:["To align product capabilities with consumer needs and strategy","To develop and test digital products","To secure and allocate resources efficiently","To maintain and monitor digital products"],pos:2,e:"The main purpose of the 'discover' activity \"[…] is to ensure continual alignment of the product roadmaps and related service offerings with the needs of service consumers and with the organizational strategy\". Ref 4.2.1",x:["Developing and testing is part of the 'build' activity. \"The purpose of the ‘build’ activity is to develop, integrate, and test digital products, transforming designs into functional solutions.\" Ref 4.5.1","Securing resources belongs to the 'acquire' activity. \"The purpose of the ‘acquire’ activity is to secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring the sustainability and scalability of products and services.\" Ref 4.4.1","Maintaining and monitoring digital products is the purpose of the 'operate' activity: \"The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.\" Ref 4.7.1"]},
{n:31,m:"m6x",l:"a2",syl:"3.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Which lifecycle management activity is focused on creating prototypes and specifications?",o:["Design","Deliver","Support","Transition"],pos:0,e:"\"The purpose of the ‘design’ activity is to create prototypes and specifications for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework.\" Ref 4.3.1",x:["\"The purpose of the ‘deliver’ activity is to provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain service quality standards, and gather consumer feedback.\" Ref 4.8.1","\"The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.\" Ref 4.9.1","\"The purpose of the ‘transition’ activity is to seamlessly introduce new or updated products into operational environments and to ensure effective onboarding/offboarding of suppliers.\" Ref 4.6.1"]},
{n:32,m:"m6x",l:"a3",syl:"3.2.3",ty:"std",q:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of the 'acquire' activity?",o:["Secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently","Resolve incidents and fulfil recovery procedures","Provide services and manage user onboarding","Create functional prototypes for testing"],pos:1,e:"\"The purpose of the ‘acquire’ activity is to secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring the sustainability and scalability of products and services.\" Ref 4.4.1",x:["This describes the 'support' activity: \"The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.\" Ref 4.9.1","Service provision is the primary goal of the 'deliver' activity: \"The purpose of the ‘deliver’ activity is to provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain service quality standards, and gather consumer feedback.\" Ref 4.8.1","Creating functional prototypes for testing is the primary goal of the 'design' activity: \"The purpose of the ‘design’ activity is to create prototypes and specifications for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework.\" Ref 4.3.1"]},
{n:33,m:"m6x",l:"a8",syl:"3.2.8",ty:"std",q:"Which lifecycle management activity focuses on minimizing the impact of incidents and disasters when they happen?",o:["Support","Transition","Operate","Design"],pos:2,e:"\"The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.\" Ref 4.9.1",x:["'Transition' has to do with onboarding and offboarding of suppliers: \"The purpose of the ‘transition’ activity is to seamlessly introduce new or updated products into operational environments and to ensure effective onboarding/offboarding of suppliers.\" Ref 4.6.1","The 'operate' activity maintains system performance: \"The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.\" Ref 4.7.1","“The purpose of the ‘design’ activity is to create prototypes and specifications for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework.\" Ref 4.3.1"]},
{n:34,m:"m6x",l:"a2",syl:"4.4.2",ty:"std",q:"What does a product specification PRIMARILY describe?",o:["Critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics of a product","The initial creation used for testing a product’s design","The ability of a system to remain operational","The process of merging code into a central repository"],pos:0,e:"“Product/service specification is a detailed document that outlines critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics of a product or service to be built. It typically includes descriptions of the product or service features, functionalities, technical requirements, performance criteria, and user interface details.\" Ref 4.3.1",x:["This refers to a 'product prototype' which \"[…] is an initial creation of a product or service that demonstrates its basic form, functionality, and operational capabilities. It is used to test and refine the product or service design and functionality and test hypotheses.\" Ref 4.3.1","This is reliability, not specification. \"Reliability is the ability of a product, service, or other configuration item to perform its intended function for a specified period of time or number of cycles.\" Ref 4.7.1","This is continuous integration, not specification. \"Continuous integration is a set of techniques and tools that enables developers to frequently merge their code changes into a central repository, followed by automated builds and tests.\" Ref 4.5.2"]},
{n:35,m:"m6x",l:"a6",syl:"4.4.3",ty:"std",q:"What is an incident in IT services?",o:["An unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in service quality","Any change of state significant for management","A cause of one or more interruptions","A flaw or vulnerability in a service"],pos:1,e:"“Incident is an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service.\" Ref 4.7.2",x:["This is an 'event', not an incident. “Event is any change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item.\" Ref 4.7.2","This defines a 'problem' which is \"[…] a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents.\" Ref 4.9.1","This defines error: \"[…] a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents\". Ref 4.9.1"]},
{n:36,m:"m10",l:"m10a",syl:"7.1.1",ty:"std",q:"How does ITIL collaborate with DevOps practices to support digital product and service management?",o:["By providing the framework while DevOps supplies practices and techniques","By replacing DevOps practices with standardized service management processes","By limiting DevOps usage to software development activities","By positioning DevOps as a governance framework over ITIL"],pos:0,e:"“DevOps is a valuable complementary approach which should be used as a source of detailed practical advice enabling such activities of the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle” explaining that ITIL provides the framework while DevOps supplies practices and techniques. Ref 7.3.1",x:["“DevOps is a valuable complementary approach […]” indicates DevOps is not replaced but used alongside ITIL. Ref 7.3.1","“DevOps practices […] enabling such activities of the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle as ‘discover’, ‘design’, ‘acquire’, ‘build’, ‘transition’, and ‘operate’” shows DevOps is not limited to development only. Ref 7.3.1","“[…] ITIL® provides the framework for effective digital product and service management, […]” clarifies governance is provided by ITIL, not DevOps. Ref 7.3"]},
{n:37,m:"m9",l:"m9c",syl:"6.2.2",ty:"std",q:"How is the ITIL AI Capability Model intended to be used by organizations?",o:["To assess readiness and identify areas for improvement in using AI","To benchmark AI tools against predefined industry standards","To automate governance decisions across the ITIL Value System","To prioritize AI investments based on financial return"],pos:1,e:"The ITIL AI Capability Model is a functional classification model that helps organizations understand how AI can be applied, communicate its use, and improve governance and capability. “The model provides a functional classification of AI solutions. It helps understand and communicate the range of possible applications of AI. It can also enhance AI governance by helping organizations tailor risk profiles, controls, and countermeasures to the specific functions of AI solutions, […].” Ref 2.4.1.1",x:["The AI Capability Model is not a benchmarking or compliance standard. “The model provides a functional classification of AI solutions.” Ref 2.4.1.1","The model supports governance decisions but does not automate them. “It can also enhance AI governance by helping organizations tailor risk profiles, controls, and countermeasures […].” Ref 2.4.1.1","Financial prioritization or ROI analysis is not the purpose of the model. “The model provides a functional classification of AI solutions.” Ref 2.4.1.1"]},
{n:38,m:"m2",l:"m2d",syl:"1.4.6",ty:"std",q:"What BEST describes a Service Level Agreement (SLA)?",o:["A documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies services and their agreed levels","A legal contract between two vendors regulating financial penalties","An informal discussion between users and service providers about expectations","A guideline document for internal IT teams without customer involvement"],pos:1,e:"“Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service.\" Ref 3.2.5",x:["SLAs are not legal contracts focused on penalties, they are a \"[…] documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service\". Ref 3.2.5","SLAs are formal documented agreements, not informal discussions: \"[…] a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service\". Ref 3.2.5","SLAs involve both providers and customers, not just internal IT. An SLA \"[…] is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service\". Ref 3.2.5"]},
{n:39,m:"m3",l:"m3a",syl:"4.1.1",ty:"std",q:"Which ITIL concept includes governance, practices, and continual improvement?",o:["The ITIL Value System","The 'deliver' and 'support' value chain activities","The 'focus on value' ITIL Guiding Principle","The 'value streams and processes' dimension"],pos:0,e:"“The ITIL Value System (ITIL VS) is a model representing how all the components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation through digital products and services.” Ref 5.1",x:["The ‘deliver’ and ‘support’ activities are only two value chain activities within the lifecycle and do not encompass governance, practices, and continual improvement. “The lifecycle activities include: […] Deliver, which provides services to users and Support, which resolves issues and maintains service quality.” Ref 4.10","The ‘focus on value’ is a principle stating that all “[…] activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1","The ‘value streams and processes’ are ”workflows used by the organization to structure its value-creating activities”. Ref 5.4.1"]},
{n:40,m:"m7",l:"m7b",syl:"5.1.2",ty:"std",q:"What is the MAIN purpose of value stream mapping in an organization?",o:["To identify and prioritize improvement opportunities","To automate service monitoring","To enhance and support management practices","To control external stakeholders directly"],pos:1,e:"“Value stream mapping allows organizations to identify, map, and analyse their value streams, as well as identify and prioritize improvement opportunities.\" Ref 6.4",x:["Automation supports value stream management, not mapping: \"This understanding is supported by automation and measurement.\" Ref 6.4","It is the management practices \"[…] that enable and support the value streams, as well as the organization’s products and services. In many cases, the improvement of a management practice has impact on multiple value streams\", not the other way round. Ref 6.4","External dependencies cannot be directly controlled, but the contractual agreements may be altered to \"[…] include changes to contracts with suppliers, ways of using external services, changing the ways of cooperation\". Ref Appendix E"]}
]},
{id:"S2",t:"Official sample paper 2",qs:[
{n:1,m:"m1",l:"m1a",syl:"1.1.2",ty:"std",q:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of a product in ITIL product and service management?",o:["To offer value to consumers through a defined configuration of resources","To replace service management practices with product management","To enable value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that customers want to achieve","To ensure regulatory compliance across digital services"],pos:0,e:"A product is a configuration of an organization’s resources that is designed to offer value. “Digital product is a combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers.” Ref 3.1.2",x:["The emphasis is on integration, not replacement of product and service management disciplines. “ITIL offers an end-to-end Lifecycle Model for digital products and services, where the concepts of digital product and service are integrated.” Ref 1.2","This is the definition of a service, not a product. “Service is a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks.” Ref 3.1.1","This has to do with governance and information security management, not with the purpose of a product. “Governance of digital technology is a human-based system by which the current and future use of digital technology is governed.” Ref 5.3.1"]},
{n:2,m:"m1",l:"m1a",syl:"1.1.2",ty:"std",q:"What is the PRIMARY role of a digital service?",o:["To enable value co-creation by facilitating customer outcomes","To define processes and workflows for value creation","To replace product management practices with service management","To ensure compliance with policies and external regulations"],pos:1,e:"The core purpose of a service (including a digital service) is to enable value co-creation by helping consumers achieve desired outcomes, while relieving them of the need to manage certain costs and risks. “Service is a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks.” Ref 3.1.1",x:["This describes processes and value streams, which are part of the ‘value streams and processes’ dimension, not the role of a digital service. “Value stream is a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.” Ref 2.3.1","Digital services do not replace product management. ITIL integrates product and service management into a unified approach. “ITIL offers an end-to-end Lifecycle Model for digital products and services, where the concepts of digital product and service are integrated.” Ref 1.2","This describes governance of digital technology, which oversees compliance and control. “Governance is the system by which an organization is directed and controlled.” Ref 5.3.1"]},
{n:3,m:"m1",l:"m1a",syl:"1.1.3",ty:"std",q:"Which option is CORRECT in the context of a digital product and a digital service?",o:["A digital service enables value through the use of digital products","A digital product replaces the need for services","A digital service is limited to internal IT systems","A digital service is independent of a digital product"],pos:0,e:"In ITIL, a digital service is a service that relies on digital products. Digital products provide the capabilities, while the digital service uses those capabilities to enable value co-creation for consumers. “Digital service is a service that fully or largely relies on digital products.” Ref 3.1.2",x:["Products support services; they do not eliminate them. “Products provide capabilities, while service offerings describe the potential value they offer to customers.” Ref 3.1.2","Digital services are not limited to internal IT. Digital services are delivered to external customers, users, and organizations, across many industries. “Today, the services sector plays a crucial role in the modern economy […] including: […] Digital services.” Ref 3.1","A digital service cannot exist independently of a digital product, because it relies on digital products to function. “Digital services are always based on digital products.” Ref 3.1.2"]},
{n:4,m:"m1",l:"m1b",syl:"1.2.4",ty:"std",q:"A printer is delivered to a customer, while ongoing maintenance and support are provided by the supplier.\nWhich combination of service interactions is shown in this situation?",o:["Transfer of goods and service actions","Access to resources and service actions","Service offering and service actions","Transfer of goods and service journey"],pos:2,e:"The printer being delivered is a transfer of goods, while the ongoing maintenance and support provided by the supplier represent service actions. ITIL defines these as two distinct forms of service interaction, and both are present here. “Transfer of goods: some services include transfer of goods from a service provider to a service consumer.” “Service action: An action performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and a service consumer.” Ref 3.1.2",x:["“Access to resources” applies when ownership is not transferred and the consumer is granted usage rights (e.g., cloud services, rented equipment). “Access to resources: the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions.” Ref 3.1.2","A service offering is a description of services, not a form of interaction. “Service offering is a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group.” Ref 3.1.2","While a transfer of goods does occur, a service journey describes the entire sequence of interactions over time, not a specific type of interaction. “Service journey is the sum of activities and interactions performed by organizations engaged in service relationships to fulfil their roles as a service provider and a service consumer.” Ref 3.2.3"]},
{n:5,m:"m1",l:"m1b",syl:"1.2.4",ty:"std",q:"How does ‘access to resources’ support value co-creation in a service interaction?",o:["By allowing consumers to use provider resources","By transferring responsibility for resources to consumers","By limiting resource use to internal teams","By replacing service actions with automation"],pos:0,e:"In ITIL, access to resources is a specific form of service interaction where the service consumer uses the service provider’s resources under agreed conditions. This access enables value co-creation because consumers can achieve outcomes without owning or managing those resources. “Access to resources: the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions.” Ref 3.1.2",x:["Access to resources does not transfer responsibility for managing resources to the consumer. Consumers do not have to manage specific costs and risks related to those resources. “Service is a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks.” Ref 3.1.1","Access to resources is specifically about service consumers using provider resources. Limiting access to internal teams would prevent value co-creation with consumers. “Access to resources: the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions.” Ref 3.1.2","Automation may support access to resources, but access to resources does not replace service actions. Multiple forms of service interaction may coexist. “According to the definition of service offering, there are three forms of interaction between a service consumer and a service: Transfer of goods […] Service action […] Access to resources.” Ref 3.1.2"]},
{n:6,m:"m1",l:"m1d",syl:"1.3.5",ty:"std",q:"Which of the following is TRUE about value co-creation?",o:["Value is created jointly through interactions between service providers and service consumers","Value is created only by the service provider and delivered to the consumer","Value is defined and consumed by a service provider","Value is created independently of service relationships"],pos:3,e:"Value is co-created, it does not exist unless both parties participate. “It is always a combination of service consumption (by the service consumer) and service provision (by the service provider).” Ref 3.1.1",x:["Value is not produced unilaterally by the provider and “handed over” to the consumer. Value depends on how the service is used and experienced by the consumer. “[…] the co-creation of value through services has two perspectives: It is always a combination of service consumption (by the service consumer) and service provision (by the service provider).” Ref 3.1.1.1","Value is perceived by the service consumer, not defined or consumed by the provider. “Value is the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something.” Ref 3.1.1","Value co-creation occurs through service relationships. Without a service relationship, value through services cannot be realized. “Service relationships include service provision, service consumption, and service relationship management.” Ref Glossary"]},
{n:7,m:"m2",l:"m2d",syl:"1.4.11",ty:"std",q:"How do ‘utility’ and ‘warranty’ together support value co-creation?",o:["Utility ensures the service is fit for purpose, while warranty ensures it is fit for use","Utility focuses on cost control, while warranty focuses on risk avoidance","Utility and warranty apply only to products, not services","Utility ensures the service is fit for use, while warranty ensures it is fit for purpose"],pos:3,e:"Utility and warranty are two complementary aspects of service value. Utility describes what the service does (fitness for purpose), while warranty describes how the service performs (fitness for use). “Utility is the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need.” “Warranty can be summarized as ‘how the service performs’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for use’.” Ref 3.2.4",x:["While costs and risks are part of service value, utility and warranty are defined in terms of functionality and assurance. “Service value is the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of a service.” Ref 3.1.1","ITIL applies utility and warranty to both services and products. “Utility is the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need.” “Warranty is the assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements.” Ref 3.2.4","Utility relates to fit for purpose, while warranty relates to fit for use, not the other way around. “Utility can be summarized as ‘what the service does’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for purpose’.” “Warranty can be summarized as ‘how the service performs’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for use’.” Ref 3.2.4"]},
{n:8,m:"m1",l:"m1d",syl:"1.3.6",ty:"std",q:"Why does ITIL consider outcomes, costs, and risks together when explaining value co-creation?",o:["Because value is created when desired outcomes are achieved while costs and risks are optimized","Because outcomes eliminate the need to manage costs and risks","Because costs are more important than outcomes","Because risks are more important than outcomes"],pos:0,e:"ITIL explains value co-creation as a balance between outcomes, costs, and risks. Value is not created by outcomes alone; it emerges when desired outcomes are achieved while costs and risks are appropriately optimized for the service consumer. These three elements must be considered together to understand whether a service is truly valuable. “Service value is the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of a service. Achieving desired outcomes requires resources (and therefore costs) and is often associated with risks. Service providers help their consumers to achieve outcomes, and in doing so, take on some of the associated costs and risks.” Ref 3.1.1",x:["Outcomes do not exist independently of costs and risks. “Achieving desired outcomes requires resources (and therefore costs) and is often associated with risks.” Ref 3.1.1","Outcomes are central to value creation, while costs are one of the factors that influence whether value is perceived as positive or negative. Outcomes are supported by acceptable costs and risks. “Achieving desired outcomes requires resources (and therefore costs) and is often associated with risks.” Ref 3.1.1","Risks are evaluated in relation to their impact on achieving outcomes. “Service providers help their consumers to achieve outcomes, and in doing so, take on some of the associated costs and risks.” Ref 3.1.1"]},
{n:9,m:"m2",l:"m2c",syl:"1.4.3",ty:"std",q:"What does a service journey describe?",o:["End-to-end interactions between a service provider and a service consumer","The internal workflows and processes of a service provider","The end-to-end lifecycle of a product","The sequence of value chain activities"],pos:0,e:"A service journey is the sum of activities and interactions between a service provider and a service consumer across their relationship. It focuses on interactions, not just internal work or lifecycle stages. “Service journey is the sum of activities and interactions performed by organizations engaged in service relationships to fulfil their roles as a service provider and a service consumer.” Ref 3.2.3",x:["Internal workflows and processes are part of value streams, processes, and value chain activities, not the service journey. The service journey includes both parties in the service relationship. “The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, […].” Ref 2.3","The end-to-end lifecycle of a product is described by the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model, not the service journey. The service journey is relationship-focused, not lifecycle-stage-focused. “To ensure quality and continual improvement of digital products and services, organizations have to manage products and services throughout their lifecycle.” Ref 1.3","Value chain activities describe how organizations manage products and services, while the service journey describes how providers and consumers interact. They are related but not the same concept. “Value chain is an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service.” Ref 2.3.1"]},
{n:10,m:"m2",l:"m2a",syl:"1.4.4",ty:"std",q:"Which role authorizes budget for a service in a service relationship?",o:["Sponsor","Customer","User","Product vendor"],pos:2,e:"The sponsor role is responsible for authorizing the budget for service consumption. This role approves funding and financial commitment for the service. “Sponsor is the role that authorizes budget for service consumption.” Ref 3.2.1.1",x:["The customer defines requirements and takes responsibility for outcomes, but does not authorize the budget. “Customer is the role that defines the requirements for products and services and takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption.” Ref 3.2.1.1","The user consumes and uses the service; they do not approve funding. “User is the role that uses services.” Ref 3.2.1.1","A product vendor creates and improves digital products, but is not a service consumer role and does not authorize budgets for service consumption. “Digital product vendor is an organization responsible for the creation and continual improvement of digital products and related service offerings.” Ref 3.2.1"]},
{n:11,m:"m2",l:"m2d",syl:"1.4.5",ty:"std",q:"What is service quality MOST concerned with?",o:["How well a service meets agreed requirements and expectations","How quickly incidents are resolved","The speed of service development","The social responsibility of a service provider"],pos:0,e:"Service quality is being concerned with how well a service satisfies stated and implied needs, which are expressed through agreed requirements and expectations. It is not limited to speed, development pace, or ethics alone, but to the overall ability of the service to meet what has been agreed and expected. “Service quality is the sum of the characteristics of a service that are relevant to its ability to satisfy stated and implied needs.” Ref 3.2.4",x:["Incident resolution speed is only one aspect of service management and does not, on its own, define service quality. It is part of support performance, not the full scope of service quality. This option refers to incident management and operational support metrics. “Incident is an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service.” Ref 4.7.2","The speed of developing a service relates to product development, lifecycle efficiency, or organizational velocity, not directly to service quality as perceived by consumers. “Service quality is the sum of the characteristics of a service that are relevant to its ability to satisfy stated and implied needs.” Ref 3.2.4","Social responsibility is addressed under sustainability, which is one component of service levels, but it does not define service quality on its own. “Sustainability is the assurance that a product or service meets and will continue to meet the requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth.” Ref 3.2.4"]},
{n:12,m:"m2",l:"m2d",syl:"1.4.6",ty:"std",q:"Which of the following is typically used to establish a shared understanding of expected and achieved service quality?",o:["Service Level Agreement","Product specification","Product and service roadmaps","Service level requirements"],pos:1,e:"A Service Level Agreement (SLA) is the common mechanism used to establish a shared understanding between a service provider and a customer regarding expected and achieved service quality. It formalizes what level of service is expected and how it will be measured and managed. “The common way to establish a shared understanding of the expected and achieved service quality and to manage service improvement is a Service Level Agreement (SLA).” Ref 3.2.5",x:["A product specification describes what a product or service should be built to do, not how service quality will be agreed, measured, or reviewed during service delivery. This option refers to ‘design’ and ‘build’ activities “Product/service specification is a detailed document that outlines critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics of a product or service to be built.” Ref 4.3.1","Roadmaps describe future direction and planned improvements. They relate to planning and prioritization, especially in ‘discovery’ and ‘design’ activities. “The key outputs of this activity include an updated product roadmap, product and service improvement initiatives, […].” Ref 4.2.1","Service level requirements are inputs to service design and agreements, but they do not, by themselves, establish a shared understanding unless they are formalized in an SLA. “Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service.” Ref 4.8.1"]},
{n:13,m:"m9",l:"m9c",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"How does the ‘information and technology’ dimension support effective product and service management?",o:["By enabling the use of data, information, and technology required to deliver services","By defining organizational roles and responsibilities needed for product development","By defining workflows and activities required for product development","By managing relationships with external suppliers"],pos:0,e:"The ‘information and technology’ dimension in ITIL focuses on the data, information, and technologies that support digital products and services and the management systems behind them. This dimension ensures that the right technological capabilities and information assets are available to enable effective product and service management. “The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4",x:["Defining roles, responsibilities, skills, and structures belong to the ‘organizations and people’ dimension. The ‘organizations and people’ dimension “[…] ensures that the way an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, is well defined and supports its overall strategy and operating model”. Ref Glossary","Workflows, processes, and activities are addressed under the ‘value streams and processes’ dimension. They refer to how work is structured and executed across value streams. “The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3","Managing external suppliers and partners is the focus of the ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension. This option describes supplier relationships and contracts. “The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services.” Ref 2.5"]},
{n:14,m:"m9",l:"m9d",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Which dimension is concerned with management of relationships with external organizations?",o:["Partners and suppliers","Information and technology","Organizations and people","Value streams and processes"],pos:0,e:"The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension addresses how an organization manages its relationships with external organizations that contribute to the discovery, design, delivery, support, and improvement of products and services. “The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services.” Ref 2.5",x:["The ‘information and technology’ dimension focuses on data, information, and technology, and how they support products and services. “The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4","‘Organizations and people’ focuses on internal structure, culture, roles, and competencies. The ‘organizations and people’ dimension “[…] ensures that the way an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, is well defined and supports its overall strategy and operating model.” Ref Glossary","‘Value streams and processes’ address workflows and activities. “The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3"]},
{n:15,m:"m9",l:"m9e",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"Why are ‘value streams and processes’ important in product and service management?",o:["They show how activities are coordinated to create and deliver value","They specify the tools used for service monitoring","They define organizational hierarchies and reporting lines","They describe supplier agreements and help manage contracts"],pos:1,e:"The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension is concerned with how workflows across activities and how those activities are organized and coordinated to enable value for stakeholders. “Value stream is a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.” Ref 2.3.1",x:["Tools for monitoring are part of the ‘information and technology’ dimension and specific management practices, not the ‘value streams and processes’ dimension. “The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4","Organizational hierarchies and reporting structures belong to the ‘organizations and people’ dimension, not ‘value streams and processes’. The ‘organizations and people’ dimension focuses on how “an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, is well defined and supports its overall strategy and operating model”. Ref Glossary","Supplier agreements and contracts belong to the ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension, not ‘value streams and processes’. “The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services.” Ref 2.5"]},
{n:16,m:"m9",l:"m9b",syl:"2.2.2",ty:"std",q:"How does the ‘organizations and people’ dimension contribute to effective product and service management?",o:["By ensuring skills, culture, and communication support value creation","By defining the technologies used to deliver services","By managing relationships with external organizations","By automating workflows across value streams"],pos:0,e:"The ‘organizations and people’ dimension focuses on ensuring that culture, leadership, skills, competencies, and communication enable the organization to work effectively and create value through products and services. The ‘organizations and people’ dimension focuses on how “an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, is well defined and supports its overall strategy and operating model”. Ref Glossary",x:["This describes the ‘information and technology’ dimension. “The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, […].” Ref 2.4","This refers to the ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension. “The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services.” Ref 2.5","This aligns with ‘value streams and processes’. “The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows.” Ref 2.3"]},
{n:17,m:"m3",l:"m3a",syl:"4.1.1",ty:"std",q:"Which set CORRECTLY lists the components of the ITIL Value System?",o:["Guiding principles, governance, value chain, management practices, continual improvement","Value streams, projects, releases, incidents, changes","Guiding principles, governance, discover, deliver","Products, services, customers, suppliers, partners"],pos:3,e:"This includes all five components of the ITIL VS. “The ITIL VS includes five components: Guiding principles, Governance, Value chain, Practices, Continual improvement.” Ref 1.3",x:["These are concepts, activities, or practices used within ITIL, but they are not the components of the ITIL Value System. “The ITIL VS includes five components: Guiding principles, Governance, Value chain, Practices, Continual improvement.” Ref 1.3","Although guiding principles and governance are ITIL VS components, ‘discover’ and ‘deliver’ are value chain activities, not ITIL VS components. “The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model “[…] includes eight lifecycle stages, also known as value chain activities: Discover, Design, Acquire, Build, Transition, Operate, Deliver and Support.” Ref 4","This reflects stakeholders and service relationship elements, not the governance and management system itself. “The ITIL Value System (ITIL VS) is a model representing how all the components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation through digital products and services.” Ref 5.1"]},
{n:18,m:"m5",l:"m5c",syl:"4.2.4",ty:"std",q:"A team is developing a new digital service. Instead of delivering all features at once, they release a small set of features, gather user feedback, and adjust the next release based on what they learn.\nWhich ITIL Guiding Principle is the team applying in this situation?",o:["Progress iteratively with feedback","Keep it simple and practical","Focus on value","Collaborate and promote visibility"],pos:2,e:"The scenario describes releasing work in small increments, gathering feedback, and adjusting future iterations. “Product and service management iterations can be sequential or simultaneous, with multiple feedback loops between them.” Ref 5.2.3",x:["This emphasizes simplification and practicality, not iteration. “Always aim to minimize the number of steps to accomplish an objective.” Ref 5.2.6","This principle is about understanding who the service consumer is and what they value. “All activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1","This focuses on collaboration and visibility of work, not incremental delivery. “Work and its results should be made visible, hidden agendas should be avoided, and information should be shared to the greatest degree possible.” Ref 5.2.4"]},
{n:19,m:"m5",l:"m5g",syl:"4.2.8",ty:"std",q:"How should the ITIL Guiding Principle ‘optimize and automate’ be applied?",o:["By optimizing processes before automating them","By replacing people with technology across all functions","By automating all activities immediately","By automating processes before optimizing them"],pos:1,e:"Optimization must come before automation. Automating inefficient or poorly designed work only increases waste and risk. “Before an activity or practice can be effectively automated, it should be optimized to whatever degree is reasonably possible.” Ref 5.2.7",x:["Automation should not happen for its own sake. “Automation for automation’s sake can increase costs, introduce significant risks, and reduce organizational resilience.” Ref 5.2.7","Automation must not be uncontrolled. “It is essential that limits are set on the optimization of products, services and practices, as they exist within a set of constraints which may include financial limitations, compliance requirements, time constraints, and resource availability.” Ref 5.2.7","This is opposite to what should happen. “Simplify and/or optimize before automating.” Ref 5.2.7.3"]},
{n:20,m:"m5",l:"m5e",syl:"4.2.6",ty:"std",q:"What does the ITIL Guiding Principle ‘think and work holistically’ emphasize?",o:["Considering all components of the value system when making decisions","Assigning responsibility for improvements to a single team","Ensuring all activities result in value creation for the stakeholders","Automating repetitive and manual tasks"],pos:0,e:"No element of the organization operates in isolation and decisions must consider the whole system, including interactions across the ITIL Value System. “No product, service, practice, process, team, or supplier stands alone. The outputs that the organization delivers to itself, its customers, and other stakeholders will suffer, unless it works in an integrated way to handle its activities as a whole, rather than as separate parts.” Ref 5.2.5",x:["This contradicts the holistic principle, which emphasizes collaboration and system-wide awareness rather than isolating responsibility within one team and reflects a siloed or fragmented approach. “The outputs that the organization delivers to itself, its customers, and other stakeholders will suffer, unless it works in an integrated way to handle its activities as a whole, rather than as separate parts.” Ref 5.2.5","This describes the ‘focus on value’ guiding principle. “All activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1","This aligns with the ‘optimize and automate’ guiding principle. “Before an activity or practice can be effectively automated, it should be optimized to whatever degree is reasonably possible.” Ref 5.2.7"]},
{n:21,m:"m5",l:"m5d",syl:"4.2.5",ty:"std",q:"How should the ITIL Guiding Principle ‘collaborate and promote visibility’ be applied?",o:["By involving stakeholders and sharing information with them","By limiting communication to management only","By reusing existing processes wherever possible","By capturing feedback after each iteration"],pos:0,e:"Collaborate and promote visibility emphasizes working with stakeholders and making work and information visible to enable better decision-making, trust, and outcomes. “When initiatives involve the right people in the correct roles, efforts benefit from better buy-in, more relevance (because better information is available for decision-making) and increased likelihood of long-term success.” Ref 5.2.4",x:["This reflects siloed working and poor collaboration, which ITIL identifies as harmful. “Cooperation and collaboration are better than isolated work, which is frequently referred to as ‘silo activity’.” Ref 5.2.4","This describes the guiding principle ‘start where you are’, not collaboration. “In the process of eliminating old, unsuccessful methods, products, or services and creating something better, […] Do not start over without first considering what is already available to be leveraged.” Ref 5.2.2","This describes ‘progress iteratively with feedback’ principle. “Seeking and using feedback before, throughout, and after each iteration will ensure that actions are focused and appropriate, […].” Ref 5.2.3.1"]},
{n:22,m:"m5",l:"m5g",syl:"4.2.9",ty:"std",q:"How does ITIL describe the way the Guiding Principles should be applied together?",o:["They should be used collectively and balanced based on the situation","They should be applied one at a time in a fixed sequence","They replace the need for governance and management practices","They should be applied independent of each other"],pos:3,e:"The guiding principles are not isolated, not hierarchical, and not sequential. They are intended to be used together, with different principles becoming more relevant depending on the context and situation. “Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other.” Ref 5.2.8",x:["This reflects a linear or prescriptive approach, which ITIL states does not apply to guiding principles. “There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8","Guiding principles support and guide decision-making, but they do not replace governance or management practices. “Working together, the components of the management system should ensure that digital products and services are managed in line with the organization’s objectives, […].” Ref 5.1","Guiding principles interact with and depend on each other. “In addition to being aware of the ITIL Guiding Principles, it is important to recognize that they interact with and depend upon each other.” Ref 5.2.8"]},
{n:23,m:"m4",l:"m4a",syl:"4.3.2",ty:"std",q:"Which governance activity is focused on ensuring adherence with policies and strategic direction?",o:["Monitor","Evaluate","Direct","Discover"],pos:2,e:"‘Monitor’ is the activity concerned with checking that performance and activities adhere to policies and strategic direction. “Monitor: monitoring the performance of the organization and its practices, products, and services. The governing body ensures that performance is in accordance with policies and strategic direction.” Ref 5.3.1",x:["‘Evaluate’ focuses on assessing the organization, its strategy, and context. “Evaluate: evaluating the organization, its strategy, portfolios, and relationships with other parties.” Ref 5.3.1","‘Direct’ is about setting direction and assigning responsibility. “Direct: assigning responsibility for, and directing the preparation and implementation of, organizational strategy and policies.” Ref 5.3.1","‘Discover’ is a product and service lifecycle management activity identifying needs and opportunities for products and services. “Discover: explore and prioritize needs and opportunities for the product and service.” Ref 4.1"]},
{n:24,m:"m6x",l:"a7",syl:"3.2.7",ty:"std",q:"Which of the following is a part of purpose of ‘deliver’ activity?",o:["Managing user onboarding","Monitoring support systems","Designing product prototypes","Obtaining supplier resources"],pos:0,e:"The purpose of the ‘deliver’ activity includes managing how users are onboarded and offboarded, as part of providing services and maintaining service quality. “The purpose of the ‘deliver’ activity is to provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain service quality standards, and gather consumer feedback.” Ref 4.8.1",x:["Monitoring systems and responding to events belong to ‘operate’ activity, which focuses on monitoring and maintaining products and systems. “The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.” Ref 4.7.1","Designing prototypes is part of the ‘design’ activity. “The purpose of the ‘design’ activity is to create prototypes and specifications for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework.” Ref 4.3.1","Obtaining resources from suppliers is part of the ‘acquire’ activity. “The purpose of the ‘acquire’ activity is to secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring the sustainability and scalability of products and services.” Ref 4.4.1"]},
{n:25,m:"m6x",l:"a6",syl:"3.2.6",ty:"std",q:"Which activity has the PRIMARY responsibility for maintaining and monitoring digital products and supporting systems?",o:["Operate","Deliver","Support","Transition"],pos:2,e:"‘Operate’ activity is responsible for maintaining and monitoring digital products and supporting systems. “The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.” Ref 4.7.1",x:["‘Deliver’ activity focuses on providing services to users. “The purpose of the ‘deliver’ activity is to provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain service quality standards, and gather consumer feedback.” Ref 4.8.1","‘Support’ deals with incidents, disasters, and restoration of service. “The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.” Ref 4.9.1","‘Transition’ is concerned with introducing new or updated products into live environments. “The purpose of the ‘transition’ activity is to seamlessly introduce new or updated products into operational environments and to ensure effective onboarding/offboarding of suppliers.” Ref 4.6.1"]},
{n:26,m:"m6x",l:"a5",syl:"3.2.5",ty:"std",q:"Which activity ensures that new or changed products are seamlessly introduced in the live environment?",o:["Transition","Build","Deliver","Operate"],pos:1,e:"‘Transition’ activity is responsible for seamlessly introducing new or updated products into the live (operational) environment. “The purpose of the ‘transition’ activity is to seamlessly introduce new or updated products into operational environments and to ensure effective onboarding/offboarding of suppliers.” Ref 4.6.1",x:["‘Build’ focuses on developing, integrating, and testing products. “The purpose of the ‘build’ activity is to develop, integrate, and test digital products, transforming designs into functional solutions.” Ref 4.5.1","‘Deliver’ is concerned with providing services to users once products are already live. “The purpose of the ‘deliver’ activity is to provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain service quality standards, and gather consumer feedback.” Ref 4.8.1","‘Operate’ is responsible for maintaining and monitoring products after they have been transitioned to the live environment. “The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.” Ref 4.7.1"]},
{n:27,m:"m6x",l:"a8",syl:"3.2.8",ty:"std",q:"What is the purpose of the ‘support’ activity?",o:["To assist users and maintain service performance","To design service architectures","To build solution components","To discover new business opportunities"],pos:0,e:"‘Support’ activity is responsible for handling incidents and disasters, restoring normal operations, and supporting users so that service performance is maintained or restored when it is disrupted. “The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.” Ref 4.9.1",x:["Designing architectures is part of ‘design’ activity in the product and service lifecycle. “The purpose of the ‘design’ activity is to create prototypes and specifications for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework.” Ref 4.3.1","Building solution components is handled during the ‘build’ activity. “The purpose of the ‘build’ activity is to develop, integrate, and test digital products, transforming designs into functional solutions.” Ref 4.5.1","Discovering opportunities is part of the ‘discover’ activity. “Discover: explore and prioritize needs and opportunities for the product and service.” Ref 4.1"]},
{n:28,m:"m6x",l:"a5",syl:"4.4.5",ty:"std",q:"What distinguishes ‘continuous deployment’ from ‘continuous delivery’?",o:["Continuous deployment automatically deploys changes to production","Continuous deployment prevents frequent releases","Continuous delivery requires manual code integration","Continuous delivery eliminates testing activities"],pos:2,e:"ITIL clearly distinguishes continuous deployment from continuous delivery by stating that continuous deployment automatically deploys every change that passes automated tests to production, without additional authorization. “Continuous deployment is a set of techniques and tools that enables every change that passes automated tests to be automatically deployed to production without additional authorization.” Ref 4.6.2",x:["Continuous deployment enables very frequent releases, potentially after every successful change. “Continuous deployment relies on continuous delivery.” “Continuous delivery is a set of techniques and tools that enables software updates to be deployed to production at any time. Frequent deployments are possible, [...]”. Ref 4.6.2","Manual code integration is contradicted by ITIL’s definition of continuous integration, which underpins continuous delivery. “Continuous integration is a set of techniques and tools that enables developers to frequently merge their code changes into a central repository, followed by automated builds and tests.” Ref 4.5.2","Testing is a core requirement of both continuous delivery and continuous deployment. “Continuous deployment is a set of techniques and tools that enables every change that passes automated tests to be automatically deployed to production without additional authorization.” Ref 4.6.2"]},
{n:29,m:"m6x",l:"a2",syl:"4.4.2",ty:"std",q:"What refers to a detailed document outlining the requirements and characteristics of a product?",o:["Product specification","Change request","Service request","Product prototype"],pos:3,e:"A product/service specification as a detailed document that outlines the requirements and characteristics of a product or service. “Product/service specification is a detailed document that outlines critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics of a product or service to be built.” Ref 4.3.1",x:["A change request proposes a modification to products or services. “Change is the addition, modification, or removal of anything that could have a direct or indirect effect on products and services.” Ref 4.7.2","A service request initiates a predefined user-initiated service action during service delivery. “Service request is a request from a user or a user’s authorized representative that initiates a service action which has been agreed as a normal part of service delivery.” Ref 4.8.2","A prototype is an initial version of a product or service used for testing and learning. “Product/service prototype is an initial creation of a product or service that demonstrates its basic form, functionality, and operational capabilities.” Ref 4.3.1"]},
{n:30,m:"m6x",l:"a6",syl:"4.4.6",ty:"std",q:"What does observability enable in digital product and service management?",o:["Understanding system behaviour through outputs such as logs, metrics, and traces","Ensuring changes are approved before being deployed","Automatically preventing incidents from occurring","Improving service reliability through engineering practices"],pos:0,e:"Observability is the ability to understand the internal state and behaviour of a system by analysing its external outputs. “Observability is the ability to understand the internal state of a complicated system by analysing its external outputs, such as metrics, logs, and traces.” Ref 4.7.1",x:["Ensuring approval of changes belongs to change enablement. Change enablement is the “[…] practice of ensuring that risks are properly assessed, authorizing changes to proceed and managing a change schedule […]”. Ref Glossary","Observability helps identify and analyse behaviour, but it does not automatically prevent incidents. Automatically preventing incidents from occurring is a result of proactive operations and incident prevention, supported by practices such as monitoring, event management, and problem management. Monitoring and event management is the “[…] practice of systematically observing services and service components, and recording and reporting selected changes of state identified as events”. Ref Glossary","Improving reliability through engineering practices is the goal of Site Reliability Engineering (SRE). “Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) is discipline that incorporates aspects of software engineering and applies them to infrastructure and operations problems. The main goal is to create scalable and highly reliable software systems.” Ref 4.7.1"]},
{n:31,m:"m6x",l:"a8",syl:"4.4.8",ty:"std",q:"What is a problem?",o:["Root cause of one or more incidents","An unplanned service interruption","A service access request from users","An incident that impacts critical services"],pos:1,e:"ITIL defines: “Problem is a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents.” Ref 4.9.1",x:["An unplanned service interruption is an incident. “Incident is an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service.” Ref 4.7.2","A request from users for access or a standard service action is a service request. “Service request is a request from a user or a user’s authorized representative that initiates a service action which has been agreed as a normal part of service delivery.” Ref 4.8.2","An incident with significant or critical impact is classified as a major incident. Major incident is an “[…] incident with significant business impact that requiring an immediate coordinated resolution”. Ref Glossary"]},
{n:32,m:"m6x",l:"a5",syl:"4.4.11",ty:"std",q:"An organization wants to evaluate how well the ‘transition’ value chain activity is performing.\nWhich metric would BEST support this evaluation?",o:["Success rate of releases deployed into the live environment","Number of ideas generated for new services","Average cost per service request","Percentage of value streams mapped"],pos:0,e:"The ‘transition’ activity is responsible for introducing new or updated products into the live environment. Its performance is therefore best evaluated by metrics related to successful deployments/releases and their impact. A high success rate of releases deployed into the live environment directly indicates low transition errors and effective transition performance. “What are the key metrics of success? […] Number and impact of transition errors.” Ref 4.6.2, Table 4.5",x:["Generating ideas is associated with identifying opportunities that belongs to the ‘discover’ activity. “Discover: explore and prioritize needs and opportunities for the product and service.” Ref 4.1","Cost per service request relates to service delivery and request fulfilment and the ‘deliver’ activity. “[…] service delivery involves fulfilment of users’ service requests, […].” Ref 4.8.2","Value stream mapping is used to analyse and improve workflows. “Value stream mapping is a technique for the visual representation and analysis of value streams. [...] it is focused on the flow of information, work, and, eventually, the value created for a service consumer.” Ref 6.3"]},
{n:33,m:"m6x",l:"a4",syl:"4.4.11",ty:"std",q:"Which metric is MOST appropriate for assessing the success of the ‘build’ value chain activity?",o:["Quality of the product solutions","Number of incidents resolved within agreed targets","Customer satisfaction score after service delivery","Percentage of service requests fulfilled on first contact"],pos:3,e:"Quality of the product solutions is a key metric of success for the ‘build’ activity. The ‘build’ activity focuses on developing, integrating, and testing products, so solution quality is the most appropriate success measure. “What are the key metrics of success? Quality of the product solutions. [...]” Ref 4.5.2, Table 4.4",x:["Resolving incidents within agreed targets is associated with the ‘support’ activity and incident management. “The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.” Ref 4.9.1","Customer satisfaction after service delivery is a measure of the ‘deliver’ activity. “What are the key metrics of success? Customer and user satisfaction with service quality. [...]” Ref 4.8.2, Table 4.7","First-contact resolution is related to service desk and support performance. “[…] service delivery involves fulfilment of users’ service requests, […].” Ref 4.8.2"]},
{n:34,m:"m6",l:"m6b",syl:"4.4.12",ty:"std",q:"How do value chain activities support an organization’s purpose?",o:["By turning strategic intent into coordinated workflows that enable outcomes for stakeholders","By defining governance controls and decision-making authority across management levels","By standardizing operational procedures aligning them to the organization's purpose to improve consistency and efficiency","By setting predefined service quality targets to direct what the operational workflows should achieve"],pos:0,e:"An organization’s purpose is enabled by value chain activities which create value in line with that purpose. “Organizations exist to create value for customers and other stakeholders. [...] To understand and communicate how an organization fulfils its purpose, an operating model is often used. [...] The highest level of the ‘value streams and processes’ dimension of an operating model can be described as organization’s value chain.” Ref 5.4.1",x:["Governance and compliance are handled by governance. “Governance is the system by which an organization is directed and controlled.” Ref 5.3.1","Value chain activities are not defined by standardization or procedural consistency. Value chain activities are flexible and can be combined in different ways depending on context. While standardization may occur within practices or processes, it is not the purpose of value chain activities, nor how they support organizational purpose. “Although the word ‘chain’ implies a sequence of tightly connected links, it is important to remember that the value chain activities are not always performed sequentially.” Ref 4.1","Setting predefined service quality targets (for example, availability, performance, or experience levels) is the role of service level management and governance, not the value chain. “Value chain: An entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service.” Ref 5.1"]},
{n:35,m:"m6",l:"m6c",syl:"4.4.12",ty:"std",q:"Why are management practices important for value chain activities?",o:["They enable value chain activities by providing the required capabilities","They define the organization’s purpose and strategy","They ensure activities are performed in a fixed order","They replace value chain activities with standardized processes"],pos:1,e:"Management practices enable and support value chain activities by providing the organizational capabilities required to perform those activities effectively. “Management practices enable and support the value chain activities.” “Each practice combines resources from the Four Dimensions to enable an organization’s ability to manage specific aspects of digital products and services.” Ref 1.3, Figure 1.9",x:["Purpose and strategy are defined by organizational leadership and governance. “Organization’s purpose describes what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why.” Ref 5.4.1","Value chain activities are not performed in a fixed sequence. “[…] the value chain activities are not always performed sequentially.” Ref 4.1","Management practices do not replace value chain activities; they enable and support them. “Management practices enable and support the value chain activities.” Ref 1.3, Figure 1.9"]},
{n:36,m:"m6",l:"m6c",syl:"4.5.4",ty:"std",q:"Why do ITIL Official Practice Guides follow a standardized structure across all practices?",o:["To make it easier for organizations to understand and apply different practices","To ensure all practices are implemented in the same sequence","To enforce uniform tooling and technology choices","To limit the flexibility of practice adoption"],pos:0,e:"ITIL Official Practice Guides follow the same structure so that readers can easily understand, compare, and apply practices consistently, regardless of which practice they are working with. “Each guide follows the same structure and addresses the following aspects of the management practices.” Ref 5.5.1",x:["Practices are adopted based on organizational context and needs. They are not to be implemented in a fixed or universal order. “The value chain activities and the practices in the ITIL VS do not form a fixed, rigid structure.” Ref 5.1","ITIL is tool-agnostic and does not prescribe specific tools or technologies. “The architecture of the ITIL VS specifically enables flexibility and discourages siloed working. The value chain activities and the practices in the ITIL VS do not form a fixed, rigid structure. Rather, they can be combined in multiple operating models and value streams to address the needs of the organization in a variety of scenarios.” Ref 5.1","ITIL emphasizes flexibility and adaptability, not restriction. The standardized structure supports learning and application, not limitation. “The architecture of the ITIL VS specifically enables flexibility and discourages siloed working.” Ref 5.1"]},
{n:37,m:"m6",l:"m6c",syl:"4.5.3",ty:"std",q:"How do ITIL Official Practice Guides benefit organizations managing digital products and services?",o:["By supporting organizations in developing product and service management capabilities","By prescribing mandatory tools and technologies for service management","By separating product management guidance from service management guidance","By defining a fixed maturity level that organizations must achieve"],pos:2,e:"ITIL Official Practice Guides exist to support organizations by providing practical guidance on how to adopt and adapt management practices, which in turn helps them develop capabilities for managing digital products and services. “To succeed in digital product and service management, organizations develop many capabilities, from strategic to operational. ITIL offers structured practical guidance for 34 product and service management capabilities in the ITIL Official Practice Guides.” Ref 1.2.1",x:["The ITIL Official Practice Guides provide guidance on practices and capabilities, they do not prescribe specific tools or technologies. “Automation and tooling, including recommendations for successful automation of the practice.” Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4","ITIL emphasizes integration, not separation, of product and service management. “ITIL offers an end-to-end Lifecycle Model for digital products and services, where the concepts of digital product and service are integrated.” Ref 1.2","ITIL gives emphasis on continual improvement and situational application. Maturity models are assessment and improvement tools, not fixed targets. Continual improvement is a “[…] recurring organizational activity performed at all levels of an organization to ensure that the organization continually meets stakeholders’ expectations”. Continual improvement takes place in all areas of the organization and at all levels, from strategic to operational. Ref 5.6"]},
{n:38,m:"m8",l:"m8a",syl:"4.6.2",ty:"std",q:"Why does the ITIL Continual Improvement Model begin by defining the vision?",o:["To ensure improvement efforts are aligned with the organization’s objectives","To document current performance metrics before changes are made","To identify improvement actions and assign responsibilities","To confirm whether previous improvement initiatives were successful"],pos:1,e:"The ITIL Continual Improvement Model begins by defining the vision to ensure that any improvement initiative is linked to the organization’s goals, objectives, and priorities. “Each improvement initiative should support the organization’s goals and objectives. The first step of the continual improvement model is to define the vision of the initiative. This provides context for all subsequent decisions and links individual actions to the organization’s vision for the future.” Ref 5.6.1.1",x:["Documenting current performance metrics belongs to Step 2: Where are we now?, not Step 1. Step 1 does not focus on measurement or baselining; it focuses on understanding and translating the organization’s vision. “Step 2: Where are we now? […] A key element in this step is a current state assessment. […] Current state assessments should be done through objective measurement whenever possible.” Ref 5.6.1.2","Identifying improvement actions and assigning responsibilities belongs to Step 4: How do we get there? and Step 5: Take action, after the vision, current state, and target state are understood. “Step 4: How do we get there? […] a plan for addressing the challenges of the initiative can be created.” “Step 5: Take action […] the plan for the improvement is acted upon.” Ref 5.6.1.4, 5.6.1.5","This option refers to validation and evaluation of improvement outcomes, which occurs later in the model, part of Step 6: Are we getting there? “Step 6: Are we getting there? This step involves an assessment of the new current state to compare it with the agreed targets and with the baseline.” Ref 5.6.1.6"]},
{n:39,m:"m7",l:"m7a",syl:"5.1.1",ty:"std",q:"How does an enabling value stream contribute to value creation?",o:["By supporting the effective operation of core value streams","By replacing the need for core value streams","By defining customer outcomes and service expectations","By acting as the primary interface with service consumers"],pos:0,e:"An enabling value stream supports internal activities that allow core value streams to function effectively and deliver value to customers. Its role is to enable and support, not to replace or interface directly with consumers. “An enabling value stream is a value stream that enables value for internal customers to support the organization’s core value streams.” Ref 6.2",x:["Enabling value streams do not replace core value streams. Core value streams are essential because they are the ones that directly enable value for service consumers. Enabling value streams exist only in relation to core value streams. “A core value stream is a value stream that enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization’s operating model.” Ref 6.2","Defining customer outcomes and service expectations refers to customer-focused activities, which are part of core value streams and service relationship management. “A core value stream is a value stream that enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization’s operating model.” Ref 6.2","Enabling value streams do not interface directly with service consumers. That role belongs to core value streams, which handle end-to-end value delivery to customers. “A core value stream is a value stream that enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization’s operating model.” Ref 6.2"]},
{n:40,m:"m7",l:"m7b",syl:"5.3.2",ty:"std",q:"What does ITIL emphasize as a key difference between ‘value stream mapping’ and ‘value stream management’?",o:["Mapping identifies how value flows, while management ensures the value stream performs effectively over time","Mapping is used only for reporting, while management is used only for auditing","Mapping applies to services, while management applies to products","Mapping focuses on cost control, while management focuses on risk avoidance"],pos:0,e:"Value stream mapping is a technique to identify, visualize, and analyse value streams, and value stream management is an ongoing, continual activity that ensures value streams remain effective, adaptive, and improved over time. Mapping is a starting point; management sustains and governs performance. “Value stream mapping is a technique for the visual representation and analysis of value streams.” “Value stream mapping is a technique that is relatively easy to start and it also provides tangible results in the short term. However, it does not have a sustainable effect if performed as a one-off exercise. […] To maintain high quality of services and continually optimize the flow of work, organizations shift to value stream management, which means both ‘management of value streams’ and ‘management through value streams’.” Ref 6.3, 6.4",x:["Value stream mapping is not a reporting tool, nor value stream management is an auditing activity. Both are improvement-focused and value-oriented, not compliance mechanisms. “Value stream mapping and management aim to: focus on customer value and optimize the end-to-end flow for value creation.” Ref 6.1","Both value stream mapping and value stream management apply to products and services together, according with ITIL’s integrated product-and-service perspective. “To manage the actual product and service management work as performed […] organizations need to identify and map their value streams, analyse and continually improve them.” Ref 6.1","Value stream mapping and value stream management focus on value creation, flow optimization, and continual improvement. “Value stream management involves an ongoing focus on how work is done, as well as the analysis and improvement of the organization’s value streams.” Ref 6.4"]}
]}
];
window.QB=window.QB||[];
QB.push({m:"m1",l:"m1a",of:1,q:"What is a digital service?",o:["A service that fully or largely relies on digital products","A combination of technology resources designed for consumers","The transfer of goods from provider to consumer","A catalogue of services for consumers"],e:"A digital service is “a service that fully or largely relies on digital products.” Ref 3.1.2",x:["This resembles the digital product definition: \"Digital product is a combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers.\" Ref 3.1.2","Transfer of goods is a form of service interaction: \"examples include delivery of goods, user training, consultations, and so on. This form of service interaction is very common in several industries such as hospitality, education, transport, and many others.\" Ref 3.1.2","A catalogue or description of services corresponds more closely to a service offering, not a digital service itself. “Service offering is a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group.” Ref 3.1.2"]});
QB.push({m:"m1",l:"m1d",of:1,q:"What influences whether a service is perceived as valuable by a consumer?",o:["The extent to which the positive effects of the service outweigh the negative ones","The service provider’s ability to minimize their operational costs","The number of providers delivering the service to the consumer","The amount of money the service provider invests in resources"],e:"“[...] services can introduce new costs and risks, and in some cases, they can negatively affect some of the intended outcomes, while supporting others. Services are perceived by service consumers as valuable only when they have more positive effects than negative, […].” Ref 3.1.1",x:["Operational costs do not concern the consumer, but the services provided “[…] can introduce new costs and risks [...] Services are perceived by service consumers as valuable only when they have more positive effects than negative, […]”. Ref 3.1.1","“Service providers help their consumers to achieve outcomes, and in doing so, take on some of the associated costs and risks. Service providers are usually specialized in specific types of services” and hence, the number of providers is not significant for the consumer. Ref 3.1.1","The amount of money invested in resources by a service provider is unrelated to the consumer’s assessment of value. “The value for the service consumer(s): is defined by their own needs; is achieved through the support of intended outcomes and optimization of the service consumer’s costs and risks; is subjective and influenced by the service experience; changes over time and in different circumstances.” Ref 5.2.1.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m1",l:"m1a",of:1,q:"What is a digital product?",o:["A combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers","A formal description of one or more services designed to address consumer needs","The access to the service provider’s resources and their use according to the agreed terms and conditions","Actions performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and consumer"],e:"“Digital products are a combination of an organization’s resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers.” Ref 4.5.1",x:["This is the definition of service offering: \"[…] a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group. A service offering may include goods, access to resources, and service action\". Ref 3.1.2","This is the definition of access to resources: \"[…] the service consumer gains access to the service provider’s resources and uses them according to the agreed terms and conditions\". Ref 3.1.2","This is the definition of service action: \"[…] an action performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and a service consumer.\" Ref 3.1.2"]});
QB.push({m:"m2",l:"m2d",of:1,q:"A service provider commits to using recyclable components, reducing carbon emissions, and maintaining transparent supply chains. Which concept does this commitment represent?",o:["Sustainability","Utility","Warranty","User experience"],e:"“Sustainability requirements may include responsible sourcing of materials and components, responsible work and employment practices, use of clean energy, low carbon footprint, incorporation of recycled and recyclable materials, transparent supply chains, and others. These requirements may originate from national and international regulations and standards or be defined in the organization’s sustainability strategy.” Ref 3.2.4",x:["Utility is the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need. “Utility can be summarized as ‘what the service does’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for purpose’. To have utility, a service must either support the performance of the consumer or remove constraints from the consumer.” Ref 3.2.4","“Warranty is the assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements. Warranty can be summarized as ‘how the service performs’ and can be used to determine whether a service is ‘fit for use’. Warranty typically addresses such areas as the availability of the service, its capacity, levels of security, and continuity.” Ref 3.2.4","“User experience is the sum of functional and emotional interactions with a service and service provider as perceived by a service user.” Ref 3.2.3"]});
QB.push({m:"m2",l:"m2b",of:1,q:"Which type of service relationship typically focuses on support and efficiency through standardized services?",o:["Basic relationship","Cooperative relationship","Collaborative relationship","Partner relationship"],e:"The ‘typical focus’ of the ‘basic relationship’ is “support and efficiency”. Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1",x:["The 'typical focus' of the 'cooperative relationship' is \"improvement and effectiveness\". Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1","The ‘typical focus‘ of the ‘collaborative relationship‘ is \"innovation and growth\". Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1","A ‘partner relationship’ is another name for 'collaborative' and its focus is “innovation and growth”, not support and efficiency. Ref 3.2.2, Table 3.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m2",l:"m2a",of:1,q:"Which role takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption?",o:["Customer","Organization","Sponsor","Service provider"],e:"“Customer is the role that defines the requirements for products and services and takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption.” Ref 3.2.1.1",x:["An organization is a broad entity with functions and responsibilities, but it is not specifically the role accountable for service consumption outcomes. “Organization is a person or a group of people that has its own functions with responsibilities, authorities, and relationships to achieve its objectives.” Ref 3.2.1","The sponsor \"is the role that authorizes budget for service consumption.\" Ref 3.2.1.1","The service provider is responsible for delivering and supporting services, not for the consumer’s outcomes. “Service provider is an organization responsible for the delivery and support of services.” Ref 3.2.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m3",l:"m3a",of:1,q:"Which ITIL concept includes governance, practices, and continual improvement?",o:["The ITIL Value System","The ‘deliver and support’ value chain activities","The ‘focus on value’ ITIL Guiding Principle","The ‘value stream and processes’ dimension"],e:"“The ITIL Value System (ITIL VS) is a model representing how all the components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation through digital products and services.” Ref 5.1",x:["The ‘deliver’ and ‘support’ activities are only two value chain activities within the lifecycle and do not encompass governance, practices, and continual improvement. “The lifecycle activities include: […] Deliver, which provides services to users and Support, which resolves issues and maintains service quality.” Ref 4.10","The ‘focus on value’ is a principle stating that all “[…] activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1","The ‘value streams and processes’ are ”workflows used by the organization to structure its value-creating activities”. Ref 5.4.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m3",l:"m3a",of:1,q:"Which set CORRECTLY lists the components of the ITIL Value System?",o:["Guiding principles, governance, value chain, management practices, continual improvement","Value streams, projects, releases, incidents, changes","Guiding principles, governance, discover, deliver","Products, services, customers, suppliers, partners"],e:"This includes all five components of the ITIL VS. “The ITIL VS includes five components: Guiding principles, Governance, Value chain, Practices, Continual improvement.” Ref 1.3",x:["These are concepts, activities, or practices used within ITIL, but they are not the components of the ITIL Value System. “The ITIL VS includes five components: Guiding principles, Governance, Value chain, Practices, Continual improvement.” Ref 1.3","Although guiding principles and governance are ITIL VS components, ‘discover’ and ‘deliver’ are value chain activities, not ITIL VS components. “The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model “[…] includes eight lifecycle stages, also known as value chain activities: Discover, Design, Acquire, Build, Transition, Operate, Deliver and Support.” Ref 4","This reflects stakeholders and service relationship elements, not the governance and management system itself. “The ITIL Value System (ITIL VS) is a model representing how all the components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation through digital products and services.” Ref 5.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m2",l:"m2d",of:1,q:"What BEST describes a Service Level Agreement (SLA)?",o:["A documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies services and their agreed levels","A legal contract between two vendors regulating financial penalties","An informal discussion between users and service providers about expectations","A guideline document for internal IT teams without customer involvement"],e:"“Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service.” Ref 3.2.5",x:["SLAs are not legal contracts focused on penalties, they are a \"[…] documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service\". Ref 3.2.5","SLAs are formal documented agreements, not informal discussions: \"[…] a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service\". Ref 3.2.5","SLAs involve both providers and customers, not just internal IT. An SLA \"[…] is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service\". Ref 3.2.5"]});
QB.push({m:"m4",l:"m4a",of:1,q:"Which governance activity is focused on ensuring adherence with policies and strategic direction?",o:["Monitor","Evaluate","Direct","Discover"],e:"‘Monitor’ is the activity concerned with checking that performance and activities adhere to policies and strategic direction. “Monitor: monitoring the performance of the organization and its practices, products, and services. The governing body ensures that performance is in accordance with policies and strategic direction.” Ref 5.3.1",x:["‘Evaluate’ focuses on assessing the organization, its strategy, and context. “Evaluate: evaluating the organization, its strategy, portfolios, and relationships with other parties.” Ref 5.3.1","‘Direct’ is about setting direction and assigning responsibility. “Direct: assigning responsibility for, and directing the preparation and implementation of, organizational strategy and policies.” Ref 5.3.1","‘Discover’ is a product and service lifecycle management activity identifying needs and opportunities for products and services. “Discover: explore and prioritize needs and opportunities for the product and service.” Ref 4.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m4",l:"m4a",of:1,q:"What is the role of a governing body in an organization?",o:["It is accountable for performance and compliance at the highest level","It is responsible for day-to-day operations","It manages the technical details of digital products directly","It focuses exclusively on financial reporting"],e:"“Every organization is directed by a governing body: a person or group of people who are accountable at the highest level for the performance and compliance of the organization.” Ref 5.3.1",x:["Day-to-day operations are handled by management, not governance. The governing body provides direction, but execution is delegated. The governing body is concerned with strategic direction, oversight, compliance, and accountability. Furthermore, it is the Management practices that “enable and support the value chain activities”. Ref 5.4.1 Figure 2.1","Technical details of digital products fall under operational and technical teams, not under the governing body. The governing body is concerned with strategic direction, oversight, compliance, and accountability. “If the purpose of an internal technology organization is ‘to support the core business of the company with effective and efficient technology solutions’, and business-critical digital products and services are developed and delivered internally, then the internal technology organization is likely to take responsibility for all activities of the digital product and service management.” Ref 5.4.2","Exclusive focus on financial reporting is too narrow. While financial accountability is part of governance, it also covers compliance, risk, digital technology use, strategic investment, and stakeholder value. “The governing body is accountable for the organization’s compliance with policies and external regulations. Governance of the use of digital technology is an important part of corporate governance” Ref 5.3.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m2",l:"m2c",of:1,q:"Which statement about the ‘band of visibility’ is CORRECT?",o:["It represents all aspects visible between provider and consumer organizations","It includes only the activities visible to the service provider","It applies exclusively to modern, technology-based digital service models","It defines the complete path of the customer’s service journey"],e:"The ‘band of visibility’ “[…] is the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer”. Ref 3.2.3",x:["Visibility is not about access levels, it is about \"[…] the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3","Visibility is not exclusive to technology-based digital service models, but is \"[…] the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3","Visibility is unrelated to contract timelines. It is \"[…] the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer\". Ref 3.2.3"]});
QB.push({m:"m5",l:"m5a",of:1,q:"According to the ‘focus on value’ ITIL Guiding Principle, all organizational activities should link back to what?",o:["Benefits for organization, its customers, and stakeholders","Increased revenue only","Employee satisfaction","Market share dominance"],e:"The ‘focus on value’ principle stipulates that all “[…] activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1",x:["Focusing solely on revenue is too narrow because financial gain is only one dimension of value. ‘focus on value’ stresses that value also includes outcomes like customer satisfaction, loyalty, reduced cost, and long-term opportunities, not just monetary profits. Different stakeholders may have a different value definition. “This value may come in various forms, such as revenue, customer loyalty, lower cost, or growth opportunities.” Ref 5.2.1","While employee satisfaction is important for overall success, it is an enabler rather than the ultimate value outcome. It is a contributing factor but not the overarching focus of organizational activities. Additionally, it is the role of leadership to “[…] engage, inspire, and motivate people by behaving with integrity to generate respect and empower employees”. Ref 2.2.2","Market dominance may contribute to value but is not the universal measure of success. ‘focus on value’ encourages creating value for stakeholders through outcomes like revenue, loyalty, reduced cost, or growth opportunities. “All activities conducted by the organization should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization itself, its customers, and other stakeholders.” Ref 5.2.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m5",l:"m5b",of:1,q:"According to the ITIL Guiding Principle ‘start where you are’, what is the MAIN risk of starting over without considering what is already available when improving a service?",o:["Wasting time and losing valuable existing capabilities and resources","Losing opportunities for collaboration and shared understanding across teams","Overcomplicating solutions by ignoring practical, simple approaches","Failing to gather feedback needed to guide gradual improvement"],e:"When improving products or services, starting over completely is rarely wise. The approach of completely removing what has been done “[…] can be extremely wasteful, not only in terms of time, but also in terms of the loss of existing capabilities, competencies, people, and tools that could have significant value in the improvement effort. Do not start over without first considering what is already available to be leveraged.” Ref 5.2.2",x:["The concept of collaboration is unrelated to the ‘start where you are’ principle. “Collaboration that leads to real accomplishment requires information, understanding, and trust. Work and its results should be made visible, hidden agendas should be avoided, and information should be shared to the greatest degree possible.” Ref 5.2.4","Overcomplexity of solutions is dealt with in the ‘keep it simple and practical’ principle, not in ‘start where you are’. ”To avoid the ‘build trap’ when more and more features are built, overcomplicating the products, organizations should stay focused on value and keep their products and services simple and practical.” Ref 5.2.6","This has to do with the ‘progress iteratively with feedback’ principle which states that “Seeking and using feedback before, throughout, and after each iteration will ensure that actions are focused and appropriate, even in changing circumstances.” Ref 5.2.3.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m5",l:"m5g",of:1,q:"Why should organizations consider the interaction of ITIL Guiding Principles?",o:["Because principles are interdependent and complement each other","Because one principle is always superior to the rest","Because principles must be applied in a strict order","Because one principle is usually sufficient for any situation"],e:"“In addition to being aware of the ITIL Guiding Principles, it is important to recognize that they interact with and depend upon each other. For example, if an organization is committed to progressing iteratively with feedback, it should also think and work holistically to ensure that each iteration of an improvement includes all the elements necessary to deliver real results. Similarly, making use of appropriate feedback is key to collaboration, and focusing on what will truly be valuable to the customer makes it easier to keep things simple and practical.” Ref 5.2.8",x:["”Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other. There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8","Principles do not follow a strict order. ”Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other. There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8","”Organizations should not use just one or two of the principles but should consider the relevance of each of them and how they complement each other. There is no particular order or hierarchy of these principles; they are equally important for the ITIL VS.” Ref 5.2.8"]});
QB.push({m:"m6x",l:"a1",of:1,q:"What is the MAIN purpose of the ‘discover’ activity?",o:["To align product capabilities with consumer needs and strategy","To develop and test digital products","To secure and allocate resources efficiently","To maintain and monitor digital products"],e:"The main purpose of the ‘discover’ activity “[…] is to ensure continual alignment of the product roadmaps and related service offerings with the needs of service consumers and with the organizational strategy”. Ref 4.2.1",x:["Developing and testing is part of the 'build' activity. \"The purpose of the ‘build’ activity is to develop, integrate, and test digital products, transforming designs into functional solutions.\" Ref 4.5.1","Securing resources belongs to the 'acquire' activity. \"The purpose of the ‘acquire’ activity is to secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring the sustainability and scalability of products and services.\" Ref 4.4.1","Maintaining and monitoring digital products is the purpose of the 'operate' activity: \"The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.\" Ref 4.7.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m6x",l:"a1",of:1,q:"Which of the following is NOT a success metric of the ‘discover’ activity?",o:["Service performance against the agreed SLA targets","Strategic fit of the organization’s products and service offerings","Market relevance of the products and service offerings","Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings"],e:"“Service performance against the agreed SLA targets” is a key metric of the ‘deliver’ activity. The key metrics of success of the ‘discover’ activity are: “Strategic fit of the organization’s products and service offerings. Market relevance of the products and service offerings. Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings. Commercial success of the products (if applicable).” Ref 4.8.2, Table 4.7, 4.2.2, Table 4.1",x:["“Strategic fit of the organization’s products and service offerings” is a success metric of ‘discover’ activity. Ref 4.2.2, Table 4.1","Market relevance is a success metric of ‘discover’ activity: “Market relevance of the products and service offerings. Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings. Commercial success of the products (if applicable)”. Ref 4.2.2. Table 4.1","“Stakeholder satisfaction with products and service offerings” is one of the success metrics of ‘discover’ activity. Ref 4.2.2, Table 4.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m6x",l:"a8",of:1,q:"Which lifecycle management activity focuses on minimizing the impact of incidents and disasters when they happen?",o:["Support","Transition","Operate","Design"],e:"“The purpose of the ‘support’ activity is to identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback.” Ref 4.9.1",x:["'Transition' has to do with onboarding and offboarding of suppliers: \"The purpose of the ‘transition’ activity is to seamlessly introduce new or updated products into operational environments and to ensure effective onboarding/offboarding of suppliers.\" Ref 4.6.1","The 'operate' activity maintains system performance: \"The purpose of the ‘operate’ activity is to maintain and monitor digital products and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability.\" Ref 4.7.1","“The purpose of the ‘design’ activity is to create prototypes and specifications for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework.\" Ref 4.3.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m6",l:"m6b",of:1,q:"Which term describes ‘what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why’?",o:["Organization’s purpose","Value stream","Value chain","Operating model"],e:"An organization’s purpose is “[…] what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why.” Ref 5.4.1",x:["“Value stream is a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.” Ref 2.3.1","The ‘Value chain’ enables and supports the value streams, in addition, “[…] continual improvement of value streams is achieved indirectly by improving the value chain and the management practices.” Ref 6.4","The operating model is a “[…] conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself.” Ref 5.4.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m7",l:"m7b",of:1,q:"Which of the following is NOT one of the steps in value stream mapping?",o:["Value stream management","Value stream identification","Mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream","Mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream"],e:"This is not a step in value stream mapping. “Value stream mapping includes the following steps: value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements.” An organization’s purpose is a general term describing “[…] what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why”. Ref 6.3, Glossary",x:["This is one of the steps in value stream mapping. Value stream mapping includes the following steps: “value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements”. Ref 6.3","This is one of the steps in value stream mapping. Value stream mapping includes the following steps: “value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements”. Ref 6.3","This is one of the steps in value stream mapping. Value stream mapping includes the following steps: “value stream identification; mapping of the ‘as-is’ value stream; analysing the value stream; mapping a ‘to-be’ value stream; planning and implementing improvements”. Ref 6.3"]});
QB.push({m:"m7",l:"m7b",of:1,q:"What is the MAIN purpose of value stream mapping in an organization?",o:["To identify and prioritize improvement opportunities","To automate service monitoring","To enhance and support management practices","To control external stakeholders directly"],e:"“Value stream mapping allows organizations to identify, map, and analyse their value streams, as well as identify and prioritize improvement opportunities.” Ref 6.4",x:["Automation supports value stream management, not mapping: \"This understanding is supported by automation and measurement.\" Ref 6.4","It is the management practices \"[…] that enable and support the value streams, as well as the organization’s products and services. In many cases, the improvement of a management practice has impact on multiple value streams\", not the other way round. Ref 6.4","External dependencies cannot be directly controlled, but the contractual agreements may be altered to \"[…] include changes to contracts with suppliers, ways of using external services, changing the ways of cooperation\". Ref Appendix E"]});
QB.push({m:"m8",l:"m8a",of:1,q:"A company plans an improvement initiative and ensures it is clearly linked to organizational goals and objectives. Which continual improvement step is being performed?",o:["What is the vision?","Where are we now?","Take Action","Where do we want to be?"],e:"“Each improvement initiative should support the organization’s goals and objectives. The first step of the continual improvement model is to define the vision of the initiative. This provides context for all subsequent decisions and links individual actions to the organization’s vision for the future.” Ref 5.6.1.1",x:["In the \"where are we now\" step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model, the organization assesses existing products and services. “Depending on the scope of improvement, this may be an assessment of existing products and services, including user perception of value received, people’s competencies and skills, involved workflows, available information, and the capabilities of the available technological solutions.” Ref 5.6.1.2","In the ‘take action’ step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model, “[…] the plan for the improvement is acted upon. Agreed actions and experiments can be conducted as a sequence, simultaneously, or independently”. Ref 5.6.1.5","In the ‘where do we want to be’, step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model, the organization defines (Point B), “[…] the target state for the next step of the journey, […]” and what it “should look like. A journey step cannot be mapped out if the destination, or at least the direction, is not clear”. Ref 5.6.1.3"]});
QB.push({m:"m6",l:"m6c",of:1,q:"Which aspect of an ITIL Official Practice Guide describes the roles, competencies, and responsibilities needed for a specific practice?",o:["Organizations and people","Capability assessment and development","General information","Partners and suppliers"],e:"The ‘organizations and people’ aspect of an ITIL Official Practice Guide describes: “Roles, competencies, and responsibilities” as well as “organizational solutions and teams (specific to the practice)” Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4",x:["Capability assessment and development contains a “Description of the capability levels according to ITIL Maturity Model […]. Capability criteria for the practice. Recommendations for self-assessment of the practice capability level”. Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4","‘General information’ covers: “Purpose and description; Key terms and concepts; Scope; Practice success factors; Key metrics” Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4","‘Partners and suppliers’ covers: “Dependencies of the practice on third parties” and “Support from third parties”. Ref 5.5.1, Table 5.4"]});
QB.push({m:"m6",l:"m6c",of:1,q:"Which of the following describes a management practice?",o:["A set of organizational resources and capabilities designed to perform work or accomplish an objective","A series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products","A visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with stakeholders","A set of interconnected activities that an organization performs to create and deliver products"],e:"“A management practice is a set of organizational resources and capabilities designed and adopted for performing work or accomplishing an objective.” Ref 5.5",x:["This is ‘value stream’: “[…] a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.” Ref 2.3.1","This is ‘operating model’ which is “[…] a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself”. Ref 5.4.1","This is value chain: “[…] an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service.” Ref 2.3.1"]});
QB.push({m:"m9",l:"m9b",of:1,q:"Which dimension of product and service management is directly influenced by leadership commitment to psychological safety and continual learning?",o:["Organizations and people","Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes","Information and technology"],e:"The ‘Organization and people’ dimension is most concerned with establishing a culture of trust. “Digitally enabled organizations are under pressure to continually improve their performance under changing market conditions. […]. The challenge for these organizations is to foster an effective set of shared beliefs, perceptions, and values in relation to risks. This, in turn, should create a safety culture, and result in behaviour that is beneficial for all stakeholders, including the members of the organization.” Ref 2.2.1",x:["The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension focuses on external relationships and agreements, not on the internal culture of trust. It “[…] encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services. It also incorporates contracts and other agreements between the organization and its partners or suppliers.” Ref 2.5","The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension is concerned with workflows and efficiency. It “[…] addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3","“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4"]});
QB.push({m:"m9",l:"m9e",of:1,q:"Which dimension of product and service management addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows?",o:["Value streams and processes","Partners and suppliers","Information and technology","Organizations and people"],e:"“The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension address organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3",x:["“The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services. It also incorporates contracts and other agreements between the organization and its partners or suppliers.” Ref 2.5","“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4","The ‘organizations and people’ dimension ensures that an organization’s “[…] roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, are well defined and support its overall strategy and operating model”. It also ensures “[…] the way an organization is structured and managed, […]”. Ref 2.2"]});
QB.push({m:"m9",l:"m9c",of:1,q:"Which dimension of product and service management addresses data and technologies used in digital product and services?",o:["Information and technology","Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes","Organizations and people"],e:"“The ‘information and technology’ dimension addresses data, information, and technologies used in digital products and services, as well as those used as part of the organization’s product and service management systems.” Ref 2.4",x:["“The ‘partners and suppliers’ dimension encompasses an organization’s relationships with other organizations that are involved in the discovery, design, build, transition, operation, delivery, support, and/or continual improvement of products and services. It also incorporates contracts and other agreements between the organization and its partners or suppliers.” Ref 2.5","“The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.” Ref 2.3","The ’organizations and people’ dimension ensures that “[…] the way an organization is structured and managed, as well as its roles, responsibilities, and systems of authority and communication, are well defined and support its overall strategy and operating model.” Ref 2.2"]});
QB.push({m:"m10",l:"m10a",of:1,q:"How does ITIL collaborate with DevOps practices to support digital product and service management?",o:["By providing the framework while DevOps supplies practices and techniques","By replacing DevOps practices with standardized service management processes","By limiting DevOps usage to software development activities","By positioning DevOps as a governance framework over ITIL"],e:"“DevOps is a valuable complementary approach which should be used as a source of detailed practical advice enabling such activities of the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle” explaining that ITIL provides the framework while DevOps supplies practices and techniques. Ref 7.3.1",x:["“DevOps is a valuable complementary approach […]” indicates DevOps is not replaced but used alongside ITIL. Ref 7.3.1","“DevOps practices […] enabling such activities of the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle as ‘discover’, ‘design’, ‘acquire’, ‘build’, ‘transition’, and ‘operate’” shows DevOps is not limited to development only. Ref 7.3.1","“[…] ITIL® provides the framework for effective digital product and service management, […]” clarifies governance is provided by ITIL, not DevOps. Ref 7.3"]});
QB.push({m:"m10",l:"m10a",of:1,q:"Why is project management important within the ITIL framework?",o:["It supports the implementation and transformation of products and services","It governs daily service operations","It replaces product and service management practices","It defines service level targets and metrics"],e:"“PRINCE2 equips professionals with the tools to manage projects that implement or transform those products and services” explains the importance of project management for delivery and change.",x:["","",""]});
QB.push({m:"m10",l:"m10a",of:1,q:"How can ITIL and PRINCE2 Agile be used together to deliver products and services?",o:["ITIL provides service management guidance while PRINCE2 Agile manages project delivery","ITIL replaces PRINCE2 governance structures","PRINCE2 Agile defines service management practices","ITIL manages projects while PRINCE2 Agile manages the daily and overall delivery of services"],e:"“While ITIL focuses on the digital product and services management, PRINCE2 equips professionals with the tools to manage projects” explains their complementary use in delivery.",x:["","",""]});

;
// ITIL (Version 5) Foundation exam simulations: original questions in the official exam style.
// o[0] is ALWAYS the correct answer (the app shuffles). x[i] explains why o[i+1] is wrong.
window.PAPERS=[
{id:"A",t:"Simulation A",qs:[
{m:"m1",l:"m1a",ty:"std",q:"A driverless car has physical components such as wheels, seats, and a body, but it cannot perform its core function of driving passengers without its autonomous driving software. How is this product classified in relation to digital technology?",o:["Digitally enabled","Digitally supported","Purely digital","Purely analogue"],e:"'Digitally enabled' products have analogue and physical components but depend on digital technology in fulfilling their core functions. Without the software, the car's potential value disappears.",x:["Digitally supported products perform their core functions without digital technology and use it only for non-core functions.","Purely digital products are software products with no analogue or physical components, but this car has physical components.","Purely analogue products work without digital technology, but this car cannot drive without its software."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1a",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nDigital product and service management is a set of specialized organizational [?] for enabling value for customers in the form of digital products and services.",o:["capabilities","practices","resources","value streams"],e:"ITIL defines digital product and service management as 'a set of specialized organizational capabilities for enabling value for customers in the form of digital products and services'.",x:["Management practices are sets of organizational resources and capabilities, but 'practices' is not the word used in this definition.","Products combine an organization's resources, but this definition describes specialized organizational capabilities.","Value streams are series of steps to create and deliver products and services. They are not the term used in this definition."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1b",ty:"std",q:"A broadband provider gives each new subscriber a router, which the subscriber owns from then on, together with the associated rights and responsibilities. Which component of the service offering does this represent?",o:["Transfer of goods","Access to resources","Service actions","Service interaction"],e:"Goods are tangible resources transferred from a service provider to a service consumer, together with ownership and associated rights and responsibilities. Handing over the router for the subscriber to own is a transfer of goods.",x:["With access to resources, the consumer uses the provider's resources while ownership stays with the service provider.","Service actions are performed by the provider, or by the provider and the consumer, for example user training or consultations.","Service interaction happens while a service is consumed. It is not one of the three components a service offering may include."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1c",ty:"std",q:"In the ITIL Service Relationship Model, what flows from the service consumer organization to the service provider organization?",o:["Needs, requirements, and feedback","Service delivery and support","Goods and ownership rights","Budget approval and sponsorship"],e:"Figure 1.5 shows service delivery flowing from the service provider organization, while needs, requirements, and feedback flow from the service consumer organization.",x:["Service delivery flows in the opposite direction, from the service provider organization to the consumer.","Goods are transferred from the provider to the consumer, not from the consumer to the provider.","Authorizing budget is the sponsor role inside the consumer organization. It is not the flow shown between organizations in the model."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1d",ty:"std",q:"A meal delivery service brings a prepared dinner to a customer's door on time. Thanks to this, the customer can host friends at home without spending the evening cooking. In ITIL terms, which is the OUTCOME?",o:["The customer can host friends without spending the evening cooking","The prepared dinner is delivered to the customer's door on time","The courier completes the delivery route and records the drop-off","The provider charges a delivery fee for each order it fulfils"],e:"An outcome is a result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outputs. The delivered dinner is an output, and hosting friends without cooking is the result the customer achieves.",x:["The delivered dinner is an output: a tangible deliverable of the provider's activity.","Completing the route is the provider's activity, which produces outputs rather than the customer's result.","A delivery fee is a cost imposed by the service, not an outcome for the customer."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1d",ty:"std",q:"Patients enter their symptoms and medical history in a clinic's booking app before an appointment. The clinic uses this information to prepare, and both the patients and the clinic achieve a better visit. Which statement best describes how value is created here?",o:["Provider and consumer jointly create value by combining resources and actions","The provider creates the value alone and delivers it to a passive consumer","Value is created only when goods are transferred to the patient","The consumer creates the value alone, and the provider only charges a fee"],e:"Value co-creation is the collaborative process by which service providers, consumers, and other stakeholders jointly create value through the use of products and services, by combining resources, capabilities, and actions across the service relationship.",x:["Value is co-created: consumers contribute their own resources and actions, as the patients do here.","Transfer of goods is only one possible part of a service offering, and value can be co-created without it.","The provider contributes resources and capabilities too, so value is not created by the consumer alone."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2a",ty:"std",q:"A retail company acquires a cloud analytics service. The finance director authorizes the budget for it, the head of analytics defines the requirements and takes responsibility for the results, and store managers use the dashboards. Which role does the finance director have?",o:["Sponsor","Customer","User","Product vendor"],e:"The sponsor is the service consumer role that authorizes budget for service consumption. The finance director approves the budget, so acts as the sponsor.",x:["The customer defines the requirements and takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption, which is the head of analytics here.","Users use the services, which describes the store managers.","A product vendor is an organization responsible for creating and continually improving digital products, not a role inside the consumer organization."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2b",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about collaborative service relationships (partnerships) are CORRECT?\n1. Their typical focus is innovation and growth.\n2. They typically involve only the operational level of the organizations.\n3. They may use bespoke contracts, outcome-based agreements, or no formal agreement.\n4. They are typical for commercial off-the-shelf and highly standardized commodity services.",o:["1 and 3","1 and 2","2 and 4","3 and 4"],e:"Table 3.1 shows that collaborative relationships typically focus on innovation and growth and use bespoke contracts, outcome-based agreements, or no formal agreement.",x:["Statement 2 is wrong: collaborative relationships typically involve the operational, tactical, and strategic levels.","Both are wrong: partnerships involve all three organizational levels, and off-the-shelf commodity services are typical of basic relationships.","Statement 4 is wrong: collaborative relationships typically involve custom or bespoke services with unique value propositions."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2c",ty:"std",q:"A service provider wants to understand which aspects of its own organization its consumers can see, and which aspects of the consumers' organizations are visible to it. Which concept describes this?",o:["Band of visibility","Service journey","Service interaction","Service level"],e:"The band of visibility is the sum of the aspects of the service consumer organizations visible to the service provider, and the aspects of the service provider organizations visible to the service consumer.",x:["The service journey focuses on activities and interactions over time, not on the visible aspects of each organization.","Service interaction is how the provider and consumer engage while the service is provided and consumed, not what each can see of the other.","A service level is a set of metrics that define expected or achieved service quality."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2d",ty:"neg",q:"Which is NOT one of the four categories of service level metrics?",o:["Availability","Utility","Sustainability","User experience"],e:"The four categories of service level metrics are utility, warranty, user experience, and sustainability. Availability is one of the areas that warranty typically addresses, not a category of its own.",x:["Utility is a category: the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need.","Sustainability is a category: the assurance of meeting requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth.","User experience is a category: the sum of the functional and emotional interactions with a service as perceived by a user."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2d",ty:"std",q:"A digital service meets all its agreed requirements for availability and capacity. However, the provider cannot show that the service will continue to meet requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth. Which category of service level metrics needs attention?",o:["Sustainability","Warranty","Utility","User experience"],e:"Sustainability is the assurance that a product or service meets and will continue to meet the requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth.",x:["Warranty typically addresses availability, capacity, security, and continuity, and the service already meets its agreed requirements here.","Utility is the functionality offered to meet a particular need, summarized as 'what the service does'.","User experience is the sum of the functional and emotional interactions with a service as perceived by a user."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2a",ty:"std",q:"A grocery retailer consumes cloud hosting from one company and payment services from another, while it provides online ordering and home delivery services to its own customers. What does this illustrate?",o:["An organization can be a service consumer and a service provider at the same time","An organization must choose to be either a provider or a consumer","Only the organizations at the end of a supply chain act as service consumers","Service relationships always involve exactly one provider and one consumer"],e:"In the ITIL Service Relationship Model, organizations form chains and networks, where each organization acts as a service consumer for multiple providers and a service provider for multiple consumers.",x:["Organizations are not limited to one role: each can be a consumer for some providers and a provider for some consumers.","Organizations throughout the chain consume services, as the retailer consuming hosting and payment services shows.","Organizations form chains and networks, acting as consumers for multiple providers and providers for multiple consumers."]},
{m:"m3",l:"m3a",ty:"neg",q:"Which is NOT a component of the ITIL Value System?",o:["Service offerings","Governance","Continual improvement","Practices"],e:"Figure 1.7 shows the components of the ITIL Value System as guiding principles, governance, the value chain, practices, and continual improvement. Service offerings are formal descriptions of services, not a component of the value system.",x:["Governance is a component of the ITIL Value System.","Continual improvement is a component of the ITIL Value System.","Practices are a component of the ITIL Value System that enable and support the value chain."]},
{m:"m4",l:"m4a",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nGovernance is the system by which an organization is directed and [?].",o:["controlled","improved","measured","funded"],e:"ITIL defines governance as 'the system by which an organization is directed and controlled'.",x:["Improvement is not part of the definition. Governance is about how the organization is directed and controlled.","Measurement supports monitoring, but the definition says 'directed and controlled'.","Funding decisions can be governed, but the definition says 'directed and controlled'."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5a",ty:"std",q:"Before redesigning its self-service portal, a team first identifies who its service consumers and key stakeholders are, why they use the portal, and how they feel when using it. Which guiding principle is the team applying?",o:["Focus on value","Start where you are","Think and work holistically","Collaborate and promote visibility"],e:"When focusing on value, the first step is to know who is being served. The provider needs to know why consumers use the services and how they feel when using them.",x:["'Start where you are' is about observing and reusing what already exists, not identifying who is served and why.","'Think and work holistically' is about handling activities in an integrated way, as a whole rather than as separate parts.","'Collaborate and promote visibility' is about involving the right people in the correct roles and making work visible."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5b",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the 'start where you are' guiding principle are CORRECT?\n1. Reports can be relied on to reflect reality accurately.\n2. Measurement should be used to support, not replace, what is observed.\n3. The act of measuring never affects the results being measured.\n4. Over-reliance on data analytics and reporting can introduce bias and risk into decision-making.",o:["2 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"'Start where you are' advises using measurement to support, not replace, what is observed, and warns that over-reliance on data analytics and reporting can unintentionally introduce bias and risk into decision-making.",x:["Statement 1 is wrong: there is frequently a discrepancy between reports and reality.","Both are wrong: reports often differ from reality, and the act of measuring can sometimes affect results.","Statement 3 is wrong: the act of measuring can sometimes affect results, making them inaccurate."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5c",ty:"std",q:"A team delivers a large change in short timeboxed iterations and builds user feedback into each one. According to ITIL, which benefit should this way of working provide?",o:["Faster responses to customer and business needs","A fixed scope that cannot change once agreed","Fewer iterations, each with a longer duration","Less need to collect stakeholder feedback"],e:"Working in a timeboxed, iterative manner with feedback loops embedded into the workflow allows greater flexibility, faster responses to customer and business needs, earlier detection of failures, and better overall quality.",x:["Iterative work increases flexibility, and initiatives are re-evaluated to reflect changes in circumstances.","The principle favours manageable iterations that return tangible results in a timely manner.","Feedback must be built into every iteration and sought before, throughout, and after each one."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5d",ty:"std",q:"A project manager insists that every stakeholder must agree before any decision can be made, and the project has stalled. What does the 'collaborate and promote visibility' guiding principle advise?",o:["Collaboration does not mean consensus","Decisions require the agreement of all stakeholders","Only customers should be involved in decisions","Visibility matters less than reaching full agreement"],e:"A recommendation for applying 'collaborate and promote visibility' is that collaboration does not mean consensus. Involving the right people does not require everyone to agree before progress is made.",x:["This contradicts the recommendation that collaboration does not mean consensus.","Customers are the most significant stakeholders, but suppliers, relationship managers, and internal and external teams are also important collaborators.","Lack of visibility causes poor decisions, disengagement, and low adoption, so visibility is essential."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5f",ty:"std",q:"A team designing a new returns process tries to define a separate procedure for every possible exception, and the design is becoming very complicated. Which guiding principle does this conflict with?",o:["Keep it simple and practical","Focus on value","Progress iteratively with feedback","Optimize and automate"],e:"'Keep it simple and practical' warns that providing a solution for every exception leads to over-complication. Designers cannot cover every exception, so they should design rules to handle exceptions effectively.",x:["'Focus on value' links all activities to value for stakeholders. Exception handling is specifically addressed by 'keep it simple and practical'.","'Progress iteratively with feedback' is about manageable iterations and feedback loops.","'Optimize and automate' is about optimizing work and automating it where appropriate."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5g",ty:"std",q:"An organization decides to apply only the 'optimize and automate' guiding principle to its improvement programme, because efficiency is its top priority. What does ITIL recommend?",o:["Consider the relevance of all the principles and how they complement each other","Apply 'optimize and automate' first and the other principles in a fixed order","Apply each principle separately so that they do not influence each other","Choose the one or two principles that best fit the organization's top priority"],e:"The guiding principles interact with and depend on each other. Organizations should not rely on just one or two principles, but consider the relevance of all of them and how they complement each other.",x:["There is no fixed order or hierarchy among the guiding principles.","The principles interact with and depend on each other, so they should not be applied in isolation.","ITIL states that organizations should not rely on just one or two principles."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6a",ty:"std",q:"To handle an urgent issue, a product team moves straight from 'support' to 'design' and then to 'build', without following the order in which the activities are usually listed. Is this consistent with ITIL?",o:["Yes, because lifecycle management activities are not performed in a fixed sequence","No, because every activity must follow the order from 'discover' to 'support'","No, because 'support' can only be followed by the 'operate' activity","Yes, because 'design' and 'build' are optional for digital products"],e:"The lifecycle management activities enable and support the lifecycle but are not performed in a fixed sequence. From the organizational perspective they form a value chain, and in practice they combine differently.",x:["ITIL states that the lifecycle management activities are not performed in a fixed sequence.","No rule restricts which activity can follow 'support', because the activities are not performed in a fixed sequence.","ITIL does not describe 'design' and 'build' as optional. The approach is valid because there is no fixed sequence."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6c",ty:"neg",q:"Which of the following practices is NOT in the group of 22 product and service management practices?",o:["Relationship management","Service desk","Business analysis","Information security management"],e:"Relationship management is one of the 12 general management practices. Service desk, business analysis, and information security management belong to the 22 product and service management practices.",x:["Service desk is one of the 22 product and service management practices.","Business analysis is one of the 22 product and service management practices.","Information security management is one of the 22 product and service management practices in ITIL (Version 5)."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6b",ty:"std",q:"According to ITIL, what do an organization's core value streams depend on?",o:["Management practices","Service level agreements","Governing bodies","Product roadmaps"],e:"The organization's purpose defines the operating model, which includes value chain activities that form core value streams. Core value streams depend on management practices, which enable the value chain activities and core value streams. See Figure 5.3 in the lesson.",x:["SLAs define the agreed level of each service with a customer, but core value streams depend on management practices.","Governing bodies direct and control the organization, but core value streams depend on management practices.","Product roadmaps are the key output of 'discover', not what core value streams depend on."],img:"figs/itil/m6b_5.webp"},
{m:"m6x",l:"a1",ty:"std",q:"A company continually reviews external influences such as new regulations, economic trends, and technology changes, together with input from customers and shareholders, to keep its product roadmap aligned with consumer needs and its strategy. Which value chain activity is this?",o:["Discover","Design","Acquire","Deliver"],e:"The purpose of 'discover' is to ensure continual alignment of the product roadmaps and related service offerings with the needs of service consumers and with the organizational strategy. It is influenced by PESTLE factors and stakeholder input.",x:["'Design' creates specifications and prototypes for products and services.","'Acquire' secures and allocates the necessary resources efficiently.","'Deliver' provides services to users, manages user onboarding and offboarding, and gathers consumer feedback."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a2",ty:"std",q:"Before development starts, a team builds a clickable mock-up of a new mobile app to test hypotheses with users and refine the design. What is the team creating?",o:["A product prototype","A product specification","A product release","A service offering"],e:"A product/service prototype is an initial version that demonstrates basic form, functionality, and operational capabilities. It is used to test and refine the design and test hypotheses, ranging from visual mock-ups to fully functional models.",x:["A specification is a detailed document outlining critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics, not a working mock-up for testing hypotheses.","A release is a version of a product made available for use, which comes after design and build.","A service offering is a formal description of one or more services for a target consumer group."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a5",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about continuous delivery and continuous deployment are CORRECT?\n1. Continuous deployment relies on continuous delivery.\n2. Continuous delivery automatically deploys every change that passes automated tests, without additional authorization.\n3. Continuous deployment requires manual authorization for every change.\n4. With continuous delivery, deployment decisions are taken case by case.",o:["1 and 4","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"Continuous delivery enables software updates to be deployed to production at any time, with deployment decisions taken case by case. Continuous deployment automatically deploys every change that passes automated tests without additional authorization, and it relies on continuous delivery.",x:["Statement 2 is wrong: it describes continuous deployment, not continuous delivery.","Both are wrong: statement 2 describes continuous deployment, and continuous deployment needs no additional authorization.","Statement 3 is wrong: continuous deployment deploys changes to production without additional authorization."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a6",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nAn event is any change of [?] that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item.",o:["state","owner","version","priority"],e:"ITIL defines an event as 'any change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item'.",x:["A change of owner is not part of the definition. An event is a change of state.","A new version relates to releases. The definition of an event refers to a change of state.","Priority is not part of the definition. An event is any significant change of state."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a8",ty:"std",q:"Support teams notice that three incidents reported this week seem to share a common cause, which has not yet been analysed. In ITIL terms, what is this common cause?",o:["A problem","A known error","An event","A disaster"],e:"A problem is a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents. Because the cause has not yet been analysed, it is not yet a known error.",x:["A known error is a problem that has been analysed but not resolved, and this cause has not been analysed yet.","An event is any change of state that has significance for management, not the cause of incidents.","A disaster is a sudden unplanned event that causes great damage or serious loss, not the cause of several incidents."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a3",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the 'acquire' activity are CORRECT?\n1. Its key output is an updated product and service roadmap.\n2. It includes sourcing resources externally or reallocating them internally.\n3. It may be triggered by supplier review cycles, using CMDB data to inform decisions.\n4. It is performed only when a completely new product is being developed.",o:["2 and 3","1 and 2","1 and 4","3 and 4"],e:"The 'acquire' activity includes sourcing externally or reallocating internally. It may be triggered by new product, operations, or supplier review cycles, using CMDB data to inform decisions.",x:["Statement 1 is wrong: an updated roadmap is the key output of 'discover', while 'acquire' produces acquired resources and services.","Both are wrong: the roadmap is the output of 'discover', and 'acquire' can also be triggered by operations or supplier review cycles.","Statement 4 is wrong: 'acquire' may also be triggered by operations or supplier review cycles, not only by new products."]},
{m:"m7",l:"m7a",ty:"std",q:"A retailer has a value stream that provides newly hired store staff with accounts, devices, and training, so that they can serve the retailer's customers. How is this value stream categorized?",o:["Enabling value stream","Core value stream","Value chain pattern","Service journey"],e:"An enabling value stream enables value for internal customers to support the organization's core value streams. Equipping new staff supports the core value stream of serving customers.",x:["A core value stream enables value for consumers in the form intended by the operating model, such as serving the retailer's customers.","Value chain patterns summarize typical operating models for different organizational purposes. They are not a category of value stream.","A service journey is the sum of activities and interactions between providers and consumers, not a category of value stream."]},
{m:"m7",l:"m7b",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nIn value stream mapping, after the stream has been analysed, the next step is to map the '[?]' stream.",o:["to-be","as-is","end-to-end","core"],e:"The steps of value stream mapping are: identify the value stream, map the 'as-is' stream, analyse the stream, map the 'to-be' stream, and plan and implement improvements.",x:["The 'as-is' stream is mapped before the analysis, as the second step.","Value streams cover end-to-end delivery, but the step after the analysis is mapping the 'to-be' stream.","'Core' is a category of value stream, not a mapping step."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"std",q:"An improvement team has completed its planned actions. When it assesses progress against the objectives and expected value, it finds that the results fall short. What should it do?",o:["Plan additional actions or a new iteration","Close the initiative and report it as a failure","Restart the model by redefining the vision","Treat the original objectives as achieved"],e:"In the 'Are we getting there?' step, progress is assessed against objectives and value. If results fall short, additional actions or a new iteration are planned.",x:["When results fall short, ITIL recommends planning additional actions or a new iteration rather than simply closing the initiative.","The guidance for this step is to plan additional actions or a new iteration, not to start again from the vision.","Success should be confirmed only when progress actually meets the objectives and value."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the ITIL Continual Improvement Model are CORRECT?\n1. It is optimized for improvements in chaotic contexts.\n2. It must be completed in a single iteration.\n3. It is a universal model for iterative improvements within the value system.\n4. Detailed recommendations for it are given in the ITIL Continual Improvement Official Practice Guide.",o:["3 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","2 and 4"],e:"The ITIL Continual Improvement Model is a universal model for iterative improvements within the value system, optimized for ordered and complex contexts. Detailed recommendations are in the ITIL Continual Improvement Official Practice Guide.",x:["Both are wrong: the model is optimized for ordered and complex contexts, and it is designed for iterative improvements.","Statement 1 is wrong: the model is optimized for ordered and complex contexts, not chaotic ones.","Statement 2 is wrong: the model is designed for iterative improvements."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"std",q:"While carrying out agreed improvement actions, a team realizes that its approach is not working. It decides to change direction and revisit an earlier step of the model. How does ITIL view this?",o:["It is acceptable, as 'take action' encourages working iteratively","It is a failure, because the agreed plan must be followed","It is acceptable only if the governing body approves it","It is not allowed, as each step can be performed only once"],e:"In the 'take action' step, agreed actions and experiments are conducted iteratively. Teams may adapt, change direction, or revisit earlier steps, while staying focused on objectives and managing risks and visibility.",x:["ITIL encourages adapting and changing direction during 'take action' rather than following the plan rigidly.","No approval by the governing body is needed to adapt during 'take action'.","Revisiting earlier steps is explicitly part of working iteratively."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9c",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nAgentic AI refers to AI systems that are designed to act on behalf of users by autonomously performing predefined tasks within agreed [?], while remaining subject to human oversight and governance.",o:["boundaries","budgets","service levels","timelines"],e:"ITIL defines agentic AI as AI systems designed to act on behalf of users by autonomously performing predefined tasks within agreed boundaries, while remaining subject to human oversight and governance.",x:["Budgets are not part of the definition. Agentic AI acts within agreed boundaries.","Service levels define expected or achieved service quality. The definition refers to agreed boundaries.","Timelines are not part of the definition. Agentic AI performs tasks within agreed boundaries."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9b",ty:"std",q:"Three teams that rarely communicate with each other jointly build a product. The result is a product made of three poorly integrated parts that mirror the teams' structure. Which concept explains this?",o:["Conway's Law","The Reverse Conway approach","Safety culture","Complexity thinking"],e:"Conway's Law states that organizations which design systems are constrained to produce designs which are copies of their communication structures. How an organization communicates and is structured strongly influences how its products are designed.",x:["The Reverse Conway approach deliberately designs team structures to match the desired architecture, which did not happen here.","Safety culture is an organizational culture in which people are comfortable being (and expressing) themselves.","Complexity thinking is an approach to analysis and decision-making based on understanding the levels of complexity in systems."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9d",ty:"std",q:"A retailer decides to stop running its own data centre and to use a cloud provider instead, because sourcing the service externally is more economical. Which factor influencing supplier strategy is the main driver?",o:["Cost concerns","Strategic focus","Demand patterns","Corporate culture"],e:"Cost concerns shape supplier strategy by optimizing according to whether sourcing externally is more economical.",x:["Strategic focus is about core versus non-core and outsourced functions, while the stated reason here is economic.","Demand patterns refer to seasonal or variable demand.","Corporate culture refers to historical preferences and bias."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9a",ty:"std",q:"According to the ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management, which external factors affect every dimension?",o:["Political, economic, social, technological, legal, and environmental","Utility, warranty, user experience, and sustainability of services","Strategic, tactical, and operational decisions made by the governing body","Discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, and support"],e:"Every dimension is affected by multiple external factors: political, economic, social, technological, legal, and environmental (PESTLE). External factors influence all four dimensions and must be considered. See Figure 1.4 in the lesson.",x:["Utility, warranty, user experience, and sustainability are the four categories of service level metrics, not the factors affecting the dimensions.","Strategic, tactical, and operational are organizational levels involved in service relationships, not the external factors that affect the dimensions.","These are the eight lifecycle stages and activities, not the external factors that affect the four dimensions."],img:"figs/itil/m9a_2.webp"},
{m:"m9",l:"m9e",ty:"std",q:"According to the 'value streams and processes' dimension, how should organizations deal with the varying complexity of their work contexts?",o:["Embrace it and develop the ability to respond to specific circumstances","Standardize all work so that every context can be treated as ordered","Avoid complex contexts by outsourcing all complex work to suppliers","Apply the same fixed process to every situation"],e:"The value streams and processes dimension states that organizations should embrace the varying complexity of their work contexts and develop the ability to respond according to the specific circumstances.",x:["ITIL describes four types of complexity context (ordered, complex, chaotic, and confused), so not every context can be treated as ordered.","Outsourcing complex work is not the recommendation. Organizations should develop the ability to respond to each context.","Organizations should respond according to the specific circumstances, not apply one fixed process everywhere."]},
{m:"m10",l:"m10a",ty:"std",q:"A retail organization manages its digital products and services using ITIL. Its development teams want practical techniques for rapid, continuous delivery. According to the course, what is the best approach?",o:["Combine ITIL with DevOps ways of working","Replace ITIL with PRINCE2 for all operations","Stop applying ITIL practices to development","Use PRINCE2 to automate software deployments"],e:"ITIL defines how organizations manage digital products and services, while DevOps provides practical techniques for rapid, continuous delivery. Together they enable resilient, adaptive, and customer-focused outcomes.",x:["PRINCE2 provides methods for project, programme, and portfolio management. It does not replace ITIL for operations.","ITIL and DevOps are complementary, so ITIL practices should work together with DevOps ways of working.","PRINCE2 supports the delivery of changes through projects, while rapid, continuous delivery techniques come from DevOps."]}
]},
{id:"B",t:"Simulation B",qs:[
{m:"m1",l:"m1a",ty:"std",q:"An organization wants its employees to have email, without having to manage the servers, licences, security patching, or the specific costs and risks that come with them. According to ITIL definitions, what does the organization need?",o:["A service","A product","Goods","A value chain"],e:"A service is a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks.",x:["A product is a configuration of an organization's resources designed to offer value. Owning and running it would not remove the specific costs and risks.","Goods are tangible resources transferred with ownership, which would leave the organization managing them.","A value chain is an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service, not what a consumer acquires."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1b",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about service offerings are CORRECT?\n1. A service offering is a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group.\n2. Transfer of goods is common in digital services, and providers aim to increase it.\n3. When the consumer gains access to resources, ownership of the resources passes to the consumer.\n4. Service request fulfilment is a common form of service action.",o:["1 and 4","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"A service offering is a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group, and service request fulfilment is a common form of service action.",x:["Statement 2 is wrong: transfer of goods is rare in digital services, and providers aim to minimize the need for it.","Both are wrong: transfer of goods is rare in digital services, and resources accessed by the consumer remain owned by the provider.","Statement 3 is wrong: with access to resources, the resources remain owned by the service provider."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1c",ty:"std",q:"According to the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model, how are digital products and services managed over time?",o:["Through a shared lifecycle with eight stages","Through two separate lifecycles","Through four fixed lifecycle stages","Through a lifecycle that ends at build"],e:"The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model includes eight lifecycle stages. Organizations manage digital products and services throughout their shared lifecycle by performing eight lifecycle management activities of the same name.",x:["Digital products and services are managed throughout a shared lifecycle, not separate ones.","The model includes eight lifecycle stages, and the activities are not performed in a fixed sequence.","The lifecycle continues after building, through transition, operate, deliver, and support."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1d",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nValue is the perceived benefits, usefulness, and [?] of something.",o:["importance","cost","quality","availability"],e:"ITIL defines value as 'the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something'.",x:["Cost is the amount of money spent on an activity, resource, product, or service. It is not part of the definition of value.","Quality relates to service quality and service levels, not to the definition of value.","Availability is an area typically addressed by warranty, not part of the definition of value."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1d",ty:"std",q:"A city bike-sharing service helps commuters travel cheaply and flexibly, and it helps the city reduce traffic congestion and emissions. What does this illustrate?",o:["The same outputs can support different outcomes for different stakeholders","A service has a single outcome that all stakeholders share equally","Outcomes are the deliverables produced by the service provider","Value is created only for the consumer who pays for the service"],e:"Outcomes vary for different stakeholders. Service providers produce outputs that help consumers and other stakeholders achieve different outcomes, as in the ITIL Car Rental example of customers and cities.",x:["Outcomes vary for different stakeholders, so one service can support several different outcomes.","Deliverables are outputs. An outcome is a result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outputs.","Value co-creation involves providers, consumers, and other stakeholders, such as the city in this example."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1a",ty:"std",q:"A company sells subscriptions to a budgeting application that users download to their phones. The product consists only of software, with no hardware or other physical parts. How is this product classified in relation to digital technology?",o:["Purely digital","Digitally enabled","Digitally supported","Purely analogue"],e:"'Purely digital' products are software products with no analogue or physical components, like this budgeting application.",x:["Digitally enabled products have analogue and physical components but depend on digital technology for their core functions.","Digitally supported products perform their core functions without digital technology and use it only for non-core functions.","Purely analogue products work without digital technology."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2a",ty:"std",q:"A software company creates and continually improves a point-of-sale application and its related service offerings. Retailers then acquire services based on this application from local IT providers. Which role does the software company have?",o:["Product vendor","Service provider","Service consumer","Sponsor"],e:"A product vendor is an organization responsible for the creation and continual improvement of digital products and related service offerings.",x:["The local IT providers deliver and support the services, which makes them the service providers.","The retailers procure and use the services, which makes them service consumers.","A sponsor is a service consumer role that authorizes budget for service consumption."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2b",ty:"std",q:"A company acquires a CRM platform that must be configured and customized to fit its sales processes. Operational and tactical managers from both organizations meet regularly to improve the effectiveness of the service. Which type of service relationship is this?",o:["Cooperative","Basic","Collaborative","Transactional"],e:"Cooperative relationships typically focus on improvement and effectiveness, involve the operational and tactical levels, and cover services that must be configured or customized to fulfil the needs of the service consumer.",x:["Basic relationships focus on support and efficiency at the operational level, with standardized or off-the-shelf services.","Collaborative relationships focus on innovation and growth, involve the strategic level too, and cover bespoke services.","ITIL describes three types of service relationship: basic, cooperative, and collaborative. 'Transactional' is not one of them."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2c",ty:"std",q:"A consumer has completed onboarding. Its users are now using the service, and both the consumer and the provider engage in the service interaction. Which step of the ITIL Service Journey Model is this?",o:["Co-create","Agree","Onboard","Reflect"],e:"In the 'co-create' step, the consumer consumes the service, the provider provides it, and both engage in the service interaction.",x:["In 'agree', both parties negotiate and agree target service levels.","In 'onboard', the consumer procures and performs its transition activities, and the provider plans and executes its own.","In 'reflect', both parties track, assess, and evaluate value and evaluate the journey."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2d",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nUser experience is the sum of the functional and [?] interactions with a service and service provider as perceived by a service user.",o:["emotional","financial","contractual","technical"],e:"ITIL defines user experience (UX) as 'the sum of the functional and emotional interactions with a service and service provider as perceived by a service user'.",x:["Financial aspects relate to costs, not to the definition of user experience.","Contractual aspects belong to agreements such as SLAs, not to the definition of user experience.","Technical aspects are not part of the definition. User experience combines functional and emotional interactions."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2d",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about Service Level Agreements (SLAs) are CORRECT?\n1. An SLA is an informal, undocumented promise made by a service provider.\n2. The formality, scope, and customization of an SLA depend on the service type and the relationship.\n3. SLAs for user-facing services should include user experience metrics.\n4. SLAs for digital services should cover warranty only.",o:["2 and 3","1 and 2","1 and 4","3 and 4"],e:"An SLA is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer. Its formality, scope, and customization depend on service type and relationship, and user-facing services should include user experience metrics.",x:["Statement 1 is wrong: an SLA is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer.","Both are wrong: an SLA is documented, and digital service SLAs should cover utility, warranty, and sustainability.","Statement 4 is wrong: digital service SLAs should cover utility, warranty, and sustainability."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2a",ty:"std",q:"A self-employed web designer provides services to several small businesses. According to ITIL, can the designer be considered an organization?",o:["Yes, because an organization may be a person or a group of people","No, because an organization must be an entire legal entity such as a company","No, because an organization must always consist of several people","Yes, but only while the designer is acting as a service provider"],e:"ITIL defines an organization as a person or a group of people that has its own functions with responsibilities, authorities, and relationships to achieve its objectives. It may be a self-employed individual, a multinational corporation, or anything in between.",x:["An organization may be an entire legal entity, but also part of one, a group of entities, or a self-employed individual.","The definition includes a single person, such as a self-employed individual.","Being an organization does not depend on the role. In service relationships, an organization may act as a provider or a consumer."]},
{m:"m3",l:"m3a",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nThe purpose of the ITIL Value System is to ensure that the organization continually and sustainably [?] value with all stakeholders through the use and management of products and services.",o:["co-creates","delivers","captures","measures"],e:"The purpose of the ITIL VS is to ensure that the organization continually and sustainably co-creates value with all stakeholders through the use and management of products and services.",x:["Value is not simply delivered by the provider. The ITIL VS ensures that it is co-created with stakeholders.","Capturing value for the organization alone is not the purpose. Value is co-created with all stakeholders.","Measuring value is not the wording of the purpose. The ITIL VS ensures that value is co-created with all stakeholders."]},
{m:"m4",l:"m4a",ty:"neg",q:"Governance of digital technology is realized through a set of activities performed by the governing body. Which is NOT one of these activities?",o:["Execute","Evaluate","Direct","Monitor"],e:"Governance of digital technology is realized through four activities: engage stakeholders, evaluate, direct, and monitor. Executing the work is not one of them.",x:["'Evaluate' is a governance activity: assessing the current state of the organization, its strategies, portfolios, policies, and relationships.","'Direct' is a governance activity: establishing and modifying policies and strategies.","'Monitor' is a governance activity: checking performance and compliance."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5a",ty:"std",q:"Last year, customers valued a bank's budgeting feature highly. Since a competitor launched a better alternative, the same feature is rarely used and is seen as less useful. What does this illustrate about value for the service consumer?",o:["It changes over time and in different circumstances","It is defined by the provider's own internal objectives","It stays constant once the service has been designed","It depends only on the price charged for the service"],e:"Under 'focus on value', value for the service consumer is defined by their own needs, is subjective, and changes over time and in different circumstances.",x:["Value for the consumer is defined by the consumer's own needs, not by the provider's objectives.","Value for the consumer changes over time and in different circumstances.","Value is achieved through support of intended outcomes and optimization of the consumer's costs and risks, not price alone."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5b",ty:"neg",q:"A team is applying the 'start where you are' guiding principle. Which action would NOT be part of applying this principle?",o:["Simplify and/or optimize before automating","Evaluate the current state","Identify and build on successful existing practices","Apply your risk management skills"],e:"'Simplify and/or optimize before automating' is a recommendation for applying 'optimize and automate'. The other options are recommendations for applying 'start where you are'.",x:["Evaluating the current state is a recommendation for applying 'start where you are'.","Identifying and building on successful existing practices is a recommendation for applying 'start where you are'.","Applying risk management skills is a recommendation for applying 'start where you are'."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5c",ty:"std",q:"A team has spent months analysing every part of a complex system, but it has not started any improvement because it wants to understand everything first. Which recommendation of 'progress iteratively with feedback' is the team ignoring?",o:["Comprehend the whole but do something","Fast does not mean incomplete","Collaboration does not mean consensus","Do fewer things but do them better"],e:"A recommendation for applying 'progress iteratively with feedback' is to comprehend the whole but do something. Teams should not delay all action until they understand everything.",x:["'Fast does not mean incomplete' warns against delivering incomplete results, not against delaying action.","'Collaboration does not mean consensus' is a recommendation for 'collaborate and promote visibility'.","'Do fewer things but do them better' is a recommendation for 'keep it simple and practical'."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5e",ty:"std",q:"When planning a new store payment system, a team considers staff skills, data and technology, supplier contracts, and end-to-end workflows together, rather than looking at the technology alone. Which guiding principle is the team applying?",o:["Think and work holistically","Keep it simple and practical","Start where you are","Optimize and automate"],e:"In the ITIL VS, a holistic approach includes considering all four dimensions of product and service management: organizations and people, information and technology, partners and suppliers, and value streams and processes.",x:["'Keep it simple and practical' focuses on minimizing steps and eliminating what does not add value.","'Start where you are' focuses on assessing and reusing what already exists.","'Optimize and automate' focuses on optimizing work and then automating it where appropriate."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5f",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the 'keep it simple and practical' guiding principle are CORRECT?\n1. If a process, product, service, action, or metric fails to increase value or produce a useful outcome, it should be eliminated.\n2. Product solutions should generate only relevant data, with record-keeping optimized and automated.\n3. Every possible exception should have its own documented solution.\n4. Product teams should keep all requested features, whatever value they add.",o:["1 and 2","1 and 3","2 and 4","3 and 4"],e:"'Keep it simple and practical' says to eliminate anything that fails to increase value or produce a useful outcome, and that product solutions should generate only relevant data, with record-keeping optimized and automated.",x:["Statement 3 is wrong: providing a solution for every exception leads to over-complication.","Statement 4 is wrong: product teams should prioritize features that maximize value and dismiss those that do not.","Both are wrong: covering every exception over-complicates designs, and features that do not maximize value should be dismissed."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5g",ty:"std",q:"On the 'road to optimization', which step comes directly after understanding and agreeing the context in which the proposed optimization exists?",o:["Assess the current state of the proposed optimization","Execute the improvements in an iterative way","Continually monitor the impact of optimization","Agree the future state and priorities"],e:"The road to optimization starts with understanding and agreeing the context, then assessing the current state, agreeing the future state and priorities, ensuring stakeholder engagement and commitment, executing iteratively, and continually monitoring the impact.",x:["Iterative execution comes later, after the future state is agreed and stakeholder engagement is ensured.","Monitoring the impact is the last step on the road to optimization.","The future state and priorities are agreed after the current state has been assessed."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6a",ty:"std",q:"A provider's mobile app is well designed, but users complain about slow responses from its support teams and confusing billing processes. Which statement best explains their poor experience?",o:["Experience is shaped by both the digital product and the provider's system","Experience depends on the digital product alone, if it is well designed","Experience is shaped mainly by the provider's marketing and brand image","Experience is determined by the price compared with that of competitors"],e:"User and customer experience is shaped by the digital product and the service provider's system, so a well-designed app is not enough on its own.",x:["Experience depends on the provider's system as well as the product, as the complaints about support and billing show.","Brand image may set expectations, but ITIL states that experience is shaped by the digital product and the provider's system.","Price is not what the lifecycle model identifies as shaping user and customer experience."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6c",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the ITIL management practices are CORRECT?\n1. There are 22 general management practices.\n2. Each practice supports only one value chain activity.\n3. ITIL includes 34 management practices in two groups.\n4. Each value chain activity is enabled and supported by several management practices.",o:["3 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","2 and 4"],e:"ITIL includes 34 management practices in two groups (12 general management practices and 22 product and service management practices), and each value chain activity is supported and enabled by several practices.",x:["Both are wrong: there are 12 general management practices, and each practice contributes to several activities.","Statement 1 is wrong: there are 12 general and 22 product and service management practices.","Statement 2 is wrong: practices enable and support several value chain activities, as Table B.1 shows."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6c",ty:"std",q:"A practice owner wants guidance on the roles and competencies needed for a practice and on a suitable organizational solution. Which chapter of the ITIL Official Practice Guide should they consult?",o:["Organizations and people","Value streams and processes","General information","Partners and suppliers"],e:"In the ITIL Official Practice Guides, the 'Organizations and people' chapter covers roles and competencies and the organizational solution.",x:["'Value streams and processes' covers processes and the practice's contribution to value streams.","'General information' covers purpose, scope, key terms, success factors, and key metrics.","'Partners and suppliers' covers dependencies and support."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a4",ty:"std",q:"Developers in a product team merge their code changes into a central repository several times a day, and each merge triggers automated builds and tests. What is this?",o:["Continuous integration","Continuous delivery","Continuous deployment","Release management"],e:"Continuous integration is a set of techniques and tools that enables developers to frequently merge their code changes into a central repository, followed by automated builds and tests.",x:["Continuous delivery enables software updates to be deployed to production at any time, with deployment decisions taken case by case.","Continuous deployment automatically deploys every change that passes automated tests to production.","Release management is a management practice. Frequent merging followed by automated builds and tests is continuous integration."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a5",ty:"std",q:"An organization retires a legacy application, removes it from the live environment, and decommissions the resources it used. Which value chain activity includes this work?",o:["Transition","Operate","Support","Acquire"],e:"The 'transition' activity includes onboarding and offboarding suppliers and decommissioning resources and products, as well as moving new or updated products into live environments.",x:["'Operate' maintains and monitors live products and supporting systems.","'Support' identifies and resolves incidents and disasters.","'Acquire' secures and allocates resources, rather than decommissioning them."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a7",ty:"std",q:"A new employee joins a company, and the provider sets up the employee's access to the HR portal as part of agreed user onboarding. Which value chain activity is this?",o:["Deliver","Transition","Support","Build"],e:"The purpose of 'deliver' includes providing services to users and managing user onboarding and offboarding. It involves fulfilment of users' service requests, user onboarding, and other service actions.",x:["'Transition' covers onboarding and offboarding of suppliers, not users, and moving products into live environments.","'Support' identifies and resolves incidents and disasters.","'Build' develops, integrates, and tests products and services."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a8",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA known error is a problem that has been [?] but has not been resolved.",o:["analysed","prioritized","recorded","escalated"],e:"ITIL defines a known error as 'a problem that has been analysed but has not been resolved'.",x:["Prioritization is not part of the definition. A known error is a problem that has been analysed.","Recording a problem is not enough. A known error is a problem that has been analysed but not resolved.","Escalation is not part of the definition. The key point is that the problem has been analysed."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a6",ty:"std",q:"An SRE team analyses the metrics, logs, and traces produced by a distributed system to understand what is happening inside it. Which capability is the team relying on?",o:["Observability","Reliability","Availability","Continuous integration"],e:"Observability is the ability to understand the internal state of a complicated system by analysing its external outputs, such as metrics, logs, and traces.",x:["Reliability is the ability of a product, service, or configuration item to perform its intended function for a specified period of time or number of cycles.","Availability is an area that warranty typically addresses, not the ability to understand a system's internal state.","Continuous integration enables developers to frequently merge code changes, followed by automated builds and tests."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a3",ty:"std",q:"A team tracks how long it takes to secure resources after a request, the quality of the resources and services it obtains, and stakeholder satisfaction with the timelines. These are key metrics of which value chain activity?",o:["Acquire","Design","Transition","Deliver"],e:"The key metrics of 'acquire' include acquire cycle time, the quality of the acquired resources and services, adherence to the roadmap and guidelines, and stakeholder satisfaction with the acquire activities, timelines, and acquired resources.",x:["'Design' metrics include design cycle time and the quality of specifications and prototypes.","'Transition' metrics include the number and impact of transition errors and the transition cycle.","'Deliver' metrics include service performance against the agreed SLA targets."]},
{m:"m7",l:"m7a",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA core value stream is a value stream that enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization's [?].",o:["operating model","governing body","service catalogue","product roadmap"],e:"ITIL defines a core value stream as 'a value stream that enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization's operating model'.",x:["The governing body directs and controls the organization, but the definition refers to the operating model.","The definition of a core value stream refers to the operating model, not to a service catalogue.","A product roadmap is the key output of 'discover', not part of this definition."]},
{m:"m7",l:"m7b",ty:"std",q:"Having mapped its incident handling value stream, an organization now wants an ongoing focus on how the work is done. It will use automation and measurement to keep analysing and improving the stream and the practices that enable it. What is this approach?",o:["Value stream management","Value stream mapping","Value chain pattern","Service journey"],e:"Value stream management is an ongoing focus on how work is done: the analysis and improvement of the organization's value streams, supported by automation and measurement, which also improves the practices that enable the streams.",x:["Value stream mapping is the technique for visualizing and analysing streams, which the organization has already completed.","Value chain patterns summarize typical operating models for different organizational purposes.","The service journey describes provider and consumer activities and interactions over time, not the ongoing improvement of value streams."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"std",q:"At the start of an improvement initiative, a team translates the organization's vision and objectives for its own unit and agrees a high-level vision for the improvement. Which step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model is this?",o:["What is the vision?","Where are we now?","Where do we want to be?","How do we get there?"],e:"The 'What is the vision?' step translates the organization's vision and objectives for the relevant unit, team, or individual, and creates and agrees a high-level vision for the improvement.",x:["'Where are we now?' defines the current state, producing a baseline assessment.","'Where do we want to be?' defines the desired future state with clear improvement objectives.","'How do we get there?' plans the route from the current state to the target state."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"std",q:"An improvement team knows its current state and its target state, but the best route between them is unclear. It decides to identify hypotheses and design small experiments to test them. Which step of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model is this?",o:["How do we get there?","Take action","Are we getting there?","Where do we want to be?"],e:"In 'How do we get there?', the route from the current state to the target state is planned by combining vision, current state, and target state with expertise. When the path is unclear, hypotheses and experiments are identified.",x:["In 'take action', the agreed actions and experiments are conducted after they have been planned.","'Are we getting there?' assesses progress against objectives and value.","'Where do we want to be?' defines the target state, which the team already knows."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"neg",q:"The improvement targets were not achieved in the last iteration. Which action is NOT recommended by ITIL in this situation?",o:["Use change and knowledge management to embed the improvement","Inform stakeholders of the reasons for failure","Analyse, document, and share the lessons learned","Suggest improvements for the next iteration"],e:"Using change and knowledge management to embed improvements is recommended when targets are met. When targets are not met, ITIL recommends informing stakeholders, sharing lessons learned, suggesting improvements, and treating deviations as learning opportunities.",x:["Informing stakeholders of the reasons for failure is recommended when targets are not met.","Analysing, documenting, and sharing lessons learned is recommended when targets are not met.","Suggesting improvements for the next iteration is recommended when targets are not met."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9a",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management are CORRECT?\n1. Only the 'information and technology' dimension is affected by external factors.\n2. Their purpose is to ensure a holistic approach to product and service management.\n3. They apply only to services, not to products.\n4. Each dimension is critical to the effective and efficient facilitation of value for customers and stakeholders.",o:["2 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"The purpose of the four dimensions is to ensure a holistic approach to product and service management, and each dimension is critical to the effective and efficient facilitation of value for customers and stakeholders.",x:["Statement 1 is wrong: external factors influence all four dimensions.","Both are wrong: external factors influence all four dimensions, and the dimensions apply to both products and services.","Statement 3 is wrong: the four dimensions facilitate value in the form of products and services."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9c",ty:"std",q:"A company introduces rules, policies, standards, processes, and controls to keep its data assets secure, usable, and reliable throughout their lifecycle. What is the company establishing?",o:["Data governance","AI governance","Observability","Governance of digital technology"],e:"Data governance is a system of rules, policies, standards, processes, and controls that organizations implement to manage their data assets effectively, keeping data secure, usable, and reliable throughout its lifecycle.",x:["AI governance directs, controls, and monitors the use of artificial intelligence.","Observability is the ability to understand a system's internal state by analysing its external outputs.","Governance of digital technology covers the current and future use of digital technology through directing, overseeing, and accountability, which is broader than managing data assets."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9e",ty:"std",q:"A team launches a new loyalty feature in a new market. It could not predict in advance how customers would react, but afterwards it can understand why things happened as they did. Which complexity context does this describe?",o:["Complex","Ordered","Chaotic","Confused"],e:"In the ITIL four types of complexity context, a complex context is understandable in retrospect but unpredictable.",x:["An ordered context has clear causation.","A chaotic context is a crisis situation requiring immediate action.","A confused context is unclear and requires orientation before action."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9b",ty:"std",q:"A company organizes its teams around its products and services rather than around technical functions. According to ITIL, which way of categorizing organizational structures does this relate to?",o:["Scope of work and responsibility","Geography and location","Governance drivers","Decision-making authority"],e:"ITIL gives examples of how to categorize organizational structures: geography and location, governance drivers, and scope of work and responsibility (functional, product/service-based, or project-focused). Organizing teams around products and services relates to scope of work and responsibility.",x:["Geography and location categorizes structures as centralized or distributed.","Governance drivers categorize structures as hierarchical, community-based, or market-like.","Decision-making authority is a core element of any structure, not a way of categorizing structures."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9c",ty:"std",q:"A service desk introduces an AI tool that drafts new knowledge articles and suggested replies for agents to review, based on patterns it has learned from thousands of past tickets. Which term best describes this tool?",o:["Generative AI","Agentic AI","AI governance","Site reliability engineering"],e:"Generative AI (GenAI) refers to AI systems that can create new content, such as text, images, code, or other outputs, based on patterns learned from existing data. Drafting articles and replies from past tickets is generative AI.",x:["Agentic AI acts on behalf of users by autonomously performing predefined tasks, while this tool only drafts content for agents to review.","AI governance is the system by which an organization directs, controls, and monitors the use of AI, not a type of AI tool.","Site reliability engineering applies software engineering to infrastructure and operations problems."]},
{m:"m10",l:"m10a",ty:"std",q:"A project to implement a new store management system has produced its outputs. According to the course, what role does project management play at this point?",o:["It supports the transition of project outputs into managed, valuable services","It hands the outputs to operations and has no further role in their value","It replaces the ITIL practices that manage the services in operation","It is needed only for small, simple initiatives with low complexity"],e:"Project management is essential for delivering change within the ITIL Value System, and it supports the transition of project outputs into managed, valuable services.",x:["Project management supports the transition of outputs into managed, valuable services rather than ending with a hand-over.","ITIL and project management are complementary, and project management does not replace ITIL practices.","Large or complex initiatives in particular require comprehensive project management approaches such as PRINCE2."]}
]},
{id:"C",t:"Simulation C",qs:[
{m:"m1",l:"m1a",ty:"std",q:"A retailer's mobile app is a single digital product, yet it underpins three different services: mobile payments, loyalty points, and digital receipts. What does this illustrate?",o:["One product may enable many services","Each service needs its own product","Offerings and products are the same","Digital services need no products"],e:"ITIL states that one product may enable many services and one service may be based on several products. Digital services are always based on digital products.",x:["One product may enable many services, and one service may be based on several products.","Products provide capabilities, while service offerings describe potential value, so they are different concepts.","Digital services are always based on digital products."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1b",ty:"std",q:"As part of its offering, an IT provider's staff fulfil users' requests for new accounts and password resets, as agreed with the customer. Which component of the service offering does this represent?",o:["Service actions","Access to resources","Transfer of goods","Service level agreements"],e:"Service actions are performed by the service provider, or by the provider and the consumer, and service request fulfilment is a common form of service action.",x:["With access to resources, the consumer uses resources owned by the provider, such as applications or cloud services.","Transfer of goods means tangible resources pass to the consumer together with ownership.","An SLA is a documented agreement on service levels, not one of the components a service offering may include."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1c",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle are CORRECT?\n1. To deliver high-quality services, organizations must capture and process customers' needs, requirements, and feedback.\n2. User and customer experience rely on the quality of digital products and the service provider's management system.\n3. Acquiring the necessary resources is outside the scope of the lifecycle.\n4. In the ITIL Service Relationship Model, service delivery flows from the consumer to the provider.",o:["1 and 2","1 and 3","2 and 4","3 and 4"],e:"To deliver high-quality services, organizations must capture and process customers' needs, requirements, and feedback, among other things. User and customer experience rely on the quality of digital products and the service provider's management system.",x:["Statement 3 is wrong: organizations must acquire necessary resources and build digital products to deliver high-quality services.","Statement 4 is wrong: service delivery flows from the service provider, while needs, requirements, and feedback flow from the consumer.","Both are wrong: acquiring resources is part of the lifecycle, and service delivery flows from the provider to the consumer."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1d",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA risk is a possible event that could cause harm or loss or make it more difficult to achieve [?].",o:["objectives","outputs","compliance","service levels"],e:"ITIL defines risk as 'a possible event that could cause harm or loss or make it more difficult to achieve objectives'.",x:["Outputs are deliverables of activities, while the definition of risk refers to objectives.","Compliance may be affected by risks, but the definition refers to achieving objectives.","Service levels define expected or achieved service quality, while the definition of risk refers to objectives."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1d",ty:"std",q:"A new cloud service removes a company's need to run its own servers, but it adds a monthly fee and slows down one internal process. According to ITIL, when will the company perceive this service as valuable?",o:["When its positive effects outweigh its negative effects","Only when it introduces no new costs or risks at all","As soon as it meets its technical specifications","When the provider accepts all of the company's risks"],e:"Services can introduce new costs and risks and may negatively affect some outcomes while supporting others. They are perceived as valuable only when they have more positive effects than negative ones.",x:["Services can introduce new costs and risks and still be valuable, provided the positives outweigh the negatives.","Meeting specifications does not by itself make a service valuable. Value is perceived when the positives outweigh the negatives.","Service providers take on some of the consumer's costs and risks, not all of them."]},
{m:"m1",l:"m1a",ty:"std",q:"A bank's account management service relies almost entirely on its mobile app and its core banking software. According to ITIL definitions, how is this service best described?",o:["A digital service","A digital product","A service offering","Goods"],e:"A digital service is a service that fully or largely relies on digital products. The mobile app and the core banking software are the digital products on which this service relies.",x:["A digital product is a combination of resources based on digital technology. Here the app and software are the digital products, not the service.","A service offering is a formal description of one or more services for a target consumer group, not the service itself.","Goods are tangible resources transferred to the consumer together with ownership."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2a",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA service consumer is an organization responsible for [?] and use of services.",o:["procurement","delivery","continual improvement","support"],e:"ITIL defines a service consumer as 'an organization responsible for procurement and use of services'.",x:["Delivery and support of services are the responsibility of the service provider.","Creation and continual improvement of digital products and related offerings are the responsibility of the product vendor.","Support of services is a responsibility of the service provider, together with delivery."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2b",ty:"std",q:"According to ITIL, what makes engagement differ from one service relationship to another?",o:["The level of sharing and dependency between the organizations","The number of users who consume the service each day","The geographical distance between the organizations","The programming languages used to build the products"],e:"Service relationships involve sharing resources, data, and sometimes reputation, and engagement varies by sharing and dependency. This results in three types: basic, cooperative, and collaborative.",x:["The number of users does not define the type of relationship, because engagement varies by sharing and dependency.","Distance is not a factor ITIL uses. Engagement varies by the level of sharing and dependency.","The technologies used do not determine engagement, which varies by sharing and dependency."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2c",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the ITIL Service Journey Model are CORRECT?\n1. The service journey focuses on what happens over time in a service relationship.\n2. Its steps must always be completed in a fixed sequence.\n3. In the 'Agree' step, both parties negotiate and agree target service levels.\n4. The 'Explore' step is performed only by the service provider.",o:["1 and 3","1 and 2","2 and 4","3 and 4"],e:"The service journey focuses on what happens over time, and in the 'Agree' step both the consumer and the provider negotiate and agree target service levels.",x:["Statement 2 is wrong: the steps are not always sequential and can occur in a different order or even simultaneously.","Both are wrong: the steps are not always sequential, and in 'Explore' the consumer also understands its needs and identifies providers.","Statement 4 is wrong: in 'Explore', the consumer understands needs and value and identifies service providers, while the provider targets markets."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2d",ty:"std",q:"A new expense app is fast, secure, and always available, but it cannot process claims in foreign currencies, which many employees need. Which category of service level metrics is lacking?",o:["Utility","Warranty","Sustainability","User experience"],e:"Utility is the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need. It is summarized as 'what the service does' and determines whether a service is 'fit for purpose'.",x:["Warranty ('how the service performs', 'fit for use') covers availability, capacity, security, and continuity, which are met here.","Sustainability concerns the requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth.","User experience is the sum of the functional and emotional interactions as perceived by a user, while the core issue here is a missing function."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2d",ty:"std",q:"To which services do the four categories of service level metrics apply, according to ITIL?",o:["All four categories apply to most services","Only utility and warranty apply to digital services","User experience applies only to internal services","Sustainability applies only when a formal SLA exists"],e:"Service quality is measured through four categories of service level metrics: utility, warranty, user experience, and sustainability. All four apply to most services, although the level of formality varies.",x:["All four categories apply to most services, including digital services.","User experience metrics are especially relevant for user-facing services, and all four categories apply to most services.","The level of formality varies, but sustainability applies to most services whether or not a formal SLA exists."]},
{m:"m2",l:"m2c",ty:"std",q:"After a year of using a service, the consumer and the provider each track, assess, and evaluate the value achieved and review how their service journey went. Which step of the ITIL Service Journey Model is this?",o:["Reflect","Co-create","Agree","Explore"],e:"In the 'reflect' step, both the consumer and the provider track, assess, and evaluate value and evaluate the journey.",x:["In 'co-create', the consumer consumes the service, the provider provides it, and both engage in the service interaction.","In 'agree', both parties negotiate and agree target service levels.","In 'explore', the consumer understands its needs and identifies providers, while the provider targets markets and identifies consumers."]},
{m:"m3",l:"m3a",ty:"std",q:"A retailer notices that many customers want to order online and collect in store, and sees a chance to launch a new click-and-collect service. In the ITIL Value System, what does this represent?",o:["An opportunity, one of the key inputs to the value system","Value, the outcome produced by the value system","A practice, one of the components of the value system","Governance, which directs and controls the organization"],e:"The key inputs to the ITIL VS are opportunity and demand, and its outcome is value. A chance to launch a new service in response to customer interest is an opportunity entering the value system.",x:["Value is the outcome of the ITIL VS, not the input that triggers it.","Practices are organizational capabilities within the value system, not inputs to it.","Governance is a component of the value system, not an input to it."]},
{m:"m4",l:"m4a",ty:"std",q:"Before changing anything, a governing body examines the organization's existing strategies, portfolios, policies, and supplier relationships to understand its current position. Which governance activity is this?",o:["Evaluate","Direct","Monitor","Engage stakeholders"],e:"During 'evaluate', the governing body evaluates the current state of the organization, its strategies, portfolios, policies, relationships, and so on.",x:["During 'direct', policies and strategies are established and modified, which comes after the evaluation.","During 'monitor', the governing body checks performance and compliance once policies and strategies are in place.","Engaging stakeholders is about identifying needs, clarifying commitments, and agreeing on accountability for technology use."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5a",ty:"std",q:"A service desk manager believes that the 'focus on value' guiding principle matters only during improvement projects, not during daily ticket handling. What does ITIL recommend?",o:["Focus on value during normal operational activity as well as improvement initiatives","Focus on value only during improvement initiatives, when value can be measured","Focus on value only after a new service has been transitioned to operation","Focus on value only when customers complain about the quality of a service"],e:"A recommendation for applying 'focus on value' is to focus on value during normal operational activity as well as during improvement initiatives, and to include it in every step of any improvement initiative.",x:["Focus on value applies to normal operational activity as well, not only to improvement initiatives.","Focus on value should be part of all of the organization's work, not only after transition.","Value should be the focus at all times and encouraged among all staff, not only when complaints arise."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5c",ty:"std",q:"A team wants each of its members to know where their work comes from, where their outputs go, and how their actions affect outcomes. What does 'progress iteratively with feedback' recommend building into the work?",o:["Feedback loops","Approval gates","Service level agreements","Fixed project plans"],e:"Feedback must be built into every iteration, including loops that help people know where work comes from, where outputs go, and how their actions affect outcomes.",x:["Approval gates control progress but do not show people where their work comes from and where it goes.","SLAs define the agreed level of each service with a customer, not feedback on how actions affect outcomes.","Fixed plans work against re-evaluating initiatives to reflect changes in circumstances."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5b",ty:"std",q:"A retailer wants a new support process for its self-checkout machines. Parts of the existing in-store support process already work well. What does the 'start where you are' guiding principle recommend?",o:["Identify and build on the successful existing practices","Design a completely new process to avoid old habits","Automate the current process exactly as it is today","Copy the support process of a competitor in full"],e:"'Start where you are' advises not to start over without first considering what is already available to be leveraged. A recommendation for applying it is to identify and build on successful existing practices.",x:["Removing what has been done and building something completely new is rarely necessary and can be extremely wasteful.","The guiding principles advise simplifying and/or optimizing before automating.","The current state should be evaluated and reused where possible, rather than replaced with another organization's process."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5d",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the 'collaborate and promote visibility' guiding principle are CORRECT?\n1. Collaboration means that all stakeholders must reach consensus.\n2. Lack of visibility causes poor decisions, disengagement, and low adoption.\n3. Customers are the least significant stakeholder group.\n4. Communication should happen in a way that the audience can hear.",o:["2 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"Under 'collaborate and promote visibility', lack of visibility causes poor decisions, disengagement, and low adoption, and communication should happen in a way that the audience can hear.",x:["Statement 1 is wrong: collaboration does not mean consensus.","Both are wrong: collaboration does not mean consensus, and customers are the most significant stakeholders.","Statement 3 is wrong: ITIL describes customers as the most significant stakeholders."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5e",ty:"std",q:"A team that builds a digital product insists it does not need to understand how the product is operated and supported, because another company performs those stages. Which guiding principle does this attitude conflict with?",o:["Think and work holistically","Keep it simple and practical","Focus on value","Start where you are"],e:"A holistic approach includes understanding and observing the full product and service lifecycle, even when not all stages of the lifecycle are controlled by the organization or team.",x:["'Keep it simple and practical' is about minimizing steps and removing what adds no value, not about ignoring parts of the lifecycle.","'Focus on value' links activities to value for stakeholders, while the specific issue here is ignoring the full lifecycle.","'Start where you are' is about assessing and reusing what already exists."]},
{m:"m5",l:"m5g",ty:"std",q:"A team automates a monthly reporting task but never checks whether the automation actually delivers value to the people who use the reports. Which recommendation for applying 'optimize and automate' has the team ignored?",o:["Define metrics to match value","Fast does not mean incomplete","Comprehend the whole but do something","Simplicity is the ultimate sophistication"],e:"Recommendations for applying 'optimize and automate' include simplifying and/or optimizing before automating, defining metrics to match value, and ensuring effective governance of digital technology.",x:["'Fast does not mean incomplete' is a recommendation for 'progress iteratively with feedback'.","'Comprehend the whole but do something' is a recommendation for 'progress iteratively with feedback'.","'Simplicity is the ultimate sophistication' is a recommendation for 'keep it simple and practical'."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6a",ty:"std",q:"What does the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model show about how organizations work?",o:["How organizations capture needs and feedback, and design, build, deliver, and support services","How governing bodies direct and control the organization's use of digital technology","How a single team owns a product from its design until it is decommissioned","How suppliers are selected, contracted, and onboarded by procurement teams"],e:"The lifecycle model shows how organizations capture needs and feedback, and design, build, deliver, and support services. User and customer experience is shaped by the digital product and the service provider's system.",x:["Directing and overseeing the use of digital technology is governance, not what the lifecycle model shows.","The lifecycle is not limited to a single team, and organizations may take responsibility for all or some stages.","Selecting and contracting suppliers is only one part of 'acquire', not what the lifecycle as a whole shows."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6b",ty:"std",q:"An organization's purpose is to ensure efficient and reliable delivery of IT services and the coordination of third parties responsible for product development and operation. Its own operating model covers only 'deliver' and 'support'. Who typically performs the 'acquire', 'transition', and 'operate' activities?",o:["External managed service providers","Business customers","Its own product teams","Software vendors and customers"],e:"In value chain pattern 3, the operating model covers 'deliver' and 'support'. 'Discover' is done by business customers, 'design' and 'build' by external vendors, and 'acquire', 'transition', and 'operate' by external managed service providers.",x:["Business customers typically perform 'discover' in this pattern.","The organization's own operating model covers only 'deliver' and 'support'.","Transition performed jointly with customers and/or software vendors belongs to pattern 6, not to this pattern."]},
{m:"m6",l:"m6c",ty:"neg",q:"The course describes several benefits that the ITIL Official Practice Guides bring to organizations. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",o:["A fixed implementation sequence for all 34 practices","A common structure across all 34 practices","Coverage of people, process, technology, and suppliers","Inclusion of metrics, roles, and recommendations"],e:"The Official Practice Guides are consistent (common structure), comprehensive (people, process, technology, and suppliers), practical (metrics, roles, and recommendations), and support continual improvement through the ITIL Maturity Model. A fixed implementation sequence is not one of their benefits.",x:["A common structure across all 34 practices is the 'consistent' benefit.","Coverage of people, process, technology, and suppliers is the 'comprehensive' benefit.","Inclusion of metrics, roles, and recommendations is the 'practical' benefit."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a1",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the 'discover' activity are CORRECT?\n1. It is a one-off activity performed every few years.\n2. Its key output is built and tested product and service solutions.\n3. It is performed at the organizational, portfolio, and product levels.\n4. It is influenced by PESTLE factors and by stakeholder input.",o:["3 and 4","1 and 2","1 and 3","2 and 4"],e:"'Discover' is a continual process performed at the organizational, portfolio, and product levels. It is influenced by PESTLE factors and by input from stakeholders such as customers, shareholders, and governing bodies.",x:["Both are wrong: 'discover' is a continual process, and its key output is an updated product and service roadmap.","Statement 1 is wrong: 'discover' is a continual process, not a one-off, long-interval-based activity.","Statement 2 is wrong: built and tested solutions are the output of 'build', while 'discover' produces an updated roadmap."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a2",ty:"std",q:"A design team involves users throughout design and development, evaluates its designs with users, works iteratively, and includes people from several disciplines. Which approach is the team following?",o:["Human-centred design","Continuous integration","Value stream mapping","Site reliability engineering"],e:"Human-centred design is a method and standard for making design decisions guided by users' lived experiences. Its principles include involving users throughout, user-centred evaluation, an iterative design process, and a multidisciplinary design team.",x:["Continuous integration enables developers to frequently merge code changes, followed by automated builds and tests.","Value stream mapping is a technique to visualize and analyse the flow of work, information, and value in value streams.","Site reliability engineering applies aspects of software engineering to infrastructure and operations problems."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a4",ty:"std",q:"Following an approved solution design, a team develops a new payment module, integrates it with the store systems, configures it, and tests it. Which value chain activity is this?",o:["Build","Design","Transition","Operate"],e:"The purpose of 'build' is to develop, integrate, and test digital products and services, transforming designs into functional solutions. It includes software development, system integration, configuration, and testing.",x:["'Design' creates the specifications and prototypes that 'build' then turns into working solutions.","'Transition' moves built products and services into the live environment.","'Operate' maintains and monitors live products and supporting systems."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a6",ty:"neg",q:"Which is NOT part of how the 'operate' activity works?",o:["Resolving incidents and disasters and restoring services","Running platforms and systems to ensure performance and availability","Performing routine tests, backups, monitoring, and event processing","Ensuring compliance with policies such as security, certificates, and licences"],e:"Resolving incidents and disasters and restoring services is how the 'support' activity works. 'Operate' runs platforms and systems, performs routine tests, backups, monitoring, and event processing, and ensures compliance with policies.",x:["Running platforms and systems to ensure performance and availability is part of 'operate'.","Routine tests, backups, monitoring, and event processing are part of 'operate'.","Ensuring compliance with policies such as continuity, security, certificates, and licences is part of 'operate'."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a7",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA service request is a request from a user or a user's authorized representative that initiates a service [?] which has been agreed as a normal part of service delivery.",o:["action","change","release","interaction"],e:"ITIL defines a service request as 'a request from a user or a user's authorized representative that initiates a service action which has been agreed as a normal part of service delivery'.",x:["A change is the addition, modification, or removal of anything that could affect products and services, not what a service request initiates.","A release is a version of a product or service made available for use, while a service request initiates a service action.","Service interaction takes place during service consumption, while the definition says a service request initiates a service action."]},
{m:"m6x",l:"a8",ty:"std",q:"A flood destroys a company's main data centre. As a result, the company cannot provide critical business functions for longer than its predetermined minimum period. How is this classified?",o:["A disaster","A major incident","A known error","A problem"],e:"A disaster is a sudden unplanned event that causes great damage or serious loss to an organization, resulting in failure to provide critical business functions for some predetermined minimum period of time.",x:["Major incidents have a negative business impact but sit below disasters on the scale, and failing to provide critical business functions for the predetermined period makes this a disaster.","A known error is a problem that has been analysed but has not been resolved.","A problem is a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents, not the event itself."]},
{m:"m7",l:"m7a",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nA value stream is a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a [?].",o:["service consumer","product vendor","governing body","supplier"],e:"ITIL defines a value stream as 'a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer'.",x:["Product vendors create digital products, while value streams deliver products and services to service consumers.","The governing body directs and controls the organization and is not the recipient of a value stream.","Suppliers may contribute to value streams, but the definition refers to delivery to a service consumer."]},
{m:"m7",l:"m7b",ty:"std",q:"While mapping a value stream, a team marks a three-day wait between two approval steps as non-value-adding waste. Which step of value stream mapping is the team performing?",o:["Analyse the stream","Identify the value stream","Map the 'to-be' stream","Plan and implement improvements"],e:"In the 'analyse the stream' step, value-adding activities are separated from non-value-adding activities (waste).",x:["Identifying the value stream is the first step, done before mapping and analysis.","Mapping the 'to-be' stream comes after the analysis and shows the improved flow.","Planning and implementing improvements is the final step, after the 'to-be' stream has been mapped."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"neg",q:"Which is NOT one of the steps of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model?",o:["What resources do we need?","Where are we now?","How do we keep the improvements relevant?","Are we getting there?"],e:"The steps of the model are 'What is the vision?', 'Where are we now?', 'Where do we want to be?', 'How do we get there?', 'Take action', 'Are we getting there?', and 'How do we keep the improvements relevant?'. 'What resources do we need?' is not one of them.",x:["'Where are we now?' is the second step, and it produces a baseline assessment.","'How do we keep the improvements relevant?' is the last step, and it loops back to the vision.","'Are we getting there?' is the sixth step, and it produces a new state assessment."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"list",q:"Which statements about the steps of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model are CORRECT?\n1. 'Where are we now?' includes assessing people's competencies and skills.\n2. 'What is the vision?' sets SMART objectives with CSFs and KPIs.\n3. 'Take action' requires all actions to be performed strictly in sequence.\n4. 'Where do we want to be?' may describe the target through directional progress criteria.",o:["1 and 4","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4"],e:"'Where are we now?' assesses, among other things, people's competencies and skills. 'Where do we want to be?' sets improvement objectives with CSFs and KPIs, using SMART where possible, or describes the target through directional progress criteria.",x:["Statement 2 is wrong: objectives with CSFs and KPIs are set in 'Where do we want to be?'.","Both are wrong: objectives are set in 'Where do we want to be?', and actions may be conducted in sequence, simultaneously, or independently.","Statement 3 is wrong: in 'Take action', actions may be conducted in sequence, simultaneously, or independently."]},
{m:"m8",l:"m8a",ty:"std",q:"A service provider wants to improve its working relationship with a key supplier, which has suffered from poor communication. Can the ITIL Continual Improvement Model be applied?",o:["Yes, because continual improvement applies to the entire value system, including relationships","No, because continual improvement applies only to the products and services an organization offers","No, because the model can be used only for practices and processes","Yes, but only if the supplier adopts the same improvement model"],e:"Continual improvement takes place at all levels of the organization and applies to the entire ITIL Value System, including products, services, practices, and relationships.",x:["Continual improvement applies to practices and relationships as well as to products and services.","The model is a universal model for iterative improvements within the value system, not limited to practices.","The provider can apply the model to its own relationships, and ITIL does not require the supplier to use the same model."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9b",ty:"miss",q:"Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.\nSafety culture is an organizational culture in which people are comfortable being (and [?]) themselves.",o:["expressing","protecting","promoting","evaluating"],e:"ITIL defines safety culture as 'an organizational culture in which people are comfortable being (and expressing) themselves'.",x:["Protecting is not part of the definition, which refers to people being comfortable expressing themselves.","Promoting is not part of the definition, which refers to people expressing themselves.","Evaluating is not part of the definition, which refers to people being and expressing themselves."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9c",ty:"std",q:"A company uses AI in its customer-facing services. It wants a system to direct, control, and monitor this use, so that its AI-enabled products and services create value while remaining ethical, secure, compliant, and aligned with its objectives. What does it need?",o:["AI governance","Agentic AI","Data governance","Continuous deployment"],e:"AI governance is the system by which an organization directs, controls, and monitors the use of artificial intelligence to ensure that AI-enabled digital products and services create value while remaining ethical, secure, compliant, and aligned with organizational objectives.",x:["Agentic AI describes AI systems that act autonomously on behalf of users within agreed boundaries, not a system to control AI use.","Data governance manages data assets through rules, policies, standards, processes, and controls, rather than the use of AI as a whole.","Continuous deployment automatically deploys changes that pass automated tests to production."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9e",ty:"std",q:"A company analyses how its activities are organized across departments and with other organizations, to make sure its workflows enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently. Which dimension is it mainly working on?",o:["Value streams and processes","Organizations and people","Information and technology","Partners and suppliers"],e:"The value streams and processes dimension addresses organizational and cross-organizational workflows, focusing on what activities the organization undertakes and how they are organized to enable value for stakeholders effectively and efficiently.",x:["Organizations and people concerns structure, roles, responsibilities, culture, and systems of authority and communication.","Information and technology concerns the data, information, and technologies used in products and services and in management systems.","Partners and suppliers concerns the organization's dependence on and relationships with other organizations."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9d",ty:"std",q:"An organization works with many suppliers. Instead of coordinating them itself, it appoints one external organization to integrate and manage the services of all the other suppliers. How is the organization managing its dependencies on suppliers?",o:["Through delegated service integration","Through in-house control","Through strategic focus","Through informal partnerships"],e:"Organizations depend on third-party services, and managing these dependencies may involve in-house control or delegated service integration. Appointing an external organization to integrate the other suppliers' services is delegated service integration.",x:["With in-house control, the organization coordinates its suppliers itself, which is not the case here.","Strategic focus is a factor that shapes supplier strategy (core versus non-core functions), not a way of managing supplier dependencies.","Formality ranges from strict contracts to flexible partnerships, but the scenario describes delegating the integration of suppliers."]},
{m:"m9",l:"m9c",ty:"std",q:"A store's point-of-sale system exports a long list of transaction amounts and timestamps, with no context or interpretation added. In ITIL terms, what is this list?",o:["Data","Information","Knowledge","Feedback"],e:"ITIL describes data as raw facts and numbers with no intrinsic meaning. Information is processed data with context and meaning, and knowledge is applied information and experience.",x:["Information is processed data with context and meaning, which has not been added here.","Knowledge is applied information and experience.","Feedback is information about stakeholder reactions and opinions, used as a basis for improvement."]},
{m:"m10",l:"m10a",ty:"std",q:"A retailer is running a programme of projects that will change several of its digital products and services. According to the course, how does PRINCE2 complement ITIL in this situation?",o:["It supports the delivery of changes that implement or modify products and services","It replaces the ITIL practices used for operating and supporting live services","It provides techniques for rapid, continuous software delivery","It defines the governance system for digital technology"],e:"PRINCE2 provides comprehensive methods for project, programme, and portfolio management and supports the delivery of changes that implement or modify products and services. Together, ITIL and PRINCE2 ensure alignment between strategy, change initiatives, and operations.",x:["ITIL and PRINCE2 are complementary, and PRINCE2 does not replace ITIL practices for live services.","Practical techniques for rapid, continuous delivery come from DevOps, not from PRINCE2.","Governance of digital technology is part of the ITIL Value System, not what PRINCE2 provides."]}
]}];

;
// ITIL (Version 5) learning extras. Source: official course material.
window.MNEMONICS=[
{m:"m1",t:"Role of digital technology in products",items:["Purely analogue","Digitally supported","Digitally enabled","Purely digital"],mn:"None -> Side -> Core -> All: digital technology is used for nothing, for non-core functions, for core functions, or it is the whole product.",tip:"If removing digital technology makes the product's potential value disappear, it is digitally enabled, not just digitally supported."},
{m:"m1",t:"What a service offering may include",items:["Transfer of goods","Service actions","Access to resources"],mn:"Give it, Do it, Lend it: goods are given (ownership moves), actions are done, resources are lent (the provider keeps ownership).",tip:"Ownership transfers only with goods, while with access to resources the resources stay owned by the service provider."},
{m:"m2",t:"Service consumer roles",items:["Sponsor","Customer","User"],mn:"Pay, Specify, Use: the Sponsor authorizes the budget, the Customer defines requirements and owns the outcomes, the User uses the service.",tip:"In the mobile phone example the CFO is the sponsor, the CIO and communications team are customers, and all employees (CIO and CFO included) are users."},
{m:"m2",t:"Types of service relationships",items:["Basic","Cooperative","Collaborative (partnership)"],mn:"Buy, Configure, Co-create: off-the-shelf services, configured or customized services, bespoke services, climbing operational -> tactical -> strategic.",tip:"Match the focus: support and efficiency (basic), improvement and effectiveness (cooperative), innovation and growth (collaborative)."},
{m:"m2",t:"Four categories of service level metrics",items:["Utility","Warranty","Sustainability","User experience (UX)"],mn:"Use Well, Stay Engaged: Utility + Warranty + Sustainability + Experience = service value.",tip:"No category is more important than the others, and digital service SLAs should cover utility, warranty, and sustainability, plus UX metrics for user-facing services."},
{m:"m2",t:"The ITIL Service Journey",items:["Explore","Engage","Offer","Agree","Onboard","Co-create","Reflect"],mn:"A love story: explore the scene, engage (get to know each other), offer (propose), agree (say yes), onboard (move in), co-create (build a life together), reflect (anniversary review).",tip:"The steps are not always sequential: they can occur in a different order or even simultaneously."},
{m:"m3",t:"Components of the ITIL Value System",items:["Guiding principles","Governance","Value chain","Management practices","Continual improvement"],mn:"The Figure 1.7 sandwich, top to bottom: G-G on top (Guiding principles, Governance), the Value chain in the middle, P-C at the bottom (Practices, Continual improvement).",tip:"Opportunity and demand are the inputs and value is the outcome, and neither these nor the four dimensions are components."},
{m:"m4",t:"Governance of digital technology activities",items:["Engage stakeholders","Evaluate","Direct","Monitor"],mn:"A fitness plan: talk to your coach (engage stakeholders), weigh yourself (evaluate), set the plan (direct), track progress (monitor).",tip:"Direct is where policies and strategies are established and modified, and monitor checks performance and compliance once they are in place."},
{m:"m4",t:"Governance of digital technology comprises",items:["Directing","Overseeing","Accountability"],mn:"D-O-A: without Directing, Overseeing and Accountability, technology use is Dead On Arrival.",tip:"It is a human-based system by which the current and future use of digital technology is governed."},
{m:"m5",t:"The seven ITIL Guiding Principles",items:["Focus on value","Start where you are","Progress iteratively with feedback","Collaborate and promote visibility","Think and work holistically","Keep it simple and practical","Optimize and automate"],mn:"F-S-P-C-T-K-O: Funky Squirrels Pick Cherries To Keep Overnight.",tip:"There is no fixed order or hierarchy, so use the hook only to recall the names, then match each scenario to the principle it describes."},
{m:"m5",t:"The road to optimization",items:["Understand and agree the context in which the proposed optimization exists.","Assess the current state of the proposed optimization.","Agree what the future state and priorities of the organization should be, focusing on simplification and value.","Ensure the proposed optimization has the appropriate level of stakeholder engagement and commitment.","Execute the improvements in an iterative way.","Continually monitor the impact of optimization."],mn:"Renovating a house: know the neighbourhood (context), inspect the house (current state), agree the dream plan (future state), get the family on board (stakeholders), renovate room by room (iterate), keep checking the result (monitor).",tip:"If an option has you executing before agreeing the future state or securing stakeholder commitment, it is out of order."},
{m:"m6",t:"The eight lifecycle activities (value chain)",items:["Discover","Design","Acquire","Build","Transition","Operate","Deliver","Support"],mn:"D-D-A-B-T-O-D-S: Daring Divers Always Bring Treasure Over Deep Seas.",tip:"The same eight names are the lifecycle stages and the value chain activities, but the activities are not performed in a fixed sequence."},
{m:"m6x",t:"Levels at which 'discover' is performed",items:["Organizational level","Portfolio level","Product level"],mn:"O-P-P, as in OPPortunity: zoom in from the Organization (vision, strategy, target consumers) to the Portfolio (optimize, align) to the Product (roadmaps).",tip:"Discover is a continual process at all three levels, not a one-off, long-interval-based activity."},
{m:"m6x",t:"PESTLE factors",items:["Political","Economic","Social","Technological","Legal","Environmental"],mn:"A pestle grinds six external factors in the mortar: Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal, Environmental.",tip:"PESTLE factors influence 'discover', affect all four dimensions, and monitoring them is part of thinking and working holistically."},
{m:"m7",t:"Steps of value stream mapping",items:["Identify the value stream","Map the 'as-is' stream","Analyse the stream","Map the 'to-be' stream","Plan and implement improvements"],mn:"I'M A MaP: Identify, Map as-is, Analyse, Map to-be, Plan and implement improvements.",tip:"Analysing comes between the two maps and separates value-adding activities from non-value-adding activities (waste)."},
{m:"m8",t:"ITIL Continual Improvement Model",items:["What is the vision?","Where are we now?","Where do we want to be?","How do we get there?","Take action","Are we getting there?","How do we keep the improvements relevant?"],mn:"A road trip: why go (vision), where am I (baseline), where to (target), which route (route), drive (take action), are we there yet (new state assessment), keep the car serviced (keep it relevant).",tip:"Learn each step's output too: improvement vision, baseline assessment, next target state, route to the next target state, improvement actions and experiments, new state assessment."},
{m:"m9",t:"The ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management",items:["Organizations and people","Information and technology","Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes"],mn:"O-I-P-V: Owls In Purple Vests.",tip:"Learn the exact four pairs, because distractors use look-alike pairs such as 'products and services' (the centre of Figure 1.4), which is not a dimension."},
{m:"m9",t:"The ITIL AI Capability Model (6C)",items:["Creation","Curation","Clarification","Cognition","Communication","Coordination"],mn:"An AI chef: creates a dish, curates the menu, clarifies the recipe, thinks it through (cognition), communicates with guests, coordinates the kitchen.",tip:"All six capabilities start with C and surround 'AI applications', so an option like 'Control' or 'Compliance' is not one of them."},
{m:"m9",t:"Four types of complexity context",items:["Ordered","Complex","Chaotic","Confused"],mn:"Order, then three C's: Complex = hindsight (understandable in retrospect), Chaotic = fire (crisis, act now), Confused = fog (orient before acting).",tip:"The ITIL Continual Improvement Model is optimized for improvements in ordered and complex contexts."},
{m:"m9",t:"Six key principles of data governance",items:["Strategic alignment","Accountability and ownership","Policies & procedures","Transparency & auditability","Integrity","Adaptability"],mn:"S-A-P-T-I-A: Smart Auditors Prefer Total Integrity Always.",tip:"Do not mix these up with the 6C AI capabilities: data governance keeps data secure, usable, and reliable throughout its lifecycle."}];
window.TRAPS=[
{m:"m1",t:"Output vs outcome",a:"Output",b:"Outcome",tip:"Service providers produce outputs that help their consumers achieve outcomes.",items:[["A tangible or intangible deliverable of an activity","a"],["A result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outputs","b"],["What service providers produce for their consumers","a"],["Can differ for each stakeholder, such as customers and cities","b"],["ITIL Car Rental: the customer arriving at their destination","a"],["ITIL Car Rental: fewer cars on the road and cleaner air","b"]]},
{m:"m1",t:"Transfer of goods vs access to resources",a:"Transfer of goods",b:"Access to resources",tip:"Goods change owner, while with access to resources the provider keeps ownership and the consumer uses them.",items:[["Ownership and associated rights and responsibilities move to the consumer","a"],["The resources remain owned by the service provider","b"],["Rare in digital services","a"],["The consumer gains access to the provider's resources and uses them","b"],["Providers aim to minimize the need for it","a"],["Examples: hotel rooms and vehicles","b"],["Example: equipment or consumables","a"],["Examples: applications, platforms, networks, cloud services","b"]]},
{m:"m1",t:"Costs and risks: removed vs imposed",a:"Removed or reduced by the service",b:"Imposed by the service",tip:"Reduced or removed costs and risks are what the offering promises, while imposed ones are the costs and risks of service consumption.",items:[["Costs reduced or removed as intended in a service offering","a"],["The costs of service consumption","b"],["Risks that form the value proposition","a"],["The risks of service consumption","b"],["ITIL Car Rental: the extra costs of owning a car","a"],["ITIL Car Rental: software errors or connectivity issues","b"],["ITIL Car Rental: road-safety risks customers would manage when driving","a"],["ITIL Car Rental: cyber-security incidents affecting the journey","b"]]},
{m:"m2",t:"Utility vs warranty",a:"Utility",b:"Warranty",tip:"Utility is what the service does (fit for purpose), warranty is how the service performs (fit for use).",items:[["What the service does","a"],["How the service performs","b"],["Fit for purpose","a"],["Fit for use","b"],["The functionality offered to meet a particular need","a"],["Assurance that the product or service will meet the agreed requirements","b"],["Supports the consumer's performance or removes constraints","a"],["Covers availability, capacity, security levels, and continuity","b"]]},
{m:"m2",t:"Sponsor vs customer",a:"Sponsor",b:"Customer",tip:"The sponsor authorizes the budget, while the customer defines requirements and takes responsibility for outcomes.",items:[["Authorizes budget for service consumption","a"],["Defines the requirements for products and services","b"],["Mobile phone example: the CFO","a"],["Mobile phone example: the CIO and communications team","b"],["Reviews the arrangement","a"],["Takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption","b"],["Approves costs","a"],["Analyses requirements, negotiates the contract, monitors performance","b"]]},
{m:"m2",t:"Cooperative vs collaborative relationship",a:"Cooperative",b:"Collaborative (partnership)",tip:"Cooperative means improvement and effectiveness on configured services, collaborative means innovation and growth on bespoke services up to strategic level.",items:[["Typical focus: improvement and effectiveness","a"],["Typical focus: innovation and growth","b"],["Operational and tactical levels involved","a"],["Operational, tactical, and strategic levels involved","b"],["Services that must be configured or customized for the consumer","a"],["Bespoke contracts, or no formal agreement","b"],["ITIL Car Rental: the cleaning provider","a"],["ITIL Car Rental: the driverless vehicle manufacturer","b"]]},
{m:"m2",t:"Service provider vs product vendor",a:"Service provider",b:"Product vendor",tip:"The vendor creates and continually improves digital products, while the provider delivers and supports the services.",items:[["Responsible for delivery and support of services","a"],["Responsible for creation and continual improvement of digital products","b"],["Offers and negotiates service offerings","a"],["Acquires and manages technology resources","b"],["Its service delivery flows to consumers in the Service Relationship Model","a"],["Builds and manages digital products","b"]]},
{m:"m3",t:"ITIL Value System vs value chain",a:"ITIL Value System",b:"Value chain",tip:"The value chain is the central component inside the value system, which also holds the guiding principles, governance, practices, and continual improvement.",items:[["Shows how all components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation","a"],["An entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service","b"],["Its key inputs are opportunity and demand","a"],["Discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support","b"],["Its outcome is value","a"],["Its activities are enabled and supported by management practices","b"],["Purpose: continually and sustainably co-create value with all stakeholders","a"],["The central component of the value system","b"]]},
{m:"m4",t:"Evaluate vs monitor (governance)",a:"Evaluate",b:"Monitor",tip:"Evaluate looks at the current state of the organization, while monitor tracks performance and compliance once policies and strategies are in place.",items:[["Looks at the current state of the organization","a"],["Tracks performance","b"],["Reviews the organization's strategies and portfolios","a"],["Tracks compliance","b"],["Reviews the organization's policies and relationships","a"],["Happens once policies and strategies are in place","b"]]},
{m:"m5",t:"Focus on value vs keep it simple and practical",a:"Focus on value",b:"Keep it simple and practical",tip:"Focus on value links every activity to value, while keep it simple eliminates whatever fails to add value or a useful outcome.",items:[["All activities should link back, directly or indirectly, to value","a"],["Eliminate anything that fails to increase value or produce a useful outcome","b"],["First know who is being served","a"],["Always aim to minimize the number of steps to accomplish an objective","b"],["Value for the consumer is subjective and changes over time","a"],["Design rules to handle exceptions instead of solving every exception","b"],["Know how service consumers use each service","a"],["Do fewer things but do them better","b"]]},
{m:"m5",t:"Start where you are vs progress iteratively with feedback",a:"Start where you are",b:"Progress iteratively with feedback",tip:"Start where you are observes and reuses the current state, while progressing iteratively runs manageable iterations with feedback loops.",items:[["Do not start over without first considering what is already available","a"],["Each iteration should be both manageable and managed","b"],["Have someone with little or no prior knowledge observe","a"],["Seek and use feedback before, throughout, and after each iteration","b"],["Use measurement to support, not replace, what is observed","a"],["Comprehend the whole but do something","b"],["Recognize that sometimes nothing from the current state can be reused","a"],["Fast does not mean incomplete","b"]]},
{m:"m5",t:"Collaborate and promote visibility vs think and work holistically",a:"Collaborate and promote visibility",b:"Think and work holistically",tip:"Collaborate is about the right people and visible work, while holistic is about the whole system: dimensions, lifecycle, supply chain, and PESTLE.",items:[["Customers are the most significant stakeholders","a"],["No product, service, practice, process, team, or supplier stands alone","b"],["Collaboration does not mean consensus","a"],["Consider all four dimensions of product and service management","b"],["Use dashboards, Kanban boards, and other visual tools","a"],["Continually monitor and analyse the PESTLE factors","b"],["Lack of visibility causes poor decisions, disengagement, and low adoption","a"],["Look for patterns in the interactions between system elements","b"]]},
{m:"m5",t:"Keep it simple and practical vs optimize and automate",a:"Keep it simple and practical",b:"Optimize and automate",tip:"Keep it simple cuts steps, exceptions, and low-value features, while optimize and automate scales work with technology under governance, after simplifying.",items:[["Providing a solution for every exception leads to over-complication","a"],["Technology lets organizations scale up and handle more tasks","b"],["Product solutions should generate only relevant data","a"],["Generative AI use should be subject to governance, ethical, and compliance controls","b"],["Simplicity is the ultimate sophistication","a"],["Automation for automation's sake can increase costs and risks","b"],["Product teams should dismiss features that do not maximize value","a"],["Simplify and/or optimize before automating","b"]]},
{m:"m6",t:"General vs product and service management practice",a:"General management practice",b:"Product and service management practice",tip:"ITIL has 12 general and 22 product and service management practices, and information security and service financial management sit in the product and service group.",items:[["Relationship management","a"],["Information security management","b"],["Supplier management","a"],["Service financial management","b"],["Workforce and talent management","a"],["Business analysis","b"],["Measurement and reporting","a"],["Change enablement","b"]]},
{m:"m6",t:"Value chain vs value stream",a:"Value chain",b:"Value stream",tip:"The value chain is the set of lifecycle activities, while a value stream is the real series of steps that combines them to deliver to a consumer.",items:[["The eight lifecycle management activities seen from the organizational perspective","a"],["A series of steps an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a consumer","b"],["Included in the operating model","a"],["Real workflows, not blueprints","b"],["Its activities form core value streams","a"],["Unique, context-driven de-facto workflows","b"]]},
{m:"m6x",t:"Deliver vs support",a:"Deliver",b:"Support",tip:"Deliver provides services, fulfils requests, and onboards users in line with SLAs, while support resolves incidents and disasters and restores normal operation.",items:[["Fulfils users' service requests","a"],["Identifies and resolves incidents","b"],["Manages user onboarding/offboarding","a"],["Fulfils disaster recovery procedures","b"],["Transfers goods and provides access to product resources","a"],["Investigates incidents and identifies errors in products","b"],["Key output: delivered services","a"],["Key output: restored normal product and service operation","b"]]},
{m:"m6x",t:"Operate vs support",a:"Operate",b:"Support",tip:"Operate keeps products and systems running and monitored, while support steps in when incidents and disasters occur and restores normal operation.",items:[["Maintains and monitors products, services, and supporting systems","a"],["Includes recovery and restoration of services","b"],["Performs routine tests, backups, monitoring, and event processing","a"],["Metric: speed of restoration of normal product operation and service delivery","b"],["Mostly invisible to customers unless issues arise","a"],["Triggered by operations incidents, delivery incidents, and disasters","b"],["Metric: coverage and effectiveness of monitoring and analysis","a"],["Can be automated and proactive, but also reactive when needed","b"]]},
{m:"m6x",t:"Transition vs deliver",a:"Transition",b:"Deliver",tip:"Supplier onboarding/offboarding belongs to transition, while user onboarding/offboarding belongs to deliver.",items:[["Onboarding/offboarding of suppliers","a"],["Onboarding/offboarding of users","b"],["Introduces new or updated products and services into operational environments","a"],["Directly linked to SLAs and quality criteria","b"],["Includes decommissioning resources/products","a"],["Performs service actions with or for users","b"],["May require planned interruptions, especially for legacy products","a"],["Gathers consumer feedback","b"]]},
{m:"m6x",t:"Continuous delivery vs continuous deployment",a:"Continuous delivery",b:"Continuous deployment",tip:"Continuous delivery makes deployment possible at any time but decided case by case, while continuous deployment sends every change that passes automated tests to production automatically.",items:[["Software updates can be deployed to production at any time","a"],["Every change that passes automated tests is deployed automatically","b"],["Frequent deployments are possible, but each is decided case by case","a"],["No additional authorization is needed to reach production","b"],["Usually chosen when organizations prefer a slower rate of deployment","a"],["Relies on continuous delivery","b"]]},
{m:"m7",t:"Value stream mapping vs value stream management",a:"Value stream mapping",b:"Value stream management",tip:"Mapping is a technique to visualize and analyse a stream, while management is the ongoing analysis and improvement of the organization's value streams.",items:[["A technique for visualizing and analysing value streams","a"],["An ongoing focus on how work is done","b"],["Shows the flow of information, work, and value","a"],["Covers both 'management of' and 'management through' streams","b"],["Steps: identify, map as-is, analyse, map to-be, improve","a"],["Supported by automation and measurement","b"],["Separates value-adding activities from waste","a"],["Improves the practices that enable streams","b"]]},
{m:"m8",t:"Where are we now? vs Where do we want to be?",a:"Where are we now?",b:"Where do we want to be?",tip:"'Where are we now?' assesses the current state (baseline assessment), while 'Where do we want to be?' sets the target with CSFs, KPIs, and SMART objectives (next target state).",items:[["Assess existing products and services, including users' perception of value","a"],["Set clear improvement objectives with CSFs and KPIs","b"],["Assess people's competencies and skills","a"],["Use SMART objectives where possible","b"],["Assess feedback, stories, and culture indicators","a"],["Describe the target in measurable terms or directional progress criteria","b"],["Output: baseline assessment","a"],["Output: next target state","b"]]},
{m:"m8",t:"Are we getting there? vs How do we keep the improvements relevant?",a:"Are we getting there?",b:"How do we keep the improvements relevant?",tip:"'Are we getting there?' checks progress (new state assessment), while keeping improvements relevant embeds the change or shares lessons learned when targets are missed.",items:[["Assess progress against objectives and value","a"],["Use change and knowledge management to embed improvements","b"],["If results fall short, plan additional actions or a new iteration","a"],["Leaders and managers help teams adopt new ways of working","b"],["Without SMART objectives, check whether the new state is better and closer to the vision","a"],["Inform stakeholders of the reasons for failure","b"],["Output: new state assessment","a"],["Treat deviations as learning opportunities, not blame","b"]]},
{m:"m9",t:"Organizations and people vs information and technology",a:"Organizations and people",b:"Information and technology",tip:"Culture, leadership, structure, and skills belong to organizations and people, while data, knowledge, tools, and AI governance belong to information and technology.",items:[["Culture and safety culture","a"],["Data governance and its six principles","b"],["Leadership that balances the global good with individual self-interest","a"],["Knowledge bases, analytical tools, and communication systems","b"],["Conway's law and team structures","a"],["AI governance and the 6C model","b"],["Skills and competencies such as emotional intelligence","a"],["Data, information, and knowledge (DIKW)","b"]]},
{m:"m9",t:"Generative AI vs agentic AI",a:"Generative AI (GenAI)",b:"Agentic AI",tip:"GenAI creates content from learned patterns, while agentic AI acts for users by performing predefined tasks within agreed boundaries under human oversight.",items:[["Creates new content such as text, images, or code","a"],["Designed to act on behalf of users","b"],["Based on patterns learned from existing data","a"],["Autonomously performs predefined tasks within agreed boundaries","b"],["Its growth pushes organizations to address governance, ethics, and compliance challenges","a"],["Remains subject to human oversight and governance","b"]]},
{m:"m10",t:"ITIL + DevOps vs ITIL + PRINCE2",a:"ITIL + DevOps",b:"ITIL + PRINCE2",tip:"DevOps adds rapid, continuous delivery practices, while PRINCE2 adds project, programme, and portfolio methods that align strategy, change, and operations.",items:[["Together enable resilient, adaptive, and customer-focused outcomes","a"],["Together ensure alignment between strategy, change initiatives, and operations","b"],["Together: end-to-end management of the digital product and service lifecycle","a"],["Together: effective management of the digital product and service lifecycle","b"],["Adds practical techniques for rapid, continuous delivery","a"],["Adds comprehensive methods for project, programme, and portfolio management","b"],["Adds agile, continuous development and operational practices","a"],["Needed for large or complex initiatives","b"]]}];
window.TF=[
{m:"m1",s:"A service is a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks.",a:true,e:"This is the official ITIL definition of a service."},
{m:"m1",s:"A product is a configuration of an organization's resources designed to offer value for a consumer.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of a product."},
{m:"m1",s:"A digital service is a combination of an organization's resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers.",a:false,e:"That is the definition of a digital product, while a digital service is a service that fully or largely relies on digital products."},
{m:"m1",s:"Goods are intangible resources made available to a service consumer while the provider keeps ownership.",a:false,e:"Goods are tangible resources transferred or available for transfer to the consumer, together with ownership and associated rights and responsibilities."},
{m:"m1",s:"'Digitally enabled' products depend on digital technology to fulfil their core functions.",a:true,e:"Digitally enabled products have analogue and physical components but depend on digital technology for their core functions, so without it their potential value disappears."},
{m:"m1",s:"'Digitally supported' products use digital technology to perform their core functions.",a:false,e:"Digitally supported products perform their core functions without digital technology and use it only for non-core functions."},
{m:"m1",s:"One digital product may enable many services, and one service may be based on several products.",a:true,e:"The course lists this among the relationships between digital products and digital services."},
{m:"m1",s:"Service offerings provide capabilities, while products describe potential value.",a:false,e:"It is the other way round: products provide capabilities, and service offerings describe potential value."},
{m:"m1",s:"A service offering is a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of a service offering, which may include goods, access to resources, and service actions."},
{m:"m1",s:"Transfer of goods is the most common component of digital service offerings.",a:false,e:"Transfer of goods is rare in digital services, and service providers aim to minimize the need to transfer goods."},
{m:"m1",s:"Service request fulfilment is a common form of service action.",a:true,e:"The course names service request fulfilment as a common form of service action, performed by the provider or by the provider and the consumer."},
{m:"m1",s:"With access to resources, ownership of the resources passes to the service consumer.",a:false,e:"With access to resources, the consumer uses the provider's resources, which remain owned by the service provider."},
{m:"m1",s:"An output is a result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outcomes.",a:false,e:"The terms are swapped: an outcome is a result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outputs, and an output is a deliverable of an activity."},
{m:"m1",s:"An output is a tangible or intangible deliverable of an activity.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of an output, and service providers produce outputs that help consumers achieve outcomes."},
{m:"m1",s:"Risk is a possible event that could cause harm or loss or make it more difficult to achieve objectives.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of risk."},
{m:"m1",s:"A service is perceived as valuable whenever it delivers at least one positive outcome.",a:false,e:"Services are perceived as valuable only when they have more positive effects than negative ones."},
{m:"m1",s:"Value is the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of value, and value is also the outcome of the ITIL Value System."},
{m:"m1",s:"Value co-creation is the process by which the service provider alone creates value and hands it over to consumers.",a:false,e:"Value co-creation is collaborative: providers, consumers, and other stakeholders jointly create value by combining resources, capabilities, and actions."},
{m:"m1",s:"The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model includes six lifecycle stages.",a:false,e:"The model includes eight lifecycle stages, matched by eight lifecycle management activities of the same name."},
{m:"m1",s:"Digital product and service management is a set of specialized organizational capabilities for enabling value for customers in the form of digital products and services.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of digital product and service management."},
{m:"m2",s:"Utility is summarized as 'how the service performs' and determines whether a service is fit for use.",a:false,e:"That describes warranty, while utility is 'what the service does' and determines whether a service is fit for purpose."},
{m:"m2",s:"To have utility, a service must either support the performance of the consumer or remove constraints from the consumer.",a:true,e:"This is how the course explains utility, and many services do both."},
{m:"m2",s:"Warranty typically addresses the availability of the service, its capacity, levels of security, and continuity.",a:true,e:"These are the typical areas of warranty, the assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements."},
{m:"m2",s:"Warranty can only be based on a formal agreement such as an SLA.",a:false,e:"Warranty may be based on a formal agreement, or it may be a marketing message or brand image."},
{m:"m2",s:"Sustainability is the assurance that a product or service meets and will continue to meet the requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of sustainability as a category of service level metrics."},
{m:"m2",s:"Of the four categories of service level metrics, warranty is the most important.",a:false,e:"No individual category is more important than the others, because utility, warranty, sustainability, and UX all contribute to value and outcomes."},
{m:"m2",s:"Digital service SLAs should cover utility, warranty, and sustainability, with user experience metrics added for user-facing services.",a:true,e:"These are the key SLA considerations given in the course."},
{m:"m2",s:"A service consumer is an organization responsible for the delivery and support of services.",a:false,e:"That describes a service provider, while a service consumer is responsible for the procurement and use of services."},
{m:"m2",s:"A product vendor is responsible for the creation and continual improvement of digital products and related service offerings.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of a product vendor, one of the three roles organizations can play in service relationships."},
{m:"m2",s:"The customer is the role that authorizes budget for service consumption.",a:false,e:"That is the sponsor, while the customer defines the requirements and takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption."},
{m:"m2",s:"In the mobile phone example, the CFO and the CIO are also users of the service.",a:true,e:"Users are all employees, including the CIO and the CFO, so one person can hold several consumer roles."},
{m:"m2",s:"A basic service relationship typically focuses on innovation and growth.",a:false,e:"Basic relationships focus on support and efficiency, while innovation and growth is the focus of collaborative relationships (partnerships)."},
{m:"m2",s:"Cooperative relationships typically involve the operational and tactical levels of the organization.",a:true,e:"Basic relationships are operational, cooperative ones operational and tactical, and collaborative ones add the strategic level."},
{m:"m2",s:"Collaborative relationships may operate with no formal agreement.",a:true,e:"Typical agreements for collaborative relationships are bespoke contracts, outcome-based agreements, or no formal agreement."},
{m:"m2",s:"Commercial off-the-shelf services are typical of collaborative relationships.",a:false,e:"Commercial off-the-shelf and highly standardized services are typical of basic relationships, while collaborative ones use custom or bespoke services."},
{m:"m2",s:"The service journey focuses on the visible aspects of a service relationship.",a:false,e:"The band of visibility focuses on the visible aspects, while the service journey focuses on what happens over time."},
{m:"m2",s:"The steps of the ITIL Service Journey always follow a fixed sequence.",a:false,e:"The steps are not always sequential and can occur in a different order or even simultaneously."},
{m:"m2",s:"In the ITIL Service Journey Model, 'Onboard' comes directly after 'Agree'.",a:true,e:"The steps are Explore, Engage, Offer, Agree, Onboard, Co-create, and Reflect."},
{m:"m2",s:"A service level is a set of metrics that define expected or achieved service quality.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of service level, and service quality is measured through service levels."},
{m:"m2",s:"An SLA is a documented agreement between a service provider and a user.",a:false,e:"An SLA is a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service."},
{m:"m3",s:"The key inputs to the ITIL Value System are opportunity and demand.",a:true,e:"Opportunity and demand are the inputs, and value is the outcome of the ITIL VS."},
{m:"m3",s:"The outcome of the ITIL Value System is a service offering.",a:false,e:"The outcome of the ITIL VS is value: the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something."},
{m:"m3",s:"The components of the ITIL Value System are guiding principles, governance, value chain, management practices, and continual improvement.",a:true,e:"These five components work together to facilitate value creation through digital products and services."},
{m:"m3",s:"The four dimensions of product and service management are one of the components of the ITIL Value System.",a:false,e:"The components are guiding principles, governance, value chain, management practices, and continual improvement, and management practices integrate resources from the four dimensions."},
{m:"m3",s:"The purpose of the ITIL VS is to ensure that the organization continually and sustainably co-creates value with all stakeholders through the use and management of products and services.",a:true,e:"This is the stated purpose of the ITIL Value System."},
{m:"m3",s:"Each organization's value system operates in isolation from the value systems of other organizations.",a:false,e:"Value systems interact with those of other organizations, forming an ecosystem that enables value co-creation."},
{m:"m3",s:"A management system is a system of interconnected elements that establish policy and objectives and enable the achievement of those objectives.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of a management system."},
{m:"m3",s:"In a management system, guiding principles are sets of organizational capabilities designed for accomplishing specific objectives.",a:false,e:"That describes management practices, while guiding principles are universal recommendations for decisions and actions."},
{m:"m3",s:"The value chain is the central component of the ITIL Value System.",a:true,e:"The big picture of ITIL states that the central component of the value system is a value chain."},
{m:"m4",s:"Governance is the system by which an organization is directed and controlled.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of governance."},
{m:"m4",s:"Governance of digital technology comprises planning, building, and running.",a:false,e:"The governance of digital technology system comprises directing, overseeing, and accountability."},
{m:"m4",s:"Governance of digital technology is a human-based system by which the current and future use of digital technology is governed.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of governance of digital technology, which comprises directing, overseeing, and accountability."},
{m:"m4",s:"During the 'direct' activity, the governing body monitors performance and compliance.",a:false,e:"During direction, policies and strategies are established and modified, and performance and compliance are checked in 'monitor'."},
{m:"m4",s:"The governing body engages stakeholders to identify needs, clarify commitments, and agree on accountability for technology use.",a:true,e:"This describes the 'engage stakeholders' activity of digital technology governance."},
{m:"m4",s:"In the 'evaluate' activity, the governing body establishes new policies and strategies.",a:false,e:"Evaluate assesses the current state of the organization, its strategies, portfolios, policies, and relationships, and policies are established in 'direct'."},
{m:"m4",s:"The governing body monitors performance and compliance once policies and strategies are in place.",a:true,e:"Monitoring happens with policies and strategies in place and covers both performance and compliance."},
{m:"m4",s:"Governance of digital technology is defined as an automated, technology-based control system.",a:false,e:"It is defined as a human-based system by which the current and future use of digital technology is governed."},
{m:"m5",s:"'Focus on value' says all activities should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization, its customers, and other stakeholders.",a:true,e:"This is the core statement of the 'focus on value' principle."},
{m:"m5",s:"When focusing on value, the first step is to define service level metrics.",a:false,e:"The first step is to know who is being served, meaning who the service consumer and key stakeholders are."},
{m:"m5",s:"Value for the service consumer is objective and stays the same over time.",a:false,e:"Value for the consumer is subjective, influenced by the service experience, and changes over time and in different circumstances."},
{m:"m5",s:"'Start where you are' advises removing existing services and building something completely new whenever possible.",a:false,e:"Building something completely new is rarely necessary or wise and can be extremely wasteful, so first consider what is already available to be leveraged."},
{m:"m5",s:"Under 'start where you are', a person with little or no prior knowledge can be a useful observer.",a:true,e:"Such a person has no preconceptions and may notice things that those closely involved might overlook."},
{m:"m5",s:"Measurement should replace direct observation when assessing the current state.",a:false,e:"Measurement should support, not replace, what is observed, because over-reliance on data analytics and reporting can introduce bias and risk."},
{m:"m5",s:"Applying 'start where you are' includes recognizing that sometimes nothing from the current state can be reused.",a:true,e:"This tip sits alongside evaluating the current state, building on successful existing practices, and applying risk management skills."},
{m:"m5",s:"Each iteration should be both manageable and managed, returning tangible results in a timely manner.",a:true,e:"This is how 'progress iteratively with feedback' describes iterations, whose results are built upon to create further improvement."},
{m:"m5",s:"Feedback should be sought only after each iteration is complete.",a:false,e:"Feedback should be sought and used before, throughout, and after each iteration."},
{m:"m5",s:"A feedback loop is a situation where part of the output of an activity is used for new input.",a:true,e:"This is the course definition of a feedback loop."},
{m:"m5",s:"Complaints and compliments are an example of solicited, structured feedback.",a:false,e:"In Figure 5.1, complaints and compliments are unsolicited, unstructured feedback, while surveys are solicited and structured."},
{m:"m5",s:"Working in a timeboxed, iterative manner with embedded feedback loops helps detect and respond to failures earlier.",a:true,e:"The other benefits are greater flexibility, faster responses to customer and business needs, and an overall improvement in quality."},
{m:"m5",s:"'Collaborate and promote visibility' means every decision requires consensus.",a:false,e:"The course states explicitly that collaboration does not mean consensus."},
{m:"m5",s:"When deciding whom to collaborate with, customers are the most significant stakeholders.",a:true,e:"Customers come first, followed by suppliers, relationship managers, internal and external teams, and product teams."},
{m:"m5",s:"'Think and work holistically' includes continually monitoring and analysing the PESTLE factors affecting the organization.",a:true,e:"The holistic approach also covers the four dimensions, the full lifecycle, the supply chain position, and the impact on service consumers."},
{m:"m5",s:"Thinking holistically means observing only the lifecycle stages that the organization controls.",a:false,e:"It means understanding and observing the full product and service lifecycle, even when not all stages are controlled by the organization or team."},
{m:"m5",s:"'Keep it simple and practical' recommends designing a solution for every possible exception.",a:false,e:"Providing a solution for every exception leads to over-complication, so design rules to handle exceptions instead."},
{m:"m5",s:"Under 'keep it simple and practical', a metric that fails to increase value or produce a useful outcome should be eliminated.",a:true,e:"Any process, product, service, action, or metric that fails to increase value or produce a useful outcome should be eliminated."},
{m:"m5",s:"'Optimize and automate' advises automating first and simplifying later.",a:false,e:"The principle advises to simplify and/or optimize before automating."},
{m:"m5",s:"Automation for automation's sake can increase costs, introduce significant risks, and undermine organizational resilience.",a:true,e:"That is why technology use, especially generative AI, should be subject to governance, ethical, and compliance policies and controls."},
{m:"m5",s:"The first step on the road to optimization is to execute the improvements in an iterative way.",a:false,e:"The first step is to understand and agree the context of the proposed optimization, and iterative execution is the fifth of six steps."},
{m:"m5",s:"The ITIL Guiding Principles have a fixed order of priority, with 'focus on value' at the top.",a:false,e:"There is no fixed order or hierarchy among the principles, and all are equally important for the ITIL VS."},
{m:"m5",s:"In practice, some guiding principles may be more critical than others depending on the situation.",a:true,e:"Organizations should consider all the principles, but their relevance varies with the situation."},
{m:"m5",s:"Organizations should rely on the one or two guiding principles that best fit their culture.",a:false,e:"Organizations should not rely on just one or two principles but consider the relevance of all of them and how they complement each other."},
{m:"m6",s:"ITIL includes 34 management practices organized in two groups.",a:true,e:"There are 12 general management practices and 22 product and service management practices."},
{m:"m6",s:"ITIL includes 12 product and service management practices and 22 general management practices.",a:false,e:"It is the reverse: 12 general management practices and 22 product and service management practices."},
{m:"m6",s:"Information security management is a general management practice.",a:false,e:"In ITIL (Version 5), information security management is one of the 22 product and service management practices."},
{m:"m6",s:"Service financial management is a product and service management practice.",a:true,e:"It is one of the 22 product and service management practices, alongside practices such as service desk and change enablement."},
{m:"m6",s:"Portfolio management is a general management practice.",a:true,e:"Portfolio management is one of the 12 general management practices, together with practices such as risk, strategy, and supplier management."},
{m:"m6",s:"A management practice is a set of organizational resources and capabilities designed and adopted for performing work or accomplishing an objective.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of a management practice."},
{m:"m6",s:"The lifecycle management activities are always performed in a fixed sequence from discover to support.",a:false,e:"They enable and support the lifecycle but are not performed in a fixed sequence, and from the organizational perspective they form a value chain."},
{m:"m6",s:"An operating model is a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, and how it runs itself.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of an operating model."},
{m:"m6",s:"An organization's purpose describes how the organization runs itself.",a:false,e:"The purpose describes what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders and why, while how it runs itself is part of the operating model."},
{m:"m6",s:"An organization's purpose defines its operating model, which includes value chain activities that form core value streams.",a:true,e:"Core value streams in turn depend on management practices, which enable the value chain activities and core value streams."},
{m:"m6",s:"Each value chain activity is supported and enabled by a single dedicated management practice.",a:false,e:"Each value chain activity is supported and enabled by several management practices, each contributing to a specific aspect of digital products and services."},
{m:"m6",s:"In value chain pattern 3 (IT services with coordination of third parties), the organization's operating model covers only 'deliver' and 'support'.",a:true,e:"Discover is done by business customers, design and build by external vendors, and acquire, transition, and operate by external managed service providers."},
{m:"m6",s:"A custom software development company typically performs all eight lifecycle activities itself.",a:false,e:"In value chain pattern 5 (custom software development), the operating model covers design, acquire, build, and transition (partially), with customers or third parties doing the rest."},
{m:"m6",s:"Every ITIL Official Practice Guide follows the same structure.",a:true,e:"All 34 guides share one structure, from General Information to Recommendations for the practice success."},
{m:"m6",s:"The 'General Information' chapter of a practice guide covers roles and competencies.",a:false,e:"General Information covers purpose, scope, key terms, success factors, and key metrics, while roles and competencies are in 'Organizations and people'."},
{m:"m6",s:"Management practices integrate resources from the four dimensions to form the management capabilities needed for effective product and service management.",a:true,e:"This is point 6 of the big picture of the key ITIL models."},
{m:"m6x",s:"The purpose of 'discover' is to ensure continual alignment of product roadmaps and related service offerings with consumer needs and organizational strategy.",a:true,e:"This is the purpose of 'discover', whose key output is an updated product and service roadmap."},
{m:"m6x",s:"'Discover' is a one-off activity performed at long intervals.",a:false,e:"Discover is a continual process, not a one-off, long-interval-based activity."},
{m:"m6x",s:"The purpose of 'design' is to develop, integrate, and test digital products and services.",a:false,e:"That is the purpose of 'build', while design creates specifications and prototypes detailing functionality, user experience, and operational framework."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Product and service design always includes service interactions and experience.",a:true,e:"The course stresses this whether the organization takes a proactive or a reactive design approach."},
{m:"m6x",s:"A reactive design approach anticipates needs before they are expressed.",a:false,e:"A proactive approach anticipates needs, while a reactive approach responds to requirements."},
{m:"m6x",s:"A product or service prototype is a detailed document outlining the requirements and characteristics of a product to be built.",a:false,e:"That describes a specification, while a prototype is an initial version that demonstrates basic form, functionality, and operational capabilities."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Human-centred design involves users throughout design and development.",a:true,e:"This is one of its principles, along with user-centred evaluation, an iterative design process, and a multidisciplinary design team."},
{m:"m6x",s:"The purpose of 'acquire' is to secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring the sustainability and scalability of products and services.",a:true,e:"This is the purpose of 'acquire', whose key output is acquired resources and services."},
{m:"m6x",s:"'Acquire' covers only sourcing resources from external suppliers.",a:false,e:"Acquire includes sourcing externally or reallocating internally."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Continuous integration lets developers frequently merge code changes into a central repository, followed by automated builds and tests.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of continuous integration, which can automate the 'build' activity."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Continuous deployment means updates can be deployed at any time, but each deployment is decided case by case.",a:false,e:"That is continuous delivery, while continuous deployment automatically deploys every change that passes automated tests, without additional authorization."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Continuous deployment relies on continuous delivery.",a:true,e:"Continuous delivery makes deployment possible at any time, and continuous deployment builds on it by deploying every change that passes automated tests."},
{m:"m6x",s:"A release is the movement of any service component into a controlled environment.",a:false,e:"That is deployment, while a release is a version of a product, service, or other configuration item (or a collection of them) made available for use."},
{m:"m6x",s:"A change is the addition, modification, or removal of anything that could have a direct or indirect effect on products and services.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of a change, a key term of the 'transition' activity."},
{m:"m6x",s:"The 'transition' activity includes onboarding and offboarding suppliers.",a:true,e:"Transition ensures effective supplier onboarding/offboarding and also includes decommissioning resources and products."},
{m:"m6x",s:"User onboarding and offboarding is part of the 'transition' activity.",a:false,e:"User onboarding/offboarding is part of 'deliver', while transition onboards and offboards suppliers."},
{m:"m6x",s:"An incident is any change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item.",a:false,e:"That is an event, while an incident is an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service."},
{m:"m6x",s:"'Operate' activities are mostly highly visible to customers.",a:false,e:"Operate involves activities mostly invisible to customers unless issues arise."},
{m:"m6x",s:"The 'deliver' activity focuses on delivering services to users in line with SLAs.",a:true,e:"It covers service requests, user onboarding, and other service actions, and service performance against agreed SLA targets is a key metric."},
{m:"m6x",s:"A known error is a problem that has been analysed and resolved.",a:false,e:"A known error is a problem that has been analysed but has not been resolved."},
{m:"m6x",s:"A problem is a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of a problem, while an error is a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents."},
{m:"m6x",s:"The purpose of 'support' includes fulfilling disaster recovery procedures.",a:true,e:"Support identifies and resolves incidents, fulfils disaster recovery procedures, and captures consumer feedback."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Speed of restoration of normal product operation and service delivery is a key metric of the 'operate' activity.",a:false,e:"It is a key metric of 'support', while operate is measured by metrics such as coverage and effectiveness of monitoring and analysis."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Service desk is an enabling practice for both 'deliver' and 'support'.",a:true,e:"Table B.1 marks service desk as enabling for deliver and support only."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Strategy management is an enabling practice for 'discover'.",a:false,e:"For 'discover', strategy management is a supporting practice, while enabling practices include business analysis, portfolio management, and relationship management."},
{m:"m6x",s:"Service design is an enabling practice for every lifecycle activity.",a:false,e:"Table B.1 marks service design as an enabling practice for 'design' only."},
{m:"m7",s:"A value stream is a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of a value stream."},
{m:"m7",s:"Value streams are idealized blueprints that every organization should copy.",a:false,e:"Value streams are real workflows, not blueprints, and de-facto workflows are unique and context-driven."},
{m:"m7",s:"A core value stream enables value for internal customers to support the organization's other value streams.",a:false,e:"That describes an enabling value stream, while a core value stream enables value for consumers in a form intended by the operating model."},
{m:"m7",s:"An enabling value stream enables value for internal customers to support the organization's core value streams.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of an enabling value stream."},
{m:"m7",s:"Whether a value stream is core or enabling can depend on the scope of 'organization' being considered.",a:true,e:"The categories can be cascaded, so a payroll service may be core for the technology department but enabling for the whole company."},
{m:"m7",s:"Value stream mapping starts with 'Map the as-is stream'.",a:false,e:"The first step is 'Identify the value stream', followed by mapping as-is, analysing, mapping to-be, and planning and implementing improvements."},
{m:"m7",s:"In the 'Analyse the stream' step, value-adding activities are separated from non-value-adding activities (waste).",a:true,e:"Step 3 of value stream mapping identifies waste among the value stream steps."},
{m:"m7",s:"Value stream mapping is only about eliminating waste and improving efficiency.",a:false,e:"Value stream mapping also helps create value by ensuring shared understanding of value, clarifying how it is created, and improving quality."},
{m:"m7",s:"Value stream management includes both 'management of' and 'management through' value streams.",a:true,e:"Value stream management is an ongoing focus on how work is done, supported by automation and measurement."},
{m:"m7",s:"Value stream management is a one-time technique for drawing the current workflow.",a:false,e:"Value stream management is the ongoing analysis and improvement of value streams, while mapping is the visualization technique."},
{m:"m7",s:"In value stream mapping, 'Map the to-be stream' comes before 'Analyse the stream'.",a:false,e:"The order is identify, map as-is, analyse, map to-be, and then plan and implement improvements."},
{m:"m7",s:"Value stream management improves the practices that enable value streams.",a:true,e:"It analyses and improves the organization's value streams and the practices that enable them."},
{m:"m8",s:"Continual improvement is a recurring organizational activity performed at all levels to ensure that the organization continually meets stakeholders' expectations.",a:true,e:"This is the ITIL definition of continual improvement."},
{m:"m8",s:"Continual improvement applies only to the organization's management practices.",a:false,e:"It applies to the entire ITIL Value System, including products, services, practices, and relationships."},
{m:"m8",s:"The ITIL Continual Improvement Model is optimized for improvements in chaotic and confused contexts.",a:false,e:"The model is optimized for improvements in ordered and complex contexts."},
{m:"m8",s:"The ITIL Continual Improvement Model has seven steps, starting with 'What is the vision?'.",a:true,e:"It ends with 'How do we keep the improvements relevant?', which loops back to the vision."},
{m:"m8",s:"The output of 'Where are we now?' is the next target state.",a:false,e:"'Where are we now?' produces a baseline assessment, and the next target state comes from 'Where do we want to be?'."},
{m:"m8",s:"'Where do we want to be?' sets improvement objectives with CSFs and KPIs, using SMART where possible.",a:true,e:"It defines the desired future state (Point B) in measurable terms or through directional progress criteria."},
{m:"m8",s:"'What is the vision?' includes making sure stakeholders understand the expected value and who has authority to proceed.",a:true,e:"It also translates the organization's vision and objectives for the relevant unit, team, or individual."},
{m:"m8",s:"In 'Take action', agreed actions must always be performed strictly in sequence.",a:false,e:"Agreed actions and experiments can be conducted as a sequence, simultaneously, or independently."},
{m:"m8",s:"If no SMART objectives exist, 'Are we getting there?' assesses the direction of progress.",a:true,e:"It checks whether the new state is better and closer to the vision."},
{m:"m8",s:"When improvement targets are not met, the responsible team should be identified and blamed.",a:false,e:"Deviations should be treated as learning opportunities, not blame, with lessons learned analysed, documented, and shared."},
{m:"m8",s:"When targets are met, change and knowledge management are used to embed the improvements.",a:true,e:"Leaders and managers also help teams adopt the new ways of working."},
{m:"m8",s:"The output of 'Take action' is the new state assessment.",a:false,e:"'Take action' produces improvement actions and experiments, and the new state assessment is the output of 'Are we getting there?'."},
{m:"m9",s:"The four dimensions are organizations and people, information and technology, partners and suppliers, and value streams and processes.",a:true,e:"Together they support a holistic approach to digital product and service management."},
{m:"m9",s:"'Governance and compliance' is one of the four dimensions of product and service management.",a:false,e:"Governance belongs to the ITIL Value System, while the four dimensions are organizations and people, information and technology, partners and suppliers, and value streams and processes."},
{m:"m9",s:"External PESTLE factors affect only the partners and suppliers dimension.",a:false,e:"External factors influence all four dimensions and must be considered."},
{m:"m9",s:"Safety culture is an organizational culture in which people are comfortable being (and expressing) themselves.",a:true,e:"This is the definition of safety culture in the organizations and people dimension."},
{m:"m9",s:"Conway's law says that an organization's communication structures shape the design of the systems it builds.",a:true,e:"The Reverse Conway approach uses this deliberately, designing team structures to match the desired product or service architecture."},
{m:"m9",s:"Data is processed information with context and meaning.",a:false,e:"Data is raw facts and numbers with no intrinsic meaning, while information is processed data with context and meaning."},
{m:"m9",s:"Agentic AI systems act on behalf of users by autonomously performing predefined tasks within agreed boundaries, subject to human oversight and governance.",a:true,e:"This is the course definition of agentic AI."},
{m:"m9",s:"'Control' is one of the six capabilities of the ITIL AI Capability Model.",a:false,e:"The 6Cs are creation, curation, clarification, cognition, communication, and coordination."},
{m:"m9",s:"Transparency & auditability and integrity are among the six key principles of data governance.",a:true,e:"The six are strategic alignment, accountability and ownership, policies & procedures, transparency & auditability, integrity, and adaptability."},
{m:"m9",s:"According to the course, AI excels at empathy and contextual understanding, while humans support by processing information.",a:false,e:"Humans excel at creativity, empathy, and contextual understanding, while AI supports by processing information, recognizing patterns, and reducing bias."},
{m:"m9",s:"In a chaotic context, causation is clear, so standard procedures work well.",a:false,e:"Clear causation describes an ordered context, while a chaotic context is a crisis situation requiring immediate action."},
{m:"m9",s:"AI governance aims to ensure AI-enabled digital products and services create value while remaining ethical, secure, compliant, and aligned with organizational objectives.",a:true,e:"AI governance is the system by which an organization directs, controls, and monitors the use of artificial intelligence."},
{m:"m9",s:"Chain of command and scope of control are ways to categorize organizational structures.",a:false,e:"They are core elements of any structure, while ways to categorize are geography and location, governance drivers, and scope of work and responsibility."},
{m:"m10",s:"Project management is essential for delivering change within the ITIL Value System.",a:true,e:"It also supports the transition of project outputs into managed, valuable services."},
{m:"m10",s:"PRINCE2 provides practical techniques for rapid, continuous delivery.",a:false,e:"That describes DevOps, while PRINCE2 provides comprehensive methods for project, programme, and portfolio management."},
{m:"m10",s:"Together, ITIL and PRINCE2 ensure alignment between strategy, change initiatives, and operations.",a:true,e:"PRINCE2 supports the delivery of changes that implement or modify products and services."},
{m:"m10",s:"ITIL and DevOps are competing approaches, so an organization should choose one of them.",a:false,e:"ITIL and DevOps are complementary, and together they enable resilient, adaptive, and customer-focused outcomes."}];
window.KEYFACTS=[
{m:"m1",f:"Service: a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes consumers want, without them managing specific costs and risks."},
{m:"m1",f:"Product: a configuration of an organization's resources designed to offer value for a consumer."},
{m:"m1",f:"Digital product: a combination of an organization's resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers."},
{m:"m1",f:"Digital service: a service that fully or largely relies on digital products."},
{m:"m1",f:"Goods: tangible resources transferred (or available for transfer) to a consumer with ownership and associated rights and responsibilities."},
{m:"m1",f:"Digital product and service management: a set of specialized organizational capabilities enabling value via digital products and services."},
{m:"m1",f:"Service offering: a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group."},
{m:"m1",f:"A service offering may include goods, access to resources, and service actions."},
{m:"m1",f:"Service actions are performed by the provider, or by the provider and consumer together. Service request fulfilment is a common form."},
{m:"m1",f:"Access to resources: the consumer uses the provider's resources (hotel rooms, vehicles, cloud services) while the provider keeps ownership."},
{m:"m1",f:"Products provide capabilities. Service offerings describe potential value. Accepting an offering starts service delivery and consumption."},
{m:"m1",f:"Digital services are always based on digital products. One product may enable many services, and one service may use several products."},
{m:"m1",f:"'Digitally enabled' products depend on digital technology for core functions: without it, their potential value disappears."},
{m:"m1",f:"'Digitally supported' products perform core functions without digital technology and use it only for non-core functions."},
{m:"m1",f:"'Purely digital' products are software products with no analogue or physical components."},
{m:"m1",f:"Output: a tangible or intangible deliverable of an activity. Outcome: a result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outputs."},
{m:"m1",f:"Value: the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something. Service value is the same, applied to a service."},
{m:"m1",f:"Value co-creation: providers, consumers, and other stakeholders jointly create value by combining resources, capabilities, and actions."},
{m:"m1",f:"Cost: the amount of money spent on a specific activity, resource, product or service."},
{m:"m1",f:"Risk: a possible event that could cause harm or loss or make it more difficult to achieve objectives."},
{m:"m1",f:"Consumers face costs and risks reduced or removed by a service, and costs and risks imposed by consuming it."},
{m:"m2",f:"Organization: a person or group of people with its own functions, responsibilities, authorities, and relationships to achieve objectives."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service provider: an organization responsible for delivery and support of services."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service consumer: an organization responsible for procurement and use of services."},
{m:"m2",f:"Product vendor: an organization responsible for the creation and continual improvement of digital products and related service offerings."},
{m:"m2",f:"Consumer roles: the sponsor authorizes budget, the customer defines requirements and owns outcomes, and the user uses services."},
{m:"m2",f:"Organizations form chains and networks of service relationships, consuming from many providers and providing to many consumers."},
{m:"m2",f:"The three types of service relationship are basic, cooperative, and collaborative (partnership)."},
{m:"m2",f:"Basic relationship: support and efficiency focus, operational level, off-the-shelf or commodity services, standard contracts and SLAs."},
{m:"m2",f:"Cooperative relationship: improvement and effectiveness focus, operational and tactical levels, configured or customized services."},
{m:"m2",f:"Collaborative relationship: innovation and growth focus, up to strategic level, bespoke services, sometimes no formal agreement."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service journey: activities and interactions performed by organizations in service relationships to fulfil provider and consumer roles."},
{m:"m2",f:"Band of visibility: the aspects of the consumer visible to the provider, and the aspects of the provider visible to the consumer."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service journey steps: Explore, Engage, Offer, Agree, Onboard, Co-create, Reflect. They may change order or happen simultaneously."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service quality: the sum of a service's characteristics relevant to its ability to satisfy stated and implied needs."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service level: a set of metrics that define expected or achieved service quality."},
{m:"m2",f:"Utility: the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need, 'what the service does' (fit for purpose)."},
{m:"m2",f:"Warranty: assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements, 'how the service performs' (fit for use)."},
{m:"m2",f:"Warranty typically covers availability, capacity, levels of security, and continuity of the service."},
{m:"m2",f:"Sustainability: assurance of meeting, now and in future, requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth."},
{m:"m2",f:"User experience (UX): the sum of functional and emotional interactions with a service and provider, as perceived by a service user."},
{m:"m2",f:"SLA: a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer identifying the services provided and the agreed level of each."},
{m:"m2",f:"Service value = utility + warranty + sustainability + experience. No category is more important than the others."},
{m:"m3",f:"ITIL Value System: a model of how all components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation."},
{m:"m3",f:"ITIL VS components: guiding principles, governance, value chain, management practices, and continual improvement."},
{m:"m3",f:"The key inputs to the ITIL VS are opportunity and demand. Its outcome is value."},
{m:"m3",f:"Purpose of the ITIL VS: ensure the organization continually and sustainably co-creates value with all stakeholders."},
{m:"m3",f:"Each organization has its own value system. Value systems interact and form an ecosystem that enables value co-creation."},
{m:"m3",f:"Management system: interconnected elements that establish policy and objectives and enable the achievement of those objectives."},
{m:"m3",f:"In a management system, guiding principles are universal recommendations for decisions and actions."},
{m:"m3",f:"In a management system, management practices are sets of organizational capabilities designed for accomplishing specific objectives."},
{m:"m3",f:"Value chain (management system view): interconnected activities that create and deliver products and services."},
{m:"m3",f:"The value chain is the central component of the ITIL VS: its eight activities run from discover to support."},
{m:"m4",f:"Governance: the system by which an organization is directed and controlled."},
{m:"m4",f:"Governance of digital technology: a human-based system by which the current and future use of digital technology is governed."},
{m:"m4",f:"Governance of digital technology comprises directing, overseeing, and accountability."},
{m:"m4",f:"Digital technology governance is realized through four activities: engage stakeholders, evaluate, direct, and monitor."},
{m:"m4",f:"Engage stakeholders: the governing body identifies needs, clarifies commitments, and agrees on accountability for technology use."},
{m:"m4",f:"Evaluate: the governing body assesses the organization's current state, strategies, portfolios, policies, and relationships."},
{m:"m4",f:"Direct: policies and strategies are established and modified."},
{m:"m4",f:"Monitor: with policies and strategies in place, the governing body monitors performance and compliance."},
{m:"m4",f:"Governance defines how organizations are directed and controlled, including oversight of digital technology."},
{m:"m5",f:"Guiding principles guide an organization in all circumstances, regardless of changes in goals, strategies, type of work, or structure."},
{m:"m5",f:"There is no fixed order or hierarchy among the guiding principles: all are equally important for the ITIL VS."},
{m:"m5",f:"Do not rely on just one or two guiding principles: consider all of them and how they complement each other."},
{m:"m5",f:"Focus on value: all activities should link back, directly or indirectly, to value for the organization, customers, and stakeholders."},
{m:"m5",f:"Focus on value starts by knowing who is being served: who the service consumer and key stakeholders are."},
{m:"m5",f:"Value for the consumer is defined by their own needs, is subjective, and changes over time and in different circumstances."},
{m:"m5",f:"Start where you are: do not start over without first considering what is already available to be leveraged."},
{m:"m5",f:"Start where you are: use measurement to support, not replace, what is observed. Reports and reality often differ."},
{m:"m5",f:"Start where you are: an observer with little or no prior knowledge has no preconceptions and may spot what insiders overlook."},
{m:"m5",f:"Progress iteratively with feedback: each iteration should be manageable and managed, returning tangible results in a timely manner."},
{m:"m5",f:"Feedback: information about stakeholder reactions and opinions, used as a basis for improvement."},
{m:"m5",f:"Feedback loop: a situation where part of the output of an activity is used for new input."},
{m:"m5",f:"Stakeholder feedback can be structured or unstructured, solicited or unsolicited, and captured throughout the service journey."},
{m:"m5",f:"Surveys and online feedback forms are solicited, structured feedback. Complaints and compliments are unsolicited, unstructured."},
{m:"m5",f:"Collaborate and promote visibility: the right people in the correct roles bring better buy-in, more relevance, and long-term success."},
{m:"m5",f:"Customers are the most significant stakeholders when deciding whom to collaborate with."},
{m:"m5",f:"Lack of visibility causes poor decisions, disengagement, and low adoption. Use dashboards, Kanban boards, and other visual tools."},
{m:"m5",f:"Think and work holistically: no product, service, practice, process, team, or supplier stands alone."},
{m:"m5",f:"A holistic approach considers the four dimensions, the full lifecycle, the supply chain, impact on consumers, and PESTLE factors."},
{m:"m5",f:"Keep it simple and practical: eliminate any process, product, service, action, or metric that fails to add value or a useful outcome."},
{m:"m5",f:"Providing a solution for every exception leads to over-complication. Design rules to handle exceptions instead."},
{m:"m5",f:"Optimize and automate: technology use, especially generative AI, should be subject to governance, ethical, and compliance controls."},
{m:"m5",f:"Simplify and/or optimize before automating. Automation for automation's sake can increase costs and risks."},
{m:"m5",f:"Road to optimization: context -> current state -> future state -> stakeholder engagement -> iterative execution -> monitor impact."},
{m:"m6",f:"ITIL has 34 management practices: 12 general management practices and 22 product and service management practices."},
{m:"m6",f:"Management practice: organizational resources and capabilities designed and adopted for performing work or accomplishing an objective."},
{m:"m6",f:"General management practices: architecture, knowledge, portfolio, project, relationship, risk, strategy, and supplier management."},
{m:"m6",f:"General management practices also include continual improvement, measurement and reporting, and organizational change management."},
{m:"m6",f:"Workforce and talent management is one of the 12 general management practices."},
{m:"m6",f:"Information security management and service financial management are product and service management practices in ITIL (Version 5)."},
{m:"m6",f:"Lifecycle management activities are not performed in a fixed sequence. From the organizational perspective, they form a value chain."},
{m:"m6",f:"Value chain: an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service."},
{m:"m6",f:"Organization's purpose: what an organization does for its consumers and other stakeholders, and why."},
{m:"m6",f:"Operating model: how an organization co-creates value with customers and other stakeholders, and how it runs itself."},
{m:"m6",f:"Figure 5.3: purpose defines the operating model -> it includes value chain activities -> they form core value streams."},
{m:"m6",f:"Core value streams depend on management practices, which enable both value chain activities and core value streams."},
{m:"m6",f:"Each value chain activity is supported and enabled by several management practices."},
{m:"m6",f:"Management practices integrate resources from the four dimensions to form management capabilities."},
{m:"m6",f:"All 34 ITIL Official Practice Guides share the same structure, from General Information to Recommendations for the practice success."},
{m:"m6",f:"Each practice guide has chapters for the four dimensions, plus General Information, Capability assessment and development, Recommendations."},
{m:"m6",f:"Practice guide benefits: consistent, comprehensive, practical, and continual improvement (linked to the ITIL Maturity Model)."},
{m:"m6",f:"Value chain pattern 1 (support the core business with technology) performs all lifecycle activities, from discover to support."},
{m:"m6",f:"Value chain pattern 3 (IT services plus coordination of third parties) performs only deliver and support itself."},
{m:"m6",f:"Value chain pattern 5 (custom software development): design, acquire, build, and transition (partially)."},
{m:"m6",f:"Value chain pattern 6 (IT systems operation as a business): transition (partially), operate, and support."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Discover purpose: keep product roadmaps and service offerings continually aligned with consumer needs and organizational strategy."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Discover key output: an updated product and service roadmap."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Design purpose: create specifications and prototypes for products and services, detailing functionality, UX, and operational framework."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Design approaches: proactive (anticipate needs) or reactive (respond to requirements). Design always includes interactions and experience."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Specification: a detailed document of the critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics of a product or service to be built."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Prototype: an initial version of a product or service showing its basic form, functionality, and operational capabilities."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Human-centred design: design decisions guided by users' lived experiences, formalized in ISO 13407:1999 (now ISO 9241-210:2019)."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Acquire purpose: secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring sustainability and scalability of products and services."},
{m:"m6x",f:"CMDB: a database storing configuration records throughout their lifecycle and maintaining the relationships between them."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Build purpose: develop, integrate, and test digital products and services, transforming designs into functional solutions."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Continuous integration: developers frequently merge code into a central repository, followed by automated builds and tests."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Testing: executing a system or component under specified conditions, observing or recording results, and evaluating it."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Transition purpose: seamlessly introduce new or updated products and services into operational environments and onboard/offboard suppliers."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Deployment: the movement of any service component into a controlled environment."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Release: a version of a product, service, or other configuration item, or a collection of them, made available for use."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Change: the addition, modification, or removal of anything that could have a direct or indirect effect on products and services."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Continuous delivery: updates can be deployed to production at any time, but deployment decisions are taken case by case."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Continuous deployment: every change that passes automated tests goes to production automatically, without additional authorization."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Operate purpose: maintain and monitor digital products, services, and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Event: any change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Incident: an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Reliability: ability of a product, service, or configuration item to perform its intended function for a specified time or number of cycles."},
{m:"m6x",f:"SRE applies software engineering to infrastructure and operations problems, aiming for scalable and highly reliable software systems."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Observability: the ability to understand a complicated system's internal state by analysing external outputs: metrics, logs, and traces."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Deliver purpose: provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain quality standards, and gather consumer feedback."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Service request: a request from a user or their authorized representative that starts a service action agreed as normal service delivery."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Support purpose: identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Problem: a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents. Known error: a problem analysed but not yet resolved."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Error: a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Disaster: a sudden unplanned event causing great damage or serious loss, so critical business functions fail for a set minimum period."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Support is triggered by operations incidents, delivery incidents, and disasters. Its end result is restored normal operation and delivery."},
{m:"m6x",f:"Service desk is an enabling practice for deliver and support. Service design is an enabling practice for design only."},
{m:"m6x",f:"For 'discover', strategy management is only a supporting practice, while portfolio and relationship management are enabling."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value stream: a series of steps an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value streams are real workflows, not blueprints. De-facto workflows are unique and context-driven."},
{m:"m7",f:"Core value stream: enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization's operating model."},
{m:"m7",f:"Enabling value stream: enables value for internal customers to support the organization's core value streams."},
{m:"m7",f:"Core and enabling categories cascade: a payroll service can be core for the technology department yet enabling for the whole company."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value stream mapping: a technique to visualize and analyse the flow of work, information, and value created for the service consumer."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value stream mapping steps: identify the stream, map as-is, analyse, map to-be, plan and implement improvements."},
{m:"m7",f:"Analysing a value stream separates value-adding activities from non-value-adding activities (waste)."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value stream mapping ensures shared understanding of value, clarifies how value is created, and optimizes flow and quality."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value stream management: ongoing analysis and improvement of value streams, covering both 'management of' and 'management through'."},
{m:"m7",f:"Value stream management is supported by automation and measurement and improves the practices that enable streams."},
{m:"m7",f:"ITIL Car Rental used value stream mapping to fix booking and cleaning coordination: it created value, not just less waste."},
{m:"m8",f:"Continual improvement: a recurring activity at all levels of an organization to ensure it continually meets stakeholders' expectations."},
{m:"m8",f:"Continual improvement applies to the entire ITIL VS, including products, services, practices, and relationships."},
{m:"m8",f:"ITIL Continual Improvement Model: a universal model for iterative improvements, optimized for ordered and complex contexts."},
{m:"m8",f:"The Continual Improvement Model has seven steps, from 'What is the vision?' to 'How do we keep the improvements relevant?'."},
{m:"m8",f:"'What is the vision?' output: an improvement vision aligned with business goals and objectives."},
{m:"m8",f:"'Where are we now?' output: a baseline assessment of products, competencies, workflows, technology, feedback, and culture."},
{m:"m8",f:"'Where do we want to be?' output: the next target state, with CSFs and KPIs, using SMART objectives where possible."},
{m:"m8",f:"'How do we get there?' output: the route to the next target state. With an unclear path, identify hypotheses and experiments."},
{m:"m8",f:"'Take action' output: improvement actions and experiments, run in sequence, simultaneously, or independently."},
{m:"m8",f:"'Are we getting there?' output: a new state assessment. Without SMART objectives, assess the direction of progress."},
{m:"m8",f:"Keeping improvements relevant: when targets are met, embed them with change and knowledge management."},
{m:"m8",f:"When improvement targets are not met: inform stakeholders, share lessons learned, and treat deviations as learning, not blame."},
{m:"m9",f:"The four dimensions: organizations and people, information and technology, partners and suppliers, value streams and processes."},
{m:"m9",f:"The four dimensions ensure a holistic approach, and each is critical to the effective and efficient facilitation of value."},
{m:"m9",f:"External PESTLE factors influence all four dimensions and must be considered."},
{m:"m9",f:"Culture: a set of values shared by a group of people, expressed through behaviour, ideas, beliefs, and practices."},
{m:"m9",f:"Safety culture: an organizational culture in which people are comfortable being (and expressing) themselves."},
{m:"m9",f:"Good leadership balances the global good with individual self-interest."},
{m:"m9",f:"Core structure elements: chain of command, roles and responsibilities, scope of control, decision-making authority, departments/teams."},
{m:"m9",f:"Conway's law: organizations design systems that copy their communication structures. Reverse Conway shapes teams to fit the architecture."},
{m:"m9",f:"Humans excel at creativity, empathy, and contextual understanding. AI is a collaborative partner that enhances human strengths."},
{m:"m9",f:"AI supports people by processing information, recognizing patterns, and reducing bias."},
{m:"m9",f:"The ITIL AI Capability Model (6C): creation, curation, clarification, cognition, communication, and coordination."},
{m:"m9",f:"AI governance: how an organization directs, controls, and monitors AI use so AI-enabled services stay ethical, secure, and compliant."},
{m:"m9",f:"Generative AI: AI systems that create new content, such as text, images, or code, based on patterns learned from existing data."},
{m:"m9",f:"Agentic AI: AI that acts on behalf of users, autonomously performing predefined tasks within agreed boundaries, under human oversight."},
{m:"m9",f:"Data: raw facts with no intrinsic meaning. Information: processed data with context. Knowledge: applied information and experience."},
{m:"m9",f:"Information has value only when it enables outcomes. Out-of-context data has no value."},
{m:"m9",f:"Data governance: rules, policies, standards, processes, and controls that keep data assets secure, usable, and reliable."},
{m:"m9",f:"Data governance principles 1-3: strategic alignment, accountability and ownership, policies & procedures."},
{m:"m9",f:"Data governance principles 4-6: transparency & auditability, integrity, and adaptability."},
{m:"m9",f:"Supplier strategy factors include resource scarcity (lack of skills), cost concerns, and demand patterns (seasonal or variable demand)."},
{m:"m9",f:"Supplier strategy is also shaped by strategic focus, corporate culture, subject matter expertise, and external constraints."},
{m:"m9",f:"Complexity contexts: ordered (clear causation), complex (understood in retrospect), chaotic (crisis), confused (orient before action)."},
{m:"m9",f:"Value streams and processes covers organizational and cross-organizational workflows: which activities happen and how they are organized."},
{m:"m10",f:"ITIL defines how organizations manage digital products and services. DevOps provides practical techniques for rapid, continuous delivery."},
{m:"m10",f:"Together, ITIL and DevOps enable resilient, adaptive, and customer-focused outcomes."},
{m:"m10",f:"PRINCE2 provides comprehensive methods for project, programme, and portfolio management."},
{m:"m10",f:"Combining ITIL with PRINCE2 ensures alignment between strategy, change initiatives, and operations."},
{m:"m10",f:"Project management is essential for delivering change in the ITIL VS and moves project outputs into managed, valuable services."},
{m:"m10",f:"Figure 7.1: ITIL is the language of growth, PRINCE2 the language of success, DevOps Institute the language of collaboration."},
{m:"m10",f:"Figure 7.1 centre: ITIL, PRINCE2, and DevOps together enable ongoing co-creation of value in VUCA environments."},
{m:"m10",f:"The ITIL Foundation exam: closed book, 60 minutes, 40 questions, 65% pass mark (26 of 40)."}];

;
