window.QRG=[
{m:"m10",h:"ITIL Foundation (Version 5) exam",li:["40 multiple choice questions. Pass mark: 65% (26 questions) or more.","The exam assesses recall and understanding of the ITIL framework. ITIL Foundation (Version 5) is the prerequisite for the ITIL (Version 5) higher level qualifications.","Each numbered section of this guide matches the same-numbered section of the syllabus."]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.1.1 Digital product and service management",li:["Covers the design, delivery, and continual improvement of digital products and the services based on them, across their entire lifecycle.","Gives a common language: what products and services are, how they interact and contribute to value co-creation, service offerings, service actions, and the roles of providers and consumers in collaborative service relationships.","Value is not delivered in isolation, but co-created through the alignment of outcomes, costs, risks, and quality attributes.","Holistic view through the four dimensions: organizations and people, information and technology, partners and suppliers, value streams and processes.","Figure 1.5 The ITIL Service Relationship Model: organizations A -> B -> C are linked by service relationships. Inside each organization: 1 capture and process -> 2 discover and design -> 3 acquire and build -> 4 deliver and support. Service delivery comes from the service provider, needs, requirements, and feedback come from the service consumer."],d:[["Digital product and service management","a set of specialized organizational capabilities for enabling value for customers in the form of digital products and services."]]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.1.2-1.1.3 Product, service, digital product, digital service",d:[["Product","a configuration of an organization's resources designed to offer value for a consumer."],["Service","a means of enabling value co-creation by facilitating outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks."],["Digital product","a combination of an organization's resources based on digital technology and designed to offer value to consumers."],["Digital service","a service that fully or largely relies on digital products."]]},
{m:"m8",h:"1.1.4 Continual improvement (key concept)",d:[["Continual improvement","a recurring organizational activity performed at all levels of an organization to ensure that the organization continually meets stakeholders' expectations."]]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.1.5 Lifecycle and the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model",p:["The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model (ITIL PSLM) is a structured model that describes the set of activities an organization uses to conceive, design, create, deliver, operate, and continually improve digital products and services.","It shows how products and services evolve over time and how management focus shifts between product development and service delivery to enable ongoing value creation."],d:[["Lifecycle","the full set of stages, transitions, and associated statuses in the life of a service, product, practice, or other entity."]]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.1.6 Goods",p:["Goods may accompany services and typically involve a transfer of ownership or usage rights, along with associated responsibilities, distinguishing them from service actions and access to resources."],d:[["Goods","tangible resources that are transferred or available for transfer from a service provider to a service consumer, together with ownership and associated rights and responsibilities."]]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.1.7 Utility, warranty, user experience, sustainability",d:[["Utility","the functionality offered by a product or service to meet a particular need. Utility can be summarized as 'what the service does' and can be used to determine whether a service is 'fit for purpose'. To have utility, a service must either support the performance of the consumer or remove constraints from the consumer. Many services do both."],["Warranty","the assurance that a product or service will meet the agreed requirements. Warranty can be summarized as 'how the service performs' and can be used to determine whether a service is 'fit for use'. Warranty often relates to service levels aligned with the needs of service consumers. This may be based on a formal agreement, or it may be a marketing message or brand image. Warranty typically addresses such areas as the availability of the service, its capacity, levels of security, and continuity. A service may be said to provide acceptable assurance, or 'warranty', if all defined and agreed conditions are met."],["User experience (UX)","the sum of functional and emotional interactions with a service and service provider as perceived by a service user."],["Sustainability","the assurance that a product or service meets and will continue to meet the requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth."]]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.2.1-1.2.2 Service offering and the three service interactions",d:[["Service offering","a formal description of one or more services designed to address the needs of a target consumer group. A service offering may include goods, access to resources, and service actions."],["Service action","an action performed by a service provider or jointly by a service provider and a service consumer."],["Transfer of goods","a form of service interaction in which a service provider delivers tangible goods to a service consumer, transferring ownership and the associated rights and responsibilities."],["Access to resources","a form of service interaction in which a service consumer is granted the right to use a service provider's resources under agreed conditions, without transferring ownership."]]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.2.3-1.2.4 Explaining service offerings and interactions",li:["A service offering is how an organization presents one or more services to a specific group of service consumers: what is available, how value is delivered, and under what conditions the service can be used.","Three forms of interaction between a service consumer and a service: service actions, access to resources, and, in some cases, transfer of goods. They may combine in any given offering.","Clear offerings enable consistent communication, effective expectation management, and alignment between service design, delivery, and consumption.","What might look like a transfer or action to the user may, from the provider's perspective, actually be access to resources.","Describe offerings from the consumer's perspective (for example, in a service catalogue), with clear terms about ownership and responsibilities for any goods or actions involved.","Figure 3.3: technology resources compose digital products, which enable and support service offerings, which describe digital services."]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.3.1-1.3.3 Value, value co-creation, cost, risk, output, outcome",p:["Value emerges when services are consumed and used to achieve desired outcomes, while costs, risks, and responsibilities are shared between provider and consumer."],d:[["Value","the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something."],["Value co-creation","the collaborative process by which service providers, consumers, and other stakeholders jointly create value through the use of products and services, by combining resources, capabilities, and actions across the service relationship to achieve desired outcomes."],["Cost","the amount of money spent on a specific activity, resource, product, or service."],["Risk","a possible event that could cause harm or loss, or make it more difficult to achieve objectives."],["Output","a tangible or intangible deliverable of an activity."],["Outcome","a result for a stakeholder enabled by one or more outputs."]]},
{m:"m1",h:"1.3.4-1.3.6 Outputs vs outcomes and how value is co-created",li:["Value is always determined by the beneficiary, typically the customer or user, and is achieved through outcomes that meet their needs.","A service helps consumers achieve outcomes, while the service provider takes responsibility for managing the associated costs and risks.","Outputs do not create value by themselves. Value is realized only when outputs are used effectively to enable the desired outcomes for the service consumer.","Co-creation: the provider maintains and improves the service (performance, availability, security), the consumer actively uses it to realize outcomes meaningful in their context. Feedback is exchanged, expectations adjusted, and the service evolves.","The value achieved is a product of both use and contribution, shaped over time through deliberate and continual interaction.","Achieving outcomes involves costs and risks. Providers lower the consumer's costs and risks through specialization and economies of scale.","Services can introduce new costs or risks, so value is only realized if the net effect is positive: benefits and removed burdens outweigh any new negatives (Figure 3.1: positive outcomes, costs removed, risks removed vs negative outcomes, costs introduced, risks introduced)."]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.1 Organization, service provider, service consumer, digital product vendor",p:["Service relationships are the structured interactions between a service provider and a service consumer that enable ongoing value co-creation: how the parties collaborate, communicate, and share responsibilities across the service lifecycle."],d:[["Organization","a person or a group of people that has its own functions with responsibilities, authorities, and relationships to achieve its objectives."],["Service provider","an organization responsible for delivery and support of services."],["Service consumer","an organization responsible for procurement and use of services."],["Digital product vendor","an organization responsible for the creation and continual improvement of digital products and related service offerings."]]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.2 Basic, cooperative, collaborative (partnership) service relationships",p:["UWSE is not spelled out in the QRG. It most likely stands for utility, warranty, sustainability and (user) experience, the concepts of syllabus 1.1.7."],d:[["Basic service relationship","transactional arrangement with provider-set, fixed terms and often warranty-centred SLAs as the formal focus; experience is largely assumed and governed by market signals. The band of visibility is the narrowest of the three types of service relationship, so many frictions stay hidden to the other party."],["Cooperative service relationship","negotiated arrangement where parties include UWSE-scoped experience objectives and feedback commitments in the SLA, treating measures as hypotheses. The band of visibility is wider than in a basic relationship, enabling monitoring of experience objectives while retaining separate governance."],["Collaborative service relationship (partnership)","a relationship between two organizations that involves working closely together to achieve common goals and objectives."]]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.3-1.4.4 Service journey, sponsor, customer, user",d:[["Service journey","the sum of activities and interactions performed by organizations engaged in service relationships to fulfil their roles as a service provider and a service consumer."],["Sponsor","the role that authorizes budget for service consumption."],["Customer","the role that defines the requirements for products and services and takes responsibility for the outcomes of service consumption."],["User","the role that uses services."]]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.5-1.4.6 Service quality, service level, SLA",d:[["Service quality","the sum of the characteristics of a service that are relevant to its ability to satisfy stated and implied needs."],["Service level","a set of metrics that define expected or achieved service quality."],["Service Level Agreement (SLA)","a documented agreement between a service provider and a customer that identifies the services provided and the agreed level of each service."]]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.7-1.4.9 Roles of providers, consumers, and digital product vendors",li:["Figure 3.4: the vendor acquires and manages technology resources, and builds and manages digital products. The provider offers and negotiates service offerings, and delivers and supports digital services. The consumer impacts digital products, negotiates and accepts service offerings, and consumes digital services."],d:[["Service providers","deliver outcomes that meet consumer needs. Beyond providing technical resources, they design, operate, and support services so consumers achieve value without having to manage the underlying complexity. They align offerings with consumer requirements, maintain service levels, and feed feedback into continual improvement. One organization may be a provider in some relationships and a consumer in others."],["Service consumers","clearly communicate their needs, engage with the provider's offerings, and use the services to achieve intended outcomes. They manage their own resources to use the service effectively, take part in defining requirements, and give feedback. May include the sponsor, customer, and user roles (funding, decision-making, day-to-day interaction)."],["Digital product vendors","create and continually develop the digital products that underpin service offerings, supplying the foundational technology and components that providers configure and integrate into services. Often independent of the provider-consumer relationship, but their performance and innovation directly affect service quality, capabilities, and evolution. A vendor may also act as a provider."]]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.10 Basic vs cooperative vs collaborative relationships",li:["Relationships differ in the level of interaction and shared responsibility.","Basic: limited engagement, largely transactional.","Cooperative: more structured communication and coordination to improve service performance.","Collaborative: close partnership, shared goals, and joint responsibility for continual improvement and value co-creation."]},
{m:"m2",h:"1.4.11 Utility, warranty, UX, sustainability in service relationships",li:["These four aspects define service quality and influence the consumer's perception of value.","Utility: the functional value ('what the service does'). It supports the consumer's performance or removes constraints, aligning the offering with consumer needs.","Warranty: quality factors such as availability, capacity, security, and continuity. A highly functional service (high utility) is not valuable if it is frequently unavailable or insecure.","User experience: subjective but critical. A service with utility and warranty can still fail to create value if the experience is poor or frustrating.","Sustainability: environmental and societal impact. The service is designed and delivered to be environmentally responsible, socially beneficial, and economically viable.","High value needs sufficient utility and warranty, a positive user experience, and sustainable operation where required. These categories also serve as metrics domains."]},
{m:"m9",h:"2.1.1 The ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management",p:["Collectively critical to the effective and efficient facilitation of value for customers and other stakeholders in the form of products and services. They give a holistic perspective instead of a focus on isolated components.","Neglecting any dimension can undermine the whole. The dimensions apply at all levels (from an individual product or practice to the entire ITIL Value System) and are influenced by external factors (Figure 1.4: PESTLE factors around the four dimensions, with products and services and value at the centre)."],li:["Organizations and people","Information and technology","Partners and suppliers","Value streams and processes"]},
{m:"m9",h:"2.1.2 Scope of each dimension",d:[["Organizations and people","the human side: organizational structure, governance, roles and responsibilities, and corporate culture. The right people with the appropriate skills and competencies, organized effectively. Formal structures and processes are not enough: attitudes and motivations (shaped by culture and leadership) significantly affect success."],["Culture","a set of values shared by a group of people, expressed through people's behaviour and their ideas, beliefs, and practices."],["Information and technology","the information, data, and technologies used and managed by services and by the product and service management system. Information is often one of the most valuable assets, technology is the enabler. Includes information architecture, knowledge management, and emerging technologies (AI, machine learning, automation). The tech stack and data management must fit the organization's needs and current and future demand."],["Partners and suppliers","the relationships with other organizations used to deliver products and services. Partners: close, collaborative value co-creation. Suppliers: third parties providing goods or services. Covers choosing, managing, and integrating suppliers, sourcing models (outsourcing, insourcing, multi-sourcing), contracts and agreements, supplier reliability, and relationship management."],["Value streams and processes","the workflows and processes used to create and deliver products and services. Processes (documented sequences of activities) are building blocks that execute parts of value streams. Coordinate activities across departments, remove non-value-adding steps (Lean), identify bottlenecks. Includes process controls and governance and optimization for complexity. Looks at how work is actually done ('as performed') versus how it was designed on paper."]]},
{m:"m9",h:"2.1.3 Holistic approach",li:["The four dimensions are highly interrelated. No dimension can be considered in isolation: a change in one almost always affects the others.","Example: new technology may require new skills, roles, or cultural adjustments, changes to sourcing models or contracts, and changes to workflows, controls, and activities.","A holistic approach reduces the risk of unintended consequences, supports consistent service performance, and keeps products and services balanced, sustainable, and aligned with objectives."]},
{m:"m9",h:"2.2-2.2.1 Internal and external factors (PESTLE)",p:["Internal factors reflect the organization's own context, capabilities, culture, and constraints. External factors arise from the broader environment and influence all four dimensions."],tb:{head:["External factor","Influence"],rows:[["Political","changes in regulation or government policy may impact governance, sourcing, and compliance requirements"],["Economic","conditions can influence investment, sourcing strategies, and demand"],["Social","factors affect customer expectations, workforce behaviour, and culture"],["Technological","developments drive new ways of working and service delivery"],["Legal","requirements impose obligations on data protection, security, and contracts"],["Environmental","considerations influence sustainability practices and responsible service design"]]}},
{m:"m9",h:"2.2.2 Why the four dimensions matter",li:["Each dimension highlights a specific risk area if overlooked: processes and technology fail without supportive culture, skills, and leadership, and strong internal capabilities fail if partners and suppliers are poorly integrated or misaligned.","Decisions need a system-wide perspective: assess improvements (such as automation or sourcing changes) for their impact on people, workflows, and relationships. Ignoring interdependencies gives short-term gains but long-term instability, reduced service quality, or stakeholder dissatisfaction.","A structured lens for continual improvement: find whether the root cause lies in people and skills, process and workflow design, technology choices, or external dependencies, so improvements address causes rather than symptoms."]},
{m:"m6",h:"3.1 Introduction to the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle",p:["To ensure high quality of their services, organizations:"],li:["capture and process customers' needs, requirements and feedback","discover needs and opportunities, and design digital products and services","acquire necessary resources and build digital products","deliver and support digital services."]},
{m:"m6",h:"3.1.1 The eight lifecycle management activities",p:["ITIL uses a detailed Product and Service Lifecycle Model with eight lifecycle stages. Moving through the lifecycle, the focus of management shifts between digital product and digital service (Figure 4.1 Duality)."],d:[["Discover","explore and prioritize needs and opportunities for the product and service."],["Design","create product and service solution meeting or exceeding the requirements."],["Acquire","procure or allocate resources required to build the product."],["Build","create, configure, and test technology solutions constituting the product."],["Transition","deploy the new product into the live environment."],["Operate","operate the product to ensure agreed performance."],["Deliver","deliver digital services based on live products."],["Support","restore normal operation of products and delivery of services when needed."]]},
{m:"m6",h:"3.1.2-3.1.3 Value chain activities enable the lifecycle",li:["The lifecycle is enabled by eight key value chain activities: discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support.","Each activity contributes to transforming demand or opportunity into outcomes that deliver value to stakeholders.","The value chain lets organizations define and configure value streams that reflect their specific needs and context.","The activities are not performed as a fixed sequence or linear process: they may run in parallel, be repeated, or be revisited based on changing needs, feedback, or context.","From an organizational perspective, the activities form a value chain rather than a cycle, allowing iterative use of the lifecycle."]},
{m:"m6x",h:"3.2 Purpose and outputs of the lifecycle management activities",p:["Overall purpose: to ensure that digital products and services are effectively designed, built, delivered, operated, and continually improved to support value co-creation. The activities give a flexible operating model to respond to changing needs, manage resources efficiently, and stay aligned with organizational objectives."],tb:{head:["Activity","Purpose: to...","Key outputs"],rows:[["Discover","ensure continual alignment of the product roadmaps and related service offerings with the needs of service consumers and with the organizational strategy","updated product roadmap, product and service improvement initiatives, requirements and problem statements for design"],["Design","create specifications and prototypes for products and services, detailing their functionality, user experience, and operational framework","product or service specifications and prototypes"],["Acquire","secure and allocate necessary resources efficiently, ensuring the sustainability and scalability of products and services","acquired resources or services ready for build or operation (e.g. cloud subscription, hardware delivered, vendor contract signed)"],["Build","develop, integrate, and test digital products and services, transforming designs into functional solutions","built and tested product and service solutions"],["Transition","seamlessly introduce new or updated products and services into operational environments and ensure effective onboarding/offboarding of suppliers","on/off-boarded suppliers, and products, services, and resources deployed to the target live environment"],["Operate","maintain and monitor digital products and services, and supporting systems, ensuring optimal performance and reliability","operating products and services, performance records and reports (input to 'deliver' and 'support')"],["Deliver","provide services to users, manage user onboarding/offboarding, maintain quality standards, and gather consumer feedback","delivered services, service performance and user feedback records and reports"],["Support","identify and resolve incidents, fulfil disaster recovery procedures, and capture consumer feedback","restored normal product and service operation and service delivery, which feed into improvements"]]}},
{m:"m6x",h:"3.2.1-3.2.8 Activity notes",li:["Discover: researches the context (customer needs, market trends, new technologies, strategic objectives) to decide which products or enhancements to pursue.","Design: ideas and requirements become concrete solutions (functionality, UX, data models, support and delivery processes). May take several iterations. Specifications describe what will be built, prototypes test design assumptions.","Acquire: procurement and sourcing (infrastructure, software licences, data, skills), including allocating internal resources (existing servers, team members).","Build: coding and configuration (software), assembly/configuration (hardware, infrastructure), or organizational build (a new support team or process).","Operate: behind-the-scenes technical operation (servers, networks, job scheduling, certificate and licence renewals). Direct user support belongs to 'deliver' and 'support'. Operated products enable delivery, reported deviations trigger 'support'.","Deliver: where the provider meets the user in real time. Ensures service levels (warranty) are met in each delivery, which ties into SLA management.","Support: triggered by a deviation from expected service (an incident) or a user inquiry showing dissatisfaction or confusion. Includes the service desk, incident management (restoring service), problem management (root causes of recurring issues), and serious disruptions."]},
{m:"m3",h:"4 The ITIL Value System (ITIL VS)",p:["A value system is a model that shows how all the components and activities of an organization work together to facilitate value creation through digital products and services. The ITIL VS ensures that everything the organization does is integrated and coordinated to co-create value with stakeholders.","Figure 1.7: opportunity/demand -> guiding principles, governance, value chain (discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support), practices, continual improvement -> value."]},
{m:"m3",h:"Figure 0.2 The key models of ITIL (Version 5): the big picture",p:["The ITIL Guiding Principles help make decisions at all levels and in all circumstances."],li:["1. All organizations are engaged in service relationships.","2. In service relationships, every organization takes responsibility for all or some stages of the product and service lifecycle.","3. Each organization has a value system enabling value co-creation.","4. The central component of the value system is a value chain: activities the organization performs to manage products and services.","5. The value chain is enabled and supported by the management practices.","6. Management practices integrate resources from the four dimensions to form the management capabilities required for effective product and service management within an organization."]},
{m:"m3",h:"4.1-4.1.1 The five components of the ITIL VS",p:["The five components are adaptable rather than fixed. The ITIL VS promotes flexibility and discourages isolated working practices.","Together: the guiding principles permeate and guide all aspects, governance oversees and directs the whole system, the value chain provides the operating model, management practices supply the know-how and tools, and continual improvement wraps around them."],d:[["Guiding principles","recommendations that guide an organization in all circumstances. Universal truths, followed regardless of changes in goals, strategies, or management structure, that help practitioners make good decisions at every level."],["Governance","the system by which the organization is directed and controlled. Keeps operations aligned with strategic goals and compliant with policies and regulations, providing direction and oversight. Defines who is accountable and how accountability is exercised."],["Value chain","the central element: the key activities required to respond to demand and facilitate value realization through products and services, an operating model covering end-to-end development and delivery. Each activity transforms inputs into outputs. Activities are combined into specific value streams for different scenarios."],["Management practices","sets of organizational resources and capabilities designed and adopted for performing work or accomplishing an objective. 34 practices, categorized as general management or product and service management practices. Adaptable capabilities, not rigid processes, that support the value chain activities with resources (people, processes, tools, knowledge)."],["Continual improvement","an ongoing activity embedded in all levels of the ITIL VS, both an organizational mindset and a set of practices. Acts upon feedback and performance information to drive adjustments in any part of the value system."]]},
{m:"m3",h:"4.1.2 Purpose of the ITIL VS",li:["Gives an overarching view of how an organization converts opportunity and demand into value.","Ensures decision-making, governance, and day-to-day activities are aligned and work together as a system rather than as disconnected parts.","Links strategic direction with operational execution, balancing stability and innovation while responding to change.","Value creation comes from the interaction of practices and activities within a coherent system, not from them in isolation.","Supports consistency across the organization while allowing parts of the system to be applied and adapted to context."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2-4.2.1 The seven ITIL Guiding Principles",p:["Recommendations that can guide an organization in all circumstances, regardless of changes in its goals, strategies, type of work, or management structure. Universal and enduring: they can be applied in any initiative, by any person, at any level of the organization. They also embody the core messages of ITIL and product and service management best practices."],li:["Focus on value","Start where you are","Progress iteratively with feedback","Collaborate and promote visibility","Think and work holistically","Keep it simple and practical","Optimize and automate"]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.2 Focus on value",li:["Everything the organization does should ultimately link back to value for the organization itself, its customers, or other stakeholders.","Understand the consumer's perspective and know why an action is taken and for whom.","The provider's internal goals must align with delivering benefits to the customer or end-user.","In practice: map customer journeys, solicit feedback on value perceptions, and make value outcomes (not just technical outputs) the key measure of success.","Consider value for the organization and other stakeholders (shareholders, regulators) without losing sight that services exist to facilitate customer value."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.3 Start where you are",li:["Avoid wasting what can still be useful: it is rarely necessary to rebuild from scratch.","Understand where you are now (metrics, audit of processes) to find what can be re-used or built upon. This saves time and preserves knowledge.","Incremental improvement: use the existing baseline to measure progress and retain good practices.","Use direct observation of how work is done, not only reports (reports can be biased or outdated)."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.4 Progress iteratively with feedback",li:["Do not attempt to do everything at once: break work into manageable segments, deliver in small increments, learn from each iteration, and adjust based on feedback.","About agility and continual learning, aligned with agile methods and continual improvement cycles.","'With feedback' is crucial: each iteration seeks feedback from stakeholders or metrics, so the next one is guided by real experience and data. This prevents drifting off course or over-engineering.","Small wins keep stakeholders engaged and teams motivated.","Nearly any initiative can be structured into iterations (size and duration vary with context), and feedback loops should be built into all activities."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.5 Collaborate and promote visibility",li:["Collaboration: working together across boundaries towards shared goals, involving the right people in the right roles.","Visibility: making work, progress, and information transparent to all stakeholders.","Addresses the dangers of silos and hidden agendas: break down the 'silo mentality' with a culture of openness.","Examples: cross-functional teams, regular status sharing, open documentation, inclusive governance.","Helps catch issues early (more eyes see problems) and use collective talents."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.6 Think and work holistically",li:["See the big picture and work in a way that considers the system as a whole, not just siloed parts.","Outcomes come from interconnected processes, teams, infrastructure, and partners, not from isolated actions.","'Think holistically': consider the end-to-end service and all its components and contexts. 'Work holistically': coordinate across all areas so the four dimensions (people, technology, partners, processes) are aligned.","Solutions and improvements must account for interdependencies.","In practice: systems thinking (cause-effect in systems) and representation from all stakeholder groups when designing or changing a service."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.7 Keep it simple and practical",li:["Use the minimum number of steps or the simplest approach needed to accomplish a task and ensure value.","If a process, measurement, or tool does not provide value or is unnecessarily complex, eliminate or simplify it.","Simplicity reduces chances of failure, makes things easier to understand, and often speeds up execution.","Do not add activities, processes, or bureaucratic controls unless there is a clear business need.","Practicality: solutions must work in the real world. A theoretically elegant model that cannot be applied is not useful."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.8 Optimize and automate",li:["Technology helps organizations scale up and take on more tasks, freeing people for more complex decision-making.","The use of technology, especially generative AI, should be subject to governance, ethical, and compliance policies and controls.","Automation for automation's sake can increase costs, introduce significant risks, and reduce organizational resilience.","Streamline and simplify the process or service before automating: there is little point in automating a flawed process."]},
{m:"m5",h:"4.2.9 How the guiding principles interact",li:["The principles interact with and depend upon each other.","Examples: progressing iteratively also needs holistic thinking, so each iteration includes everything needed for real results. Feedback is key to collaboration. Focusing on what is truly valuable to the customer makes it easier to keep things simple and practical.","Do not use just one or two: consider the relevance of each and how they complement each other.","No particular order or hierarchy: they are equally important, although in a given situation some may be more critical than others."]},
{m:"m4",h:"4.3-4.3.1 Governance",p:["In the ITIL VS, governance keeps the organization as a whole on course toward its strategic goals and obligations while co-creating value. Each organization's approach is shaped by its values, culture, business context, and other factors."],d:[["Governance","the system by which an organization is directed and controlled."],["Governance of digital technology","a human-based system by which the current and future use of digital technology is governed. The system comprises directing, overseeing and accountability."]]},
{m:"m4",h:"4.3.2 The four governance activities",p:["These four activities realize governance and keep the value system aligned with the organization's objectives and obligations."],d:[["Engage stakeholders","the governing body identifies internal and external stakeholders and makes sure their needs and feedback are understood and factored into high-level decision-making. It often formally commits to values or objectives on behalf of stakeholders and ensures mechanisms exist to honor those commitments."],["Evaluate","the governing body regularly evaluates the organization's performance, its environment, and its strategy: the current state of portfolios (products, services, projects), external factors (market changes, new regulations, emerging risks, new opportunities), and stakeholder feedback."],["Direct","based on evaluation, the governing body sets or adjusts the direction: strategic decisions, high-level policies, and assigning responsibility for carrying out strategy (delegating to executives and management). It ensures a clear vision and that management understands the strategic objectives and constraints (compliance requirements, risk appetite, quality standards)."],["Monitor","the governing body monitors performance and compliance against the set direction and policies: regular reports, Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) and Key Risk Indicators (KRIs), outcomes of audits or quality checks. If monitoring reveals deviations, governance must respond."]]},
{m:"m6",h:"4.4-4.4.1 Value chain",p:["The core element of an organization's operating model: the key activities needed to transform demand or opportunity into value in the form of products and services.","A structured but flexible blueprint for creating, delivering, and supporting products and services. Each activity turns inputs into outputs that feed later activities or directly deliver value to consumers.","Figure 4.2: the eight lifecycle management activities shown as a chain of stepping stones."],d:[["Value chain","an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service."]]},
{m:"m6x",h:"4.4.2-4.4.4 Specification, prototype, incident, event, release, testing",d:[["Product/service specification","a detailed document that outlines critical aspects, requirements, and characteristics of a product or service to be built. It typically includes descriptions of the product's or service's features, functionalities, technical requirements, performance criteria, and user interface details."],["Product/service prototype","an initial version of a product or service that demonstrates its basic form, functionality, and operational capabilities. It is used to test and refine the product's or service's design and functionality and test hypotheses. Prototypes vary in complexity and purpose, ranging from visual mock-ups to fully functional models."],["Incident","an unplanned interruption to a service or reduction in the quality of a service."],["Event","any change of state that has significance for the management of a product, service, or other configuration item."],["Release","a version of a product, service, or other configuration item, or a collection of configuration items, made available for use."],["Testing","the activity in which a system or component is executed under specified conditions, the results are observed or recorded, and an evaluation of some aspect or component of the system is made."]]},
{m:"m6x",h:"4.4.5-4.4.6 Continuous integration, delivery, deployment, reliability, SRE, observability",d:[["Continuous integration","a set of techniques and tools that enables developers to frequently merge their code changes into a central repository, followed by automated builds and tests."],["Continuous delivery","a set of techniques and tools that enables software updates to be deployed to production at any time. Frequent deployments are possible, but deployment decisions are taken case by case, usually because organizations prefer a slower rate of deployment."],["Continuous deployment","a set of techniques and tools that enables every change that passes automated tests to be automatically deployed to production without additional authorization. Continuous deployment relies on continuous delivery."],["Reliability","the ability of a product, service, or other configuration item to perform its intended function for a specified period of time or number of cycles."],["Site Reliability Engineering (SRE)","a discipline that incorporates aspects of software engineering and applies them to infrastructure and operations problems. The main goal is to create scalable and highly reliable software systems."],["Observability","the ability to understand the internal state of a complicated system by analysing its external outputs, such as metrics, logs, and traces."]]},
{m:"m6x",h:"4.4.7-4.4.8 Service request, disaster, problem, error, known error",d:[["Service request","a request from a user or a user's authorized representative that initiates a service action which has been agreed as a normal part of service delivery."],["Disaster","a sudden unplanned event that causes great damage or serious loss to an organization. A disaster results in an organization failing to provide critical business functions for some predetermined minimum period of time."],["Problem","a cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents."],["Error","a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents."],["Known error","a problem that has been analysed but has not been resolved."]]},
{m:"m6",h:"4.4.9 Operating model",d:[["Operating model","a conceptual and/or visual representation of how an organization co-creates value with its customers and other stakeholders, as well as how the organization runs itself."]]},
{m:"m6x",h:"4.4.10 Problem vs error vs known error",p:["Successive levels of understanding about the underlying causes of incidents, guiding how the organization responds."],li:["Problem: incidents are occurring and the root cause has not yet been fully identified or confirmed. Focus on investigation and analysis to prevent recurrence, not only on immediate restoration.","Error: analysis has determined the specific cause. Shift from investigation to control: the organization knows what is wrong, even if it has not yet decided how or when to fix it permanently.","Known error: the cause has been analysed and formally recorded (but not resolved), often with an effective workaround. Incidents are handled more efficiently while a permanent solution is assessed, prioritized, and implemented through change enablement if justified."]},
{m:"m6x",h:"4.4.11 Key success metrics of the value chain activities",p:["Each activity has specific key success metrics to measure performance, find improvement opportunities, and confirm its contribution to value co-creation."],tb:{head:["Activity","Success means","Key metrics"],rows:[["Discover","strategic alignment of products and service offerings, market relevance, positive stakeholder perceptions","strategic fit of offerings, market relevance, stakeholder satisfaction, commercial success (where applicable)"],["Design","solutions that meet requirements for utility, warranty, and innovation","quality of specifications and prototypes, design cycle time, adherence to the product and service roadmap and guidelines, stakeholder satisfaction with design"],["Acquire","timely and effective procurement of resources","quality of acquired resources and services, acquire cycle time, adherence to the roadmap and guidelines, stakeholder satisfaction with the activity, timelines, and acquired resources"],["Build","quality and timeliness of the developed solutions","quality of product and service solutions, build cycle, adherence to design specifications and roadmaps, stakeholder satisfaction with the solutions"],["Transition","products and services introduced with minimal disruption","transition cycle (timeliness and duration), negative impact on availability and performance, number and impact of transition errors, adherence to the roadmap and guidelines, stakeholder satisfaction with the transition"],["Operate","reliable, secure, and high-performing digital products and services","coverage and effectiveness of monitoring and analysis, live reliability, performance, and information security, number and impact of incidents and performance deviations, adherence to the roadmap and guidelines, stakeholder satisfaction with operation"],["Deliver","user experience and fulfilment of service actions","customer and user satisfaction with quality, service performance against agreed SLA targets, adherence to the product and service strategy and guidelines, stakeholder satisfaction with delivery"],["Support","how effectively issues are resolved and service is restored","customer and user satisfaction with support, speed of restoring normal operation and delivery, negative impact of incidents and disasters, performance against agreed SLAs, adherence to the product and service strategy and guidelines"]]}},
{m:"m6",h:"4.4.12 Purpose, operating model, value chain, and practices",li:["Purpose: what the organization does for its consumers and stakeholders and why it does it.","Operating model: a conceptual or visual representation of how value is co-created with customers and how the organization operates internally. In ITIL, operating models are structured around the four dimensions.","At the highest level, the value streams and processes dimension can be seen as the organization's value chain: the entire set of activities it undertakes to fulfil its purpose and create value (discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support).","Each value chain activity is supported and enabled by several management practices, which together manage digital products and services throughout their lifecycle (Figure 1.9).","Figure 5.3: the organization's purpose defines the operating model(s), which include value chain activities, which form core value streams, which depend on management practices. Management practices enable the value streams and value chain activities."]},
{m:"m6",h:"4.5-4.5.2 Management practices and the two practice groups",p:["ITIL has 34 management practices, split in two groups."],d:[["Management practice","a set of organizational resources and capabilities designed and adopted for performing work or accomplishing an objective."],["Product and service management practices","specific to digital product and service management. They are key to the product and service management activities of organizations that manage their digital products and services in line with ITIL's Lifecycle Model."],["General management practices","can be applied to any product or service, as well as to the general management activities of an organization."]]},
{m:"m6",h:"Table 5.3 The 34 ITIL management practices",tb:{head:["Product and service management (22)","General management (12)"],rows:[["Availability management","Architecture management"],["Business analysis","Continual improvement"],["Capacity and performance management","Knowledge management"],["Change enablement","Measurement and reporting"],["Deployment management","Organizational change management"],["Incident management","Portfolio management"],["Information security management","Project management"],["Infrastructure and platform management","Relationship management"],["IT asset management","Risk management"],["Monitoring and event management","Strategy management"],["Problem management","Supplier management"],["Release management","Workforce and talent management"],["Service catalogue management",""],["Service configuration management",""],["Service continuity management",""],["Service design",""],["Service desk",""],["Service financial management",""],["Service level management",""],["Service request management",""],["Service validation and testing",""],["Software development and management",""]]}},
{m:"m6",h:"4.5.3 Role of the management practices in the ITIL VS",li:["Practices support and enable value chain activities by providing the specialized knowledge, capabilities, and resources needed to perform work effectively.","Whenever a value chain activity is carried out, relevant practices are applied so it is executed in a consistent, controlled, and value-focused manner.","Practices do not function independently or follow a fixed sequence: they are used flexibly across multiple activities, depending on needs and context.","Integrated with governance, the guiding principles, and continual improvement, they keep operational and improvement work aligned with strategy and focused on value.","Two levels of involvement: enabling (E) = directly involved in the activity. Supporting (S) = not directly involved, but supports it with information and methods.","Example: 'design' is enabled by 17 practices (including service design, architecture management, measurement and reporting) and supported by seven (including IT asset management, knowledge management)."]},
{m:"m6",h:"4.5.4 Structure of the ITIL Practice Guides (Table 5.4)",p:["Every ITIL Practice Guide follows the same structure."],tb:{head:["Chapter","Contents"],rows:[["General information","Purpose and description · Key terms and concepts · Scope · Practice success factors · Key metrics"],["Value streams and processes","The processes and activities of the practice · The contribution of the practice to service value streams"],["Organizations and people","Roles, competencies, and responsibilities · Organizational solutions and teams (specific to the practice)"],["Information and technology","Key information used by the practice · Automation and tooling, including recommendations for successful automation of the practice"],["Partners and suppliers","Dependencies of the practice on third parties · Support from third parties"],["Capability assessment and development","Description of the capability levels according to the ITIL Maturity Model · Capability criteria for the practice · Recommendations for self-assessment of the practice capability level"],["Recommendations for the practice success","Recommendations for successful development and application of the practice, mapped to the ITIL Guiding Principles"]]}},
{m:"m6",h:"4.5.5 Benefits of the ITIL Practice Guides",li:["Practical, detailed guidance on applying management practices in real organizational contexts.","Consistent and effective implementation through clear purpose statements, key concepts, and actionable recommendations that go beyond high-level theory.","Modular and adaptable: organizations adopt and tailor practices to their specific needs, maturity, and environment.","Help improve service performance, reduce risk, and support continual improvement, aligned with the ITIL VS and guiding principles."]},
{m:"m6",h:"4.5.6 Metric and Critical Success Factor (CSF)",d:[["Metric","a measurement or calculation that is monitored or reported for management and improvement."],["Critical Success Factor (CSF)","a necessary precondition for the achievement of intended results."]]},
{m:"m8",h:"4.6-4.6.1 Steps of the ITIL Continual Improvement Model",p:["Continual improvement is a core element of ITIL and one of the five components of the ITIL VS."],li:["1. What is the vision?","2. Where are we now?","3. Where do we want to be?","4. How do we get there?","5. Take action.","6. Are we getting there?","7. How do we keep the improvements relevant?"]},
{m:"m8",h:"4.6.2 What happens in each step",d:[["1. What is the vision?","define the high-level vision and objectives for improvement, aligned with business strategy. (Figure 5.4: improvement vision aligned with business goals and objectives)"],["2. Where are we now?","assess the current state: a baseline of current performance, current process capability, measuring and understanding the present situation realistically. (Baseline assessment)"],["3. Where do we want to be?","define the target state in measurable terms. Set specific goals or KPIs for improvement. (Next target state)"],["4. How do we get there?","plan the improvement initiatives: brainstorm solutions, evaluate options, create an improvement plan or roadmap. (Route to the next target state)"],["5. Take action","implement the improvement plan, as a project or as changes executed through the normal value chain. Follow the plan and manage change properly. (Improvement actions and experiments)"],["6. Are we getting there?","evaluate whether the improvement achieved the desired results: measure the new state and compare it to the targets set in step 3. If positive, verify by metrics (maybe celebrate). If not fully achieved, analyse why. (New state assessment)"],["7. How do we keep the improvements relevant?","sustain the improvements and look for the next opportunities: embed the change in the culture or processes so it does not regress, then start the cycle anew. Prevents one-off efforts and fosters a culture of continuous improvement."]]},
{m:"m8",h:"4.6.3 Continual improvement in the ITIL VS",li:["Both a structured organizational activity and a mindset, embedded across all VS components.","A recurring activity at all levels: not confined to one team or initiative, it applies to all services, processes, practices, and capabilities, with contributions from every level.","The model is a high-level guide. Using it increases the likelihood of success by aligning improvements with stakeholder value and the strategic vision.","Iterative: work is divided into manageable steps, each with a specific objective achieved incrementally. Scope and detail of each step vary with the improvement.","Usable as a structured workflow or a conceptual framework. Steps are not strictly linear: earlier stages are often revisited as new information emerges.","Sometimes current and target states are clear, allowing a planned path. Sometimes only the need for change is apparent and the direction must be discovered through experimentation."]},
{m:"m7",h:"5.1.1-5.1.2 Value streams, mapping, and management",p:["Value stream mapping and management means identifying how value flows through the organization, visualizing it, analysing it for bottlenecks or waste, and then managing (improving) those flows continuously. Value streams focus on end-to-end workflows that deliver value outcomes."],d:[["Value stream","a series of steps that an organization uses to create and deliver products and services to a service consumer."],["Core value stream","a value stream that enables value for consumers in a form intended by the organization's operating model."],["Enabling value stream","a value stream that enables value for internal customers to support the organization's core value streams."],["Value stream mapping","a technique for the visual representation and analysis of value streams."],["Value stream management","the practice of overseeing value streams on a continual basis."]]},
{m:"m7",h:"5.1.3 Complexity thinking",d:[["Complexity thinking","an approach to analysis and decision-making based on the recognition and understanding of the various levels of complexity inherent in the systems and the context in which they operate."]]},
{m:"m7",h:"5.1.4 Core vs enabling value streams",li:["The difference lies in their direct contribution to value creation.","Core value streams: the primary activities that create and deliver value to customers and users, transforming demand into outcomes that directly realize value.","Enabling value streams: provide the capabilities, resources, and controls that core value streams need. They do not usually deliver value directly to the customer, but they are essential for core value streams to operate effectively, reliably, and at scale."]},
{m:"m7",h:"5.2-5.2.1 Optimizing workflows for complexity",p:["Application of value streams: using them in practice to design, organize, and improve workflows as effective end-to-end ways of working, particularly in complex and changing environments."],li:["In complex, fast-changing environments, workflows cannot rely on rigid, linear processes.","Design value streams that are flexible, adaptable, and focused on outcomes rather than strict process compliance.","Reduce unnecessary handoffs and dependencies, enable fast feedback, and let people adjust activities to context.","ITIL describes four broad categories of context, each requiring different approaches to decision-making and action (Figure 2.1, inspired by Cynefin):"],tb:{head:["Context","Description"],rows:[["Ordered","Clear causation"],["Complex","Understandable in retrospect but unpredictable"],["Chaotic","Crisis situation requiring immediate action"],["Confused","Unclear context requiring orientation before action"]]}},
{m:"m7",h:"5.3-5.3.1 Purpose of value stream mapping and management",p:["Purpose: to enable organizations to optimize their workflows for efficiency and effectiveness, ensuring that each step in the stream contributes to value and that delays, waste, or friction are minimized."],li:["Value stream mapping: a technique for visual representation and analysis of a value stream. A map is typically a diagram of each step, inputs and outputs, decision points, and loops, with metrics (time per step, queue lengths, handoff points). It often distinguishes value-adding steps from non-value-adding steps (waste).","Value stream management: the ongoing practice of overseeing value streams so they are continually improved and deliver maximum value. It uses the mapping insights to monitor performance, manage work-in-progress, and coordinate across teams to keep the stream flowing."]},
{m:"m7",h:"5.3.2 Mapping vs management",li:["Complementary roles in continual improvement.","Mapping is the starting point: an analytical activity to visualize workflows, identify inefficiencies, and propose enhancements. Typically a discrete effort (workshop or project) giving a static picture at a point in time. A diagnostic tool that does not produce lasting results alone.","Management is the ongoing application of that insight in regular operations: continuously monitoring value stream performance (lead times, throughput, quality) and initiating further changes.","Mapping enables planning, management ensures execution and sustainability.","Mapping treated as a one-time initiative without integration into management practices has limited impact. Sustainable improvement needs value stream management embedded across the ITIL VS, with ongoing feedback and measurement."]},
{m:"m7",h:"5.3.3 Elements of a value stream map",li:["Captures how work and information flow through a value stream from initiation to customer-facing outcomes, showing how value is created in practice rather than how it was designed to work.","Flow of activities, the teams or roles involved, and the handoffs and dependencies between steps.","Time data: cycle time for individual activities, wait time between steps, and end-to-end lead time and flow efficiency.","Contextual and analytical information: whether activities add value, support value creation, coordinate work, or do not add value from the consumer's perspective.","Used to visualize the current ('as-is') state, identify bottlenecks and quality issues, and design improved future ('to-be') value streams."]},
{m:"m9",h:"6.1-6.1.1 AI, AI maturity, GenAI, Agentic AI",p:["ITIL and AI complement each other: AI improves decision-making, efficiency, and service quality, while ITIL provides the governance, value focus, and ethical framework so AI is applied responsibly."],d:[["Artificial Intelligence (AI)","the capability of digital systems to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as learning from data, recognizing patterns, making predictions, or supporting decision-making."],["AI maturity","the extent to which an organization is able to effectively, responsibly, and consistently use AI capabilities to support its objectives, including governance, data quality, skills, processes, and ethical considerations."],["Generative AI (GenAI)","AI systems that can create new content, such as text, images, code, or other outputs, based on patterns learned from existing data."],["Agentic AI","AI systems that are designed to act on behalf of users by autonomously performing predefined tasks within agreed boundaries, while remaining subject to human oversight and governance."]]},
{m:"m9",h:"6.1.2 AI across the lifecycle activities",p:["Across the lifecycle, AI facilitates continuous feedback, reduces manual effort, and drives data-informed improvement, while remaining aligned with governance, stakeholder value, and the ITIL Guiding Principles."],tb:{head:["Activities","How AI assists"],rows:[["Discover, design","automates the analysis of large datasets (customer feedback, usage metrics, market trends) to identify needs and inform design decisions"],["Acquire, build","supports automation of code generation, testing, and resource planning, accelerating development with better accuracy and efficiency"],["Transition into operate","automated deployment planning, real-time monitoring, and predictive analysis to respond to risks before they impact service quality"],["Deliver, support","AI-driven automation (chatbots, self-healing systems, intelligent ticket routing, anomaly detection) improves responsiveness and consistency"]]}},
{m:"m9",h:"6.1.3 Purpose of AI in digital product and service management",p:["Purpose: to enhance human decision-making and management activities by providing deeper insights and automating routine tasks."],li:["AI learns from data, adapts to new inputs, and supports activities that traditionally require human involvement: better quality of work, more informed decisions, more efficient and effective products, services, and processes.","Used for documentation, knowledge management, user support, monitoring, analysis, and process optimization.","Faster access to information, pattern identification, and automation of repetitive tasks help organizations respond to change and manage uncertainty and complexity."]},
{m:"m9",h:"6.2-6.2.1 ITIL AI governance",p:["ITIL AI governance ensures AI is used in a controlled, ethical, and value-driven manner: aligning AI-enabled solutions with organizational objectives, managing risks, ensuring accountability, and maintaining transparency, so AI use supports trust, compliance, and sustainable value creation."],d:[["AI governance","the system by which an organization directs, controls, and monitors the use of artificial intelligence to ensure that AI-enabled digital products and services create value while remaining ethical, secure, compliant, and aligned with organizational objectives."]]},
{m:"m9",h:"6.2.2 The ITIL AI Capability Model",p:["A structured way to govern the use of AI across products and services, so AI-enabled solutions create value while managing risk and maintaining trust. AI governance is not only a technical concern but an organizational responsibility spanning strategy, operations, and stakeholder relationships.","Figure 2.2: six capabilities around AI applications: creation, curation, clarification, cognition, communication, coordination."],li:["The capabilities focus on AI use that is aligned with organizational objectives, operates within appropriate controls, complies with regulatory and ethical requirements, and remains transparent and explainable to stakeholders.","Competence and capability: people need the skills to design, manage, and oversee AI.","Continuous oversight and improvement: monitor performance, bias, and unintended consequences over time.","Applying the model embeds AI governance into the existing value system, so AI supports value co-creation, reinforces trust, and is used responsibly throughout the product and service lifecycle."]},
{m:"m9",h:"Table 2.1 The six AI capabilities",tb:{head:["Capability","Description","Examples"],rows:[["1. Creation","AI generates net-new outputs in response to prompts or triggers. This includes producing content, code, documentation, or any other artifacts that did not exist before.","answers to users' questions, individualized texts for communications, release notes, change announcements and deployment plans from structured release data, drafting service descriptions or SLAs, generating a workflow from defined conditions or business rules"],["2. Curation","AI improves the quality, organization, and relevance of existing data or knowledge by identifying redundancies, outdated information, inconsistencies, or incompliance.","flagging obsolete knowledge base articles, cleaning up duplicated records, highlighting discrepancies between SLAs and supplier contracts, validating documents against approved corporate policies and rules"],["3. Clarification","AI helps users find, understand, navigate, or improve existing content by summarizing, rephrasing, restructuring, or translating it.","summarizing a complex incident ticket for management reporting, rewriting a knowledge article for easier understanding, a context-specific summary report from a natural language prompt, AI-enhanced classification and attribution of IT costs"],["4. Cognition","AI identifies patterns, anomalies, or hidden insights across data, enabling proactive detection, forecasting, and analysis.","analysing a major incident, detecting recurring incident trends indicating a deeper problem, identifying bottlenecks in a process or value stream, identifying potential issues caused by changes, predicting workload increases from historical usage and seasonal trends"],["5. Communication","AI acts as a communicative interface helping users interact with services and systems naturally.","chatbots for technical support, virtual agents for service request submission, personalized major incident communication, AI agents that run surveys, capture feedback, and apply sentiment analysis"],["6. Coordination","AI autonomously executes, orchestrates, or triggers actions across systems, often in response to events, requests, or patterns.","triaging incoming user requests and assigning them to the right teams, automatically escalating high-impact incidents, triggering remediation workflows after detecting specific errors, events, or trends"]]}},
{m:"m10",h:"7 ITIL and other frameworks",p:["No single framework is sufficient for the complexity of modern business and technology environments. PRINCE2 and DEVOPS INSTITUTE certifications extend ITIL by supporting structured change delivery and agile, continuous product development and operation, with a shared focus on value, collaboration, and continual improvement."],li:["Figure 7.1: ITIL = digital products and service management. PRINCE2 = management of projects and programmes. DEVOPS INSTITUTE = digital product development and operations.","ITIL + PRINCE2 overlap: effective management of digital product and service lifecycle.","ITIL + DevOps overlap: end-to-end management of digital product and service lifecycle.","PRINCE2 + DevOps overlap: effective management of changes in digital products and organizations.","All three: ongoing co-creation of value in VUCA environments."]},
{m:"m10",h:"7.1-7.1.2 ITIL and DevOps",li:["ITIL: common language, governance, and a value-focused management framework that defines value, governance, roles, and responsibilities across the lifecycle.","DevOps: practices emphasizing automation, continuous integration and delivery, and close collaboration between development and operations, enabling rapid development, frequent deployment, and reliable operation.","ITIL sets clear expectations for value, quality, risk, and compliance. DevOps enables teams to meet those expectations efficiently.","DevOps practices accelerate value chain activities, ITIL practices ensure stability, accountability, and continual improvement.","Across 'discover', 'design', 'build', 'transition', and 'operate': DevOps brings fast feedback, automation, and continuous improvement, ITIL brings consistency, risk management, and integration across the organization.","Together: deliver change rapidly without sacrificing reliability, security, or customer experience, with products and services that are both agile and resilient."]},
{m:"m10",h:"7.2-7.2.1 ITIL and PRINCE2: why project management matters",p:["ITIL focuses on the ongoing management and improvement of digital products and services. PRINCE2 provides structured governance and control for projects that introduce change. Together they manage the transition from project delivery to stable, value-driven service operation."],li:["Many changes to products and services are delivered through projects rather than routine operational work.","Project management provides the structure to plan, control, and deliver significant changes within defined constraints such as time, cost, scope, and risk.","New or changed products and services are designed, built, and transitioned in a controlled, coordinated way before becoming business as usual.","It aligns change initiatives with strategy, manages stakeholder expectations, and reduces the risk of disruption to live services."]},
{m:"m10",h:"7.2.2 ITIL and PRINCE2 are complementary",li:["ITIL: how products and services are managed, operated, and improved within the value system. PRINCE2: a comprehensive, structured approach for managing the projects, programmes, and portfolios that introduce change to them.","ITIL includes Official Practice Guides for project management and portfolio management, but for complex change initiatives PRINCE2 offers a deeper and more detailed body of knowledge.","PRINCE2 Project Management: a clear, structured method for delivering projects. PRINCE2 Agile: combines this structure with agile techniques for iterative, adaptive delivery.","PRINCE2 focuses on how change is delivered, ITIL ensures the outcomes of that change are managed as services that create value over time."]},
{m:"m1",h:"What changed: scope of ITIL",li:["Fundamental ITIL concepts and principles remain the same, slightly adjusted or simply repositioned in this new context.","QRG vs ITIL 4: 'service management' is now 'digital product and service management': capabilities for enabling value for customers in the form of digital products and services."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["IT Service Management (management of the full lifecycle of IT services) and Digital Transformation.","ITSM and Service Management (including Enterprise Service Management), expanded to include Digital Product and Service Management, covering the full lifecycle of digital products and services."]]}},
{m:"m1",h:"What changed: digital products and services, service interactions",li:["Products and services: definitions unchanged (products provide capabilities, services enable value co-creation based on these capabilities).","New: specific definitions of digital product and digital service, derived from the product and service definitions (see 1.1.3).","'Digital products and services' covers the full spectrum of digital relevance: non-digital products enabled or supported by digital technology (a pen designed or produced digitally) -> technology-enabled products (a smartphone with its OS and apps) -> fully digital products (software).","Digital services are delivered through, and fundamentally dependent on, digital products, so both must be approached holistically.","Service interactions (access to resources, transfer of goods, service actions): no changes, but the key role of access to resources for digital services is emphasized.","QRG vs ITIL 4: the service definition now reads 'outcomes that consumers want to achieve, without the consumer having to manage specific costs and risks' (ITIL 4 said customers)."]},
{m:"m2",h:"What changed: key roles in value co-creation",li:["The roles of product vendor and service provider can be played by one organization or by different organizations.","Unchanged: service consumer roles (sponsor, customer, user) and types of service relationships (basic, cooperative, collaborative (partnership)).","QRG vs ITIL 4: service provider and service consumer are now organizations 'responsible for delivery and support' and 'responsible for procurement and use' of services. Sponsor, customer, and user are each 'the role that...' (ITIL 4: 'a person who...'), and the customer defines requirements for products and services."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["Service providers and service consumers.","Product vendors, service providers, service consumers."]]}},
{m:"m2",h:"What changed: service quality characteristics",li:["Sustainability is added as a key characteristic of service quality (definition in 1.1.7)."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["Utility, warranty, experience.","Utility, warranty, experience, sustainability."]]}},
{m:"m4",h:"What changed: governance",li:["The description is updated to reflect ISO/IEC 38500:2024 (Information technology, Governance of IT for the organization).","The QRG lists the four activities in this order: engage stakeholders, evaluate, direct, monitor.","QRG vs ITIL 4: governance is 'the system by which an organization is directed and controlled' (ITIL 4: 'the means by which'). Governance of digital technology is also defined (4.3.1)."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["Evaluate, direct, monitor.","Evaluate, direct, monitor, engage stakeholders."]]}},
{m:"m9",h:"What changed: the four dimensions",li:["Renamed: 'of service management' -> 'of Product and Service Management'.","Descriptions reviewed.","The ITIL AI Capability Model is added to the 'Information and technology' dimension."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["The ITIL four dimensions of service management, external PESTLE factors.","The ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management, external PESTLE factors."]]}},
{m:"m5",h:"What changed: guiding principles",li:["Still the same seven guiding principles.","Descriptions are clarified.","AI is added to the 'how to apply the principle' sections (for example, 'optimize and automate': the use of technology, especially generative AI, is subject to governance, ethical, and compliance policies and controls)."]},
{m:"m6",h:"What changed: management practices",li:["All 34 ITIL Management Practices remain unchanged and stay at the core of the framework as essential value enablers.","Foundation focus has shifted away from memorizing knowledge of individual practices towards understanding their purpose, structure, benefits, and role within the ITIL Value System.","The 34 ITIL Official Practice Guides are aligned with the new ITIL (minor updates expected) and integrated across the new ITIL modules.","QRG vs ITIL 4: the three technical practices (deployment, infrastructure and platform, software development and management) plus information security management and service financial management (both general in ITIL 4) are now product and service management practices: 22 vs 12 general.","QRG vs ITIL 4: the practice called change control in the original 2019 ITIL 4 Foundation is change enablement."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["34 practices grouped in three groups: general, service, and technical.","Same 34 practices grouped in two groups: general, and 'product and service management practices'."]]}},
{m:"m7",h:"What changed: value streams",li:["Examinable in V5 (section 5): core vs enabling value streams, value stream mapping vs value stream management, complexity thinking and the four complexity contexts."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["Basic definitions.","Clarified definitions, better link to the value chain, value stream mapping recommendations (Appendix E of the Official Book)."]]}},
{m:"m6",h:"What changed: new ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model",li:["ITIL 4: N/A. New in ITIL (Version 5).","The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model unifies digital product and service in one model.","Lifecycle management activities: discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support.","To manage digital products and services throughout their shared lifecycle, organizations perform the lifecycle management activities.","The activities enable and support the lifecycle but are not performed in a fixed sequence: this varies with the product and service architecture and the organizational context.","They are combined according to the context to form value chains."]},
{m:"m6",h:"What changed: value chain replaces the service value chain",li:["Definition: a value chain is an entire set of activities that enables value through the provision of a product or service.","The activities are linked to the lifecycle management activities of the Product and Service Lifecycle Model and presented as stepping stones, with all or some of them forming the organization's value chain.","Each value chain activity is described in a uniform way and mapped to enabling and supporting practices.","QRG vs ITIL 4: plan, engage, and improve are no longer value chain activities. 'Engage stakeholders' is now a governance activity, and improvement sits in the continual improvement component."],tb:{head:["Aspect","ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["Name","Service value chain, at the centre of the ITIL Service Value System","Value chain"],["Activities","Plan, engage, design and transition, obtain/build, deliver and support, improve","Discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, support (as in the ITIL Lifecycle Model)"]]}},
{m:"m3",h:"What changed: ITIL Value System",li:["No structural changes.","Service value chain is now value chain.","This strengthens the ITIL Value System with a clearer modular value chain model."],tb:{head:["ITIL 4: ITIL Service Value System","ITIL (Version 5): ITIL Value System"],rows:[["1. Guiding Principles","1. ITIL Guiding Principles"],["2. Governance","2. Governance"],["3. (Service) Value Chain","3. ITIL Value Chain"],["4. Practices","4. Management Practices"],["5. Continual Improvement","5. The ITIL Continual Improvement Model"]]}},
{m:"m8",h:"What changed: Continual Improvement Model",li:["Only steps 6 and 7 are renamed.","All steps are described from both 'clear' and 'discovery' perspectives, stressing the importance of experimentation when the destination and/or steps are not clear."],tb:{head:["Step","ITIL 4","ITIL (Version 5)"],rows:[["1","What is the vision?","What is the vision?"],["2","Where are we now?","Where are we now?"],["3","Where do we want to be?","Where do we want to be?"],["4","How do we get there?","How do we get there?"],["5","Take action","Take action"],["6","Did we get there?","Are we getting there?"],["7","How do we keep the momentum going?","How do we keep the improvements relevant?"]]}},
{m:"m2",h:"What changed: Service Journey Model",li:["Same model in both versions: the ITIL Service Journey Model describes the steps of the service journey and the key tasks of the service consumer and service provider.","ITIL (Version 5) gives a brief explanation of the journey shared by service providers and service consumers.","Definition to know: service journey (1.4.3)."]},
{m:"m9",h:"What changed: new ITIL AI Capability Model",li:["ITIL 4: N/A. New in ITIL (Version 5): describes AI's key functional capabilities.","Six capabilities (Table 2.1): creation, curation, clarification, cognition, communication, coordination.","Placed in the 'Information and technology' dimension. Related new definitions: AI, AI maturity, GenAI, Agentic AI, AI governance (section 6)."]},
{m:"m10",h:"What changed: exam scope (QRG vs ITIL 4 Foundation)",li:["Not from the Release Notes: comparison of this QRG with the ITIL 4 Foundation syllabus.","Examinable now: ITIL and AI (section 6), the Product and Service Lifecycle Model with the purpose and success metrics of the eight activities, value stream mapping and management with complexity thinking, practice groups, enabling vs supporting practices, the Practice Guide structure and benefits, and ITIL with DevOps and PRINCE2 (section 7).","New key terms: digital product, digital service, goods, digital product vendor, sustainability, value co-creation, service journey, product/service specification and prototype, continuous integration, delivery and deployment, reliability, SRE, observability, disaster, operating model, complexity thinking.","No longer examined: the purpose of 15 practices and the detailed knowledge of 7 practices required by ITIL 4 Foundation. V5 asks about the purpose, structure, benefits, and role of practices in the value system.","Not in the V5 QRG: the six service value chain activities (plan, engage, design and transition, obtain/build, deliver and support, improve) and the ITIL 4 definitions of IT asset, configuration item, and change."]}
];

;
