window.NOTES=[
{l:"m1a",h:"Module 1 | Syllabus",n:["At the end of this course, you will be able to:","• understand the key concepts of digital product and service management","• understand service relationships","• understand the ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management","• understand the ITIL Value System (ITIL VS)","• understand how the ITIL Guiding Principles can help an organization adopt and adapt service management","• understand the ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model and the value chain activities","• understand ITIL Management Practices","• understand the benefits of value stream mapping and management","• understand how AI affects ITIL, what AI Governance is, and how ITIL interacts with other frameworks (PRINCE2® and DEVOPS INSTITUTE®)","ITIL Foundation (Version 5) training is an introduction to ITIL (Version 5). The ITIL Foundation (Version 5) Official Book, which contains more information, is worth reading both during and after the training, to help you embed the learning. Please also refer to and utilize the resources in your Learner Kit and Learner Workbook. On successful completion of the exam, you will also gain access to the PeopleCert Plus online portal.","This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module. You will find similar slides throughout the course.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m1a",h:"How digital are our products?",n:["Products exist on a continuum based on their use of digital technology. At one end are non-digital products that do not rely on digital technology; at the other are fully digital products that contribute to value creation primarily through software and digital capabilities. Most modern products fall somewhere in-between these extremes. Goods are tangible resources transferred from a service provider to a service consumer, with ownership and associated rights and responsibilities moving to the consumer upon transfer."]},
{l:"m1a",h:"(Digital) product and (digital) service",n:["Since the mid-twentieth century, services have become central to the global economy. Products provide the capabilities on which services are built, while services may introduce costs and risks and influence outcomes in both positive and negative ways.","ITIL focuses on digital services, which enable value through digital products without transferring ownership. Digital products provide capabilities and service offerings describe potential value."]},
{l:"m1a",h:"Relationships between digital products and services",n:["Digital products and digital services are interdependent. A single product may enable many services and one service may be based on several products. While products provide capabilities, service offerings describe potential value. When customers accept service offerings, service delivery and consumption begin."]},
{l:"m1a",h:"Digital product and service management",n:["Countries and organizations are moving away from Industry 4.0, focused on automation, efficiency, and productivity, towards Industry 5.0, characterized by human-centricity, sustainability, and resilience. The roles of humans and technology are shifting from ‘humans operating technology’ to ‘humans collaborating with technology’. Sustainability becomes a core pillar, rather than a secondary concern.","This evolution requires changes in the approach to technology management. Over the last 40 years, good practices in technology management have undergone several major shifts:","• from managing technology systems to managing technology products and services","• from focusing on outputs and performance to focusing on value, outcomes, and experience","• from specialization to collaboration","• from optimizing for efficiency to enabling adaptability and resilience","• from order and standardization to embracing complexity and encouraging diversity","• from prioritizing profit to balancing profit, people, and the planet.","ITIL is a holistic, practical, and coherent framework supported by a comprehensive body of knowledge.","Digital services are always based on digital products. Products provide capabilities, while service offerings describe the potential value they offer to customers. When customers accept these offerings, service delivery and consumption begin. In a simple scenario, one service may be based on one product. More often, the relationships are more complicated: one product may enable many services, and one service may be based on several products."]},
{l:"m1b",h:"What is a service offering?",n:["A service offering formally describes a service. The definition of service offering highlights three forms of interaction between a service consumer and a service: transfer of goods, access to resources, and service actions. These interactions represent key touchpoints along the service journey that influence the overall user experience. Digital products enable and support service offerings, which in turn describe digital services."]},
{l:"m1b",h:"Service offerings may include",n:["Service offerings define how consumers interact with a service; the balance between interactions differs across industries. For digital services, the transfer of goods and service actions are less common because they rely on physical items or human effort that limit scalability. Instead, access to resources is the dominant form. This design choice reflects a broader trend: service providers aim to reduce dependence on goods or manual actions to deliver more efficient, reliable, and scalable services."]},
{l:"m1c",h:"The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle (1/2)",n:["User and customer experience depend on two interconnected factors: the quality of digital products and the service provider’s management system. To achieve this, organizations must capture and process customer needs and feedback, identify opportunities, and design effective products and services. They then acquire the necessary resources, build and test solutions, and transition them into live environments."]},
{l:"m1c",h:"The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle (2/2)",n:["Once products are live, they must be operated reliably and services delivered effectively, with support in place to restore normal operation if issues arise. The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model is structured around eight steps: discover, design, acquire, build, transition, operate, deliver, and support. As organizations move through these steps, the management focus shifts between digital product development and digital service delivery, ensuring value is consistently created for customers and stakeholders."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"Output and outcome",n:["An output is a tangible or intangible deliverable of an activity. An outcome is a result for a stakeholder, enabled by one or more outputs. Acting as a service provider, an organization produces outputs assisting consumers achieve a certain outcome. For example, for a wedding photography service, the output may be a photo album, while the outcome is the preservation of memories. Depending on the relationship, providers may need to collaborate with consumers to define outcomes."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Outcomes",n:["ITIL Car Rental is a fictional company that will be used as a case study for implementing ITIL within an organization. Basic information about this fictional company is detailed on this slide.","ITIL Car Rental (ICR) is a fictional company that will be used as a case study for implementing ITIL practices within an organization.","At various points in the course you will be asked to take the role of the ITIL Car Rental employees and read/act out their comments and views.","This scenario illustrates how a clear vision guides ITIL Car Rental’s transformation, focusing on changing customer expectations, implementing technology-enabled services, and delivering value beyond traditional car rental through seamless, customer-centric experiences.","This example shows the difference between an output and outcomes in the context of ITIL Car Rental. While the output is the customer arriving at their destination, the outcomes depend on the stakeholder. Customers experience flexibility, comfort, and affordability, while cities benefit from reduced traffic, cleaner air, and improved safety. This example reinforces the ITIL principle of designing services to create value for multiple stakeholders."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"Value, outcomes, costs, and risks",n:["Achieving a desired outcome requires resources (and therefore has certain costs) and is often associated with risks. Service providers help their consumers achieve outcomes, and in doing so, undertake some of the associated costs and risks. Services may introduce new costs and risks or negatively affect some outcomes while supporting others. Services are perceived as valuable only when the positive effects are greater than the negative. This balance is central to value co-creation."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"Cost",n:["Cost is defined as the amount of money spent on a specific activity or resource. From the service consumer’s perspective, two types of cost exist: costs removed by the service as part of the value proposition and costs imposed through service consumption. Total cost includes the provider’s price and additional expenses like training, network usage, and procurement. From the service provider’s perspective, understanding service provision costs ensures delivery within budget and financial expectations."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"Risk",n:["Risk is defined as a possible event that could cause harm or loss or make it more difficult to achieve objectives. Services can both reduce and introduce risks for consumers:","• Risks reduced or removed by the service form part of the value proposition.","• Risks imposed by the service are risks associated with service consumption.","Service providers manage service-specific risks on behalf of the consumer through effective risk management."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Risks",n:["This example shows that services can both reduce and introduce risk. ITIL Car Rental reduces risks such as road safety and vehicle maintenance but may introduce new risks, including system failures or cybersecurity issues. Managing these risks is essential to delivering service value."]},
{l:"m1d",h:"Value",n:["Value is the foundation of service management, and every service relationship aims to enable value co-creation for the consumer. Achieving desired outcomes requires resources and involves costs and risks. Service providers support consumers by taking on some of these costs and risks. Through specialization, providers develop the resources, skills, knowledge, and experience needed to deliver reliable, high-quality services that help consumers achieve outcomes effectively.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"Module 2 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"Organizations",n:["Organizations can vary in size and structure, from individuals to large multinational companies. In service relationships, organizations’ roles apply to a particular service relationship. An organization acting as a service provider in one service relationship may be playing the service consumer role in another. Many organizations also act as product vendors."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"Providers, consumers, and vendors",n:["In service relationships, organizations usually act as service providers or consumers. However, digital services depend on digital products, so the provider role is often combined with that of a digital product vendor. Vendors focus on creating and continually improving digital products, which form the basis for related service offerings. This distinction is important because expectations of value may differ across these roles."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"Roles in managing, delivering, and consuming services",n:["The figure illustrates organizations’ roles in the management, delivery, and consumption of products and services. When defining purpose and strategy, an organization determines the roles it will perform in service relationships, influencing its design and operation. Roles are relationship-specific: an organization may act as a service provider in one case and a service consumer in another. All organizations act as both service providers and service consumers, and many also function as product vendors."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"Service consumer roles",n:["Service consumer is a generic organizational role. In practice, there are three specific roles representing the service consumer organization in its relationship with service providers and product vendors: sponsors, customers, and users. In service relationships, it is important to identify these roles to ensure effective communication and stakeholder management. These roles have different service journeys and service experiences."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"Consumer roles example",n:["In this example, the company acquires mobile phone services from a provider. The roles illustrate how responsibilities are divided in practice. The CIO and communications team act as the customer, focusing on requirements definition, supplier negotiations, and performance monitoring. The CFO acts as the sponsor, ensuring that costs, funding, and contractual commitments align with organizational priorities. Finally, employees, including leadership, take on the user role by ordering, receiving, and using the service.","In practice, the roles of sponsor and customer may overlap. For example:","• A CIO may act as both customer and sponsor when they define service requirements, negotiate with the supplier, and also hold the budget accountability for the service.","• In smaller organizations, a department head may sponsor a service by approving funding while also acting as the customer by specifying requirements and assessing whether the service delivers expected outcomes.","It is important to identify these roles clearly, even when they are fulfilled by the same individual or team, because each role represents a different perspective on value. Sponsors focus primarily on investment justification and strategic alignment, while customers focus on service outcomes and performance. Recognizing these differences helps ensure effective communication, better alignment of expectations, and stronger stakeholder management."]},
{l:"m2a",h:"The service relationship model",n:["Service relationships form chains and networks, each organization can act as both a service consumer and a service provider. What appears to be one type of interaction from the consumer’s perspective may be categorized differently by the provider. Value is co-created through continuous exchanges of inputs, outputs, and feedback between parties. Each entity operates within its own lifecycle while aligning with others."]},
{l:"m2b",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Types of service relationships",n:["ITIL Car Rental has a basic relationship with charging network providers, a cooperative relationship with its cleaning provider, and a collaborative relationship with the vehicle manufacturer. Each type of relationship reflects a different level of integration, shared planning and responsibility, and should be chosen based on business needs to co-create value."]},
{l:"m2b",h:"Types of service relationships",n:["Service relationships involve sharing resources, data, and sometimes reputation with another organization. Different levels of engagement are characterized by different degrees of sharing and dependency. The three types of service relationships are basic, cooperative, and collaborative, with the last one also called partnership. When consuming a service, organizations must choose an appropriate relationship type. Both the provider and consumer must aim for the same relationship type, as misalignment can lead to misunderstanding and dissatisfaction."]},
{l:"m2b",h:"Basic, cooperative, and collaborative",n:["Basic service relationships involve standardized services offered to many consumers, such as mobile operators, broadband providers, transport companies, and cloud service providers offering storage and computing resources.","Cooperative relationships involve service providers tailoring services to customer needs, often seen with managed service providers or shared service providers within large organizations.","Collaborative relationships (partnerships) involve close cooperation, shared goals, and high trust, such as internal IT teams supporting business units or strategic partnerships between providers and consumers.","Refer to your Official Book, Table 3.1 for more details."]},
{l:"m2c",h:"Service journey (1/2)",n:["The service journey focuses on the full set of activities and interactions performed by service providers and service consumers over time. In contrast, the band of visibility focuses on which parts of those activities and interactions are visible to each party. Not all activities are visible to both sides: many provider activities occur outside the consumer’s view, while some consumer activities remain unseen by the provider. Understanding this helps organizations manage expectations, improve transparency, and avoid assuming that visibility equals value."]},
{l:"m2c",h:"Service journey (2/2)",n:["Introduce the ITIL Service Journey Model as a universal view of how service consumers and service providers interact. While shown as a sequence, the journey is not strictly linear. Steps may repeat, overlap, or vary depending on context. The seven steps are:","• Explore","• Engage","• Offer","• Agree","• Onboard","• Co-create","• Reflect","Although presented as steps, the service journey is best understood as a set of stepping stones rather than a rigid linear process."]},
{l:"m2d",h:"Service quality and service level",n:["Services enable value co-creation by facilitating desired outcomes. Because providers and consumers share responsibility, outcomes cannot always be fully specified or owned by one party. Instead, both sides agree on service quality as a combination of outputs and user experience. To manage this, expectations are translated into metrics that define the service level."]},
{l:"m2d",h:"Service level metrics (1/3)",n:["Service level metrics fall into four categories: utility, warranty, sustainability, and experience. All four are present in every service to a greater or lesser degree, depending on the type of service and the relationship between the service provider and the service consumer."]},
{l:"m2d",h:"Service level metrics (2/3)",n:["Utility defines what a service does by providing functionality that meets a need and supports outcomes or removes constraints. Warranty defines how well the service performs by ensuring it meets agreed conditions such as availability, capacity, security, and continuity. Both are essential: utility ensures the service is useful, while warranty ensures it is dependable. Without both, a service cannot deliver reliable value to consumers."]},
{l:"m2d",h:"Service level metrics (3/3)",n:["Sustainability ensures that services meet ongoing requirements for environmental stewardship, social progress, and economic growth, with practices such as responsible sourcing, clean energy use, low carbon footprint, recyclable materials, and transparent supply chains, often guided by regulations or organizational strategies. User experience (UX) is the sum of functional and emotional interactions with a service, shaped by ease of use, quality of interface, relevance to objectives, access to support, and overall perception of value."]},
{l:"m2d",h:"Service level agreements",n:["The common way to establish a shared understanding of expected and achieved service quality and to manage improvement is through a Service Level Agreement (SLA). Managing service levels and SLAs is supported by the Service Level Management (SLM) practice.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m3a",h:"Module 3 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m3a",h:"Management system",n:["The organizational context is constantly evolving, requiring management systems that support continual review, adjustment, and sustainable achievement of objectives. ITIL defines four key elements of a product and service management system: the ITIL Guiding Principles to support decision-making, the value chain to structure activities, ITIL Management Practices to provide capabilities, and continual improvement to maintain alignment with stakeholder needs. Together, these elements form the foundation of the ITIL Value System, which is completed by the oversight of governance."]},
{l:"m3a",h:"Governance and digital technology",n:["Governance of digital technology is the fifth component of the ITIL VS. While the management system defines how work is done, governance provides oversight ensuring digital products and services are managed responsibly, with accountability for both current and future use of technology. It does this through human decision-making, direction, and oversight, completing the ITIL VS model."]},
{l:"m3a",h:"The ITIL Value System",n:["The ITIL Value System (ITIL VS) shows how the ITIL Guiding Principles, value chain, management practices, continual improvement, and governance work together to enable value co-creation. It transforms opportunity and demand into value through outcomes that support organizational objectives and stakeholders. While it does not prescribe specific policies or controls, it provides a holistic framework within which management systems operate and integrate, connecting organizations within a broader service ecosystem."]},
{l:"m3a",h:"The ITIL Value System | Purpose",n:["The ITIL VS is the backbone of ITIL. It integrates all the key components to help organizations co-create value. Its strength is flexibility: the same five elements (ITIL Guiding principles, the value chain, ITIL Management Practices, continual improvement, and governance) can be combined in many ways to support operating models and value streams appropriate to the organization’s context. The ITIL VS also helps break down silos, encouraging integration and collaboration."]},
{l:"m3a",h:"ITIL VS | Inputs and outcome",n:["The ITIL VS takes inputs in the form of opportunity and demand and transforms them into outputs. In practice, these outputs are the achievement of organizational objectives and value for the organization, its customers, and other stakeholders. ITIL defines value as the perceived benefits, usefulness, and importance of something. This highlights how the ITIL VS turns opportunities and demand into sustained value for multiple stakeholders.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m4a",h:"Module 4 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m4a",h:"Governing bodies and governance",n:["Every organization has a governing body, typically a board of directors or senior executives, responsible for oversight and direction. This body is accountable for performance and compliance, ensuring adherence to internal policies and external regulations. Governance applies across the entire organization, including the use and management of digital technology. Effective governance of digital technology ensures that decisions are strategic, accountable, and aligned with organizational objectives, supporting value creation and sustainable outcomes."]},
{l:"m4a",h:"Governance of digital technology",n:["Governance is realized, in a cyclical way, through four activities: engage, evaluate, direct, and monitor. An organization’s governing body first engages with stakeholders to clarify needs, commitments, and accountability. It then evaluates strategies, portfolios, policies, and relationships. The outputs from evaluation become the inputs to direction, where strategies and policies are prepared, implemented, and responsibilities assigned. Finally, the feedback from monitoring performance feeds into future rounds of evaluation.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m5a",h:"Module 5 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m5a",h:"What is an ITIL Guiding Principle?",n:["In the past, organizations depended on fixed rules, processes, and policies. In today’s fast-changing environment, it is impossible to create set rules for all circumstances. The ITIL Guiding Principles fill this gap by giving direction when detailed rules are missing, so decisions still support organizational values and objectives. They are flexible enough to work alongside modern ways of working, such as agile and DevOps, and their universal nature means they apply across all industries and organization types."]},
{l:"m5a",h:"Overview of the ITIL Guiding Principles",n:["There are seven ITIL Guiding Principles. These are recommended mindsets or behaviours that keep organizations focused on value, collaboration, visibility, and practical action. The principles shape how products and services are designed, delivered, and improved, influencing service quality and customer experience. They support sound decision-making at all levels and help organizations adapt ITIL guidance while driving continual improvement over time."]},
{l:"m5a",h:"Focus on value",n:["To apply this principle, the organization must first know who the service consumer is and understand what matters to them, why they use the service, how it supports their goals, and the outcomes they expect. Applying the principle means gathering feedback continuously, encouraging everyone in the organization to focus on value in both daily work and improvement initiatives, and making sure every effort is measured against the outcomes and value it creates."]},
{l:"m5a",h:"Who is the service consumer?",n:["When focusing on value, the service provider must identify the service consumer and key stakeholders such as customers, users, or sponsors. This understanding is achieved through the ‘discover’ activity at different levels of the organization. The key question is always: who will benefit from what is being delivered or improved?"]},
{l:"m5a",h:"Value for the consumer",n:["Value for the consumer is not fixed. It depends on consumer needs. It is shaped by their experience and changes over time. The provider must understand the why, what, how, as well as the costs and risks for the service consumer."]},
{l:"m5a",h:"Focus on value | Applying the principle",n:["To apply the ‘focus on value’ principle means to understand how services contribute to consumer outcomes and continuously gather feedback on perceived value. Ensure staff are aware of customer expectations and their role in delivering value. Value creation should be embedded in everyday work, not limited to improvement efforts. Improvement initiatives must clearly define expected outcomes, measurement of value, and individual contributions. Leveraging AI can enhance understanding of needs through real-time insights and predictive analysis, supporting better alignment with expectations and improved satisfaction."]},
{l:"m5b",h:"Start where you are",n:["To apply this principle, avoid discarding existing methods, services, processes, tools, or people just because you are moving toward improvement. Starting over is rarely wise and often wasteful. Existing capabilities may have significant value and should be leveraged whenever possible. The key is to recognize what already works and build upon it, instead of removing everything and beginning from scratch."]},
{l:"m5b",h:"Assess where you are",n:["It is important to base the assessment of products, services, and practices on direct observation rather than assumptions or secondhand reports. A fresh perspective and even ‘simple’ questions can uncover valuable insights.","In addition to observation, organizations can use structured tools:","• ITIL Official Practice Guides: provide metrics, capability criteria, and practical recommendations for evaluating each practice.","• ITIL Maturity Model: helps assess practice capability levels and the maturity of the overall ITIL Value System.","These resources help ensure assessments are objective and comprehensive."]},
{l:"m5b",h:"The influence of measurement",n:["According to Goodhart’s Law: ‘When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.’ Therefore, metrics must be meaningful and directly related to desired outcomes. They should encourage behaviors that improve service quality, not just satisfy numbers. For example, if a service desk agent knows their call time is being measured, they may rush calls instead of fully resolving user issues."]},
{l:"m5b",h:"Start where you are | Applying the principle",n:["This principle encourages organizations to improve intelligently rather than replacing everything by default. The process begins with understanding the current environment objectively and identifying practices that already deliver value. Reusing proven approaches can save time, reduce costs, and accelerate improvement efforts. However, organizations must also evaluate potential risks, including outdated behaviors or ineffective processes. AI technologies can support faster analysis and decision-making by revealing patterns and opportunities. In exceptional situations, organizations may determine that starting from scratch is the most effective option."]},
{l:"m5c",h:"Progress iteratively with feedback",n:["Whether working to improve a product, service, practice, process, or technical environment, no improvement iteration occurs in a vacuum. While the iteration is being undertaken, circumstances can change, new priorities can arise, and the need for the iteration may be altered or even eliminated. Resist the temptation to do everything at once. Even major initiatives should be broken into smaller, manageable, and managed iterations. Each iteration should deliver tangible results in a timely manner that can be built upon to create further improvement."]},
{l:"m5c",h:"The use of feedback loops",n:["Feedback should be actively collected and processed along the value stream. Effective mechanisms capture perceptions of value, efficiency, governance, supplier interactions, and changes in demand. This information may be structured or unstructured, solicited or unsolicited, but must be analysed to identify improvements, risks, and issues."]},
{l:"m5c",h:"The role of feedback",n:["No improvement iteration occurs in a vacuum. Circumstances change continuously, so feedback must be sought before, during, and after each iteration to ensure actions remain relevant. Feedback includes information about stakeholder reactions, experiences, and opinions, and in a well-functioning organization it is continually captured along value streams. Strong feedback mechanisms provide insight into customer perceptions of value, operational efficiency and effectiveness, governance, supplier interactions, and changing demand. Once received, feedback should be analysed to identify improvement opportunities, risks, and issues."]},
{l:"m5c",h:"Combining iteration and feedback",n:["Having appropriate feedback loops between the participants of an activity gives them a better understanding of where their work comes from, where their outputs go, and how their actions and outputs affect the outcomes, which in turn enables them to make better decisions."]},
{l:"m5c",h:"Progress iteratively | Applying the principle",n:["Iterative improvement requires balancing understanding with action. Excessive analysis can create “analysis paralysis,” delaying meaningful progress. Because change is constant, organizations should continuously gather and apply feedback at all levels to remain responsive and effective. Fast iterations should still deliver complete and valuable outcomes aligned with the concept of the minimum viable product (MVP), enabling maximum learning with minimal effort. AI strengthens this approach by identifying trends, bottlenecks, and issues quickly, supporting timely adjustments and continuous incremental improvement."]},
{l:"m5d",h:"Collaborate and promote visibility",n:["Inclusion is usually better than exclusion, as unexpected sources can provide valuable ideas and perspectives. Silos that are caused by behaviors, structures, or systems, block collaboration. Applying the principles of focus on value and think and work holistically helps remove these barriers. Collaboration drives modern ways of working. Without it, agile, Lean, or any ITSM method will fail. But collaboration does not mean consensus. Input should be valued, but decisions must move forward without requiring agreement from everyone."]},
{l:"m5d",h:"Whom to collaborate with",n:["Collaboration that leads to real accomplishment requires information, understanding, and trust. Work and its results should be made visible, hidden agendas should be avoided, and information should be shared to the greatest degree possible. The more people are aware of what is happening and why, the more they will be willing to help."]},
{l:"m5d",h:"Increasing visibility",n:["Poor visibility of an initiative’s workload and progress can create the impression that the work is not a priority. When initiatives are rarely discussed after being introduced, stakeholders may assume they are unimportant. This can result in poor decision-making and reduce the organization’s ability to improve internal capabilities effectively. It also becomes difficult to determine which improvements will provide the greatest benefit."]},
{l:"m5d",h:"Collaborate | Applying the principle",n:["It is not always necessary, or wise, to gain consensus from everyone before proceeding with an initiative. Trying to satisfy every stakeholder can delay progress or produce outcomes that meet no one’s needs effectively. Organizations should also avoid relying on a single communication method and instead tailor messages to different audiences. Decisions made without relevant data are risky, so organizations must determine what information should be visible and whether the value of collecting it justifies the cost. AI tools can further support collaboration by highlighting insights, updates, and risks."]},
{l:"m5e",h:"Think and work holistically (1/3)",n:["To deliver value, organizations must integrate their activities as a whole, rather than in silos. This requires end-to-end visibility of how demand becomes outcomes and awareness that any change in one part of the system can affect others.","A holistic approach means considering all four dimensions of service management, looking across the full lifecycle of products and services, and recognizing the organization’s place in the wider supply chain. It also involves understanding the impact on service consumers and continually monitoring external PESTLE factors."]},
{l:"m5e",h:"Think and work holistically (2/3)",n:["Thinking and working holistically means recognizing that no product, service, process, or team stands alone. The organization must work in an integrated way, with all parts connected to deliver outcomes effectively. A holistic approach requires end-to-end visibility of how demand is captured and transformed into value. Any change to one element can impact others, so these effects should be identified, analysed, and addressed appropriately."]},
{l:"m5e",h:"Think and work holistically (3/3)",n:["No product, service, practice, process, or team stands alone. The outputs that the organization delivers to itself, customers, and others stakeholders will suffer unless it works in an integrated way and treats activities as a whole rather than in isolation.","A holistic approach means establishing a clear understanding of how all parts work together, supported by end-to-end visibility from demand through to outcomes. Because modern organizations are complex systems, changing one element can affect others. These impacts must be identified, analysed, and addressed."]},
{l:"m5e",h:"Think holistically | Applying the principle",n:["Different levels of complexity require different approaches. Methods suited for simple systems may not work in complex ones where relationships change frequently. With the right mechanisms, stakeholders can collaborate in a timely way and address issues holistically without delays, using knowledge from across the system to identify what is essential for success, anticipate needs, set standards, and strengthen outcomes. Automation and AI can provide end-to-end visibility, analyse complex interactions, and support integrated, holistic management."]},
{l:"m5f",h:"Keep it simple and practical (1/2)",n:["The ‘keep it simple and practical’ principle aims to minimize the number of steps needed to achieve an objective and use outcome-based thinking to deliver value. If a process, product, service, action, or metric does not add value, it should be eliminated."]},
{l:"m5f",h:"Keep it simple and practical (2/2)",n:["For example, an IT team may try to create separate approval steps for every unusual service request. Over time, the process becomes slow, difficult to follow, and frustrating for users. Instead, the organization could use guiding principles and give trained staff the authority to handle exceptions when needed. This keeps the process simple while still allowing flexibility. At the same time, teams must remember that some activities that appear unnecessary may still be essential for compliance, security, or organizational governance."]},
{l:"m5f",h:"Conflicting objectives",n:["When designing, managing, or operating practices, organizations must balance competing objectives. For example, management may require extensive data for decision-making, while record-keeping teams may prefer simpler processes with less manual data entry. Applying the ITIL Guiding Principles helps organizations find an appropriate balance between these needs. Product solutions should generate only data that adds real value to decisions, while record-keeping activities should be simplified and automated wherever possible to maximize value, improve efficiency, and reduce non-value-adding work."]},
{l:"m5f",h:"Keep it simple | Applying the principle",n:["Simplicity means cutting away unnecessary work and focusing on value creation. Empowering people and leveraging automation helps keep practices practical and easy to adopt. For example, a service desk may require staff to complete lengthy forms for every simple password reset. Over time, this creates delays and frustration without adding much value. By simplifying the process, automating repetitive steps, and trusting trained staff to handle standard requests, the organization can improve efficiency and user experience. Simple practices are easier to understand, more likely to be adopted, and allow employees to focus on activities that provide greater value."]},
{l:"m5g",h:"Optimize and automate",n:["The ‘optimize and automate’ principle focuses on getting the greatest value from both human and technical resources. Optimization means making something effective, efficient, and useful within constraints such as cost, compliance, time, and available resources. Processes should be reasonably optimized before being automated. Automation should enhance, not replace, good practices and must remain subject to governance and ethical controls, especially when generative AI is used. The goal is to free people to focus on higher-value work while reducing unnecessary risks and maintaining resilience."]},
{l:"m5g",h:"The road to optimization",n:["There are many ways in which practices, products, and services can be optimized. Regardless of the specific techniques, the path to optimization follows the above high-level steps. ITIL supports optimization efforts by providing a structured guide to implementation, ITIL Transformation Official Book, and the ITIL Continual Improvement and ITIL Measurement and Reporting Official Practice Guides. Chapter 6 of ITIL Foundation Official Book introduces an approach to product and service value stream mapping and management, a powerful method of workflow optimization."]},
{l:"m5g",h:"Using automation and AI",n:["Automation uses technology to perform steps correctly and consistently with little or no human involvement. This can range from streamlining manual tasks to continuous deployment with built-in testing. Automation can reduce costs, errors, and manual effort while improving employee experience. AI extends automation through virtual AI assistants, data insights, chatbots, and intelligent workflows. These add powerful capabilities, but also require strong governance to ensure quality, ethics, privacy, and security."]},
{l:"m5g",h:"Optimize and automate | Applying the principle",n:["Automation should follow optimization and be guided by clear metrics and other principles. Automating inefficient workflows only increases the speed of poor practices. For example, automating incident routing before simplifying the process can create faster inefficiencies rather than better outcomes. Organizations should first streamline workflows, then automate repetitive activities. Metrics such as resolution time and customer satisfaction help measure success. AI can further improve efficiency by monitoring services and identifying optimization opportunities while remaining subject to governance, ethics, privacy, and security controls."]},
{l:"m5g",h:"Principles interaction",n:["The ITIL Guiding Principles work together and support one another in practice. For example, progressing iteratively with feedback requires a holistic view to ensure improvements deliver complete results. Collaboration relies on effective feedback, while focusing on value helps organizations simplify work and prioritize activities. The principles do not follow a hierarchy, although some may become more important depending on the situation. While ITIL offers seven Guiding Principles, organizations should adapt them to reflect their culture and values, ensuring they guide decision-making and continual improvement across all levels of the organization.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m6a",h:"Module 6 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this section.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m6a",h:"The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model",n:["The ITIL Product and Service Lifecycle Model explains how organizations ensure quality products and services that meet customer and user expectations. It connects product quality with the service provider’s management system and highlights key activities, from capturing needs to designing, building, delivering, and supporting services."]},
{l:"m6a",h:"Lifecycle management activities",n:["To manage digital products and services throughout their shared lifecycle, organizations perform lifecycle management activities. These activities enable and support the product and service lifecycle but are not performed as a fixed cycle or in a strict sequence. They are combined in different ways depending on the organization’s structure, product or service architecture, and context. These activities make up the organization’s value chain, which provides an interconnected operating model for creating value through products and services."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"Operating models and value chains",n:["Organizations exist to create value for customers and other stakeholders. This is expressed through the organization’s purpose, which defines what the organization does and why. To make that purpose practical and understandable, an operating model is used. The operating model provides a conceptual or visual representation of how the organization co-creates value with its customers and stakeholders, while also showing how the organization runs itself. Finally, the value chain illustrates the set of activities the organization undertakes to fulfill its purpose and create value."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"Operating model canvas",n:["Many organizations use templates to assist in defining their operating models, including the widely used Operating Model Canvas by Campbell.","For more details please visit: Operating Model Canvas – The Operating Model Canvas tool is about high-level operating models."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"Operating model canvas and ITIL",n:["Operating models typically describe several key elements. ITIL suggests structuring operating models around the Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management explained in Chapter 2:","• Value streams and processes: workflows used by the organization to structure its value-creating activities.","• Organizations and people: culture, competencies, and teams contributing to value creation.","• Information and technology: data, information, and technology tools used in value creation.","• Partners and suppliers: third parties contributing to value creation activities."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"Operating model canvas and ITIL Value System",n:["This slide shows:","• What components are needed to operate effectively (Guiding Principles, governance, continual improvement, management practices, Four Dimensions).","• How value is created through the value chain activities.","• How different management practices support value streams.","The operating model describes the key elements required for an organization to create and deliver value."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"ITIL key models | The big picture",n:["This slide shows the key models of ITIL. It explains how ITIL incorporates models, components, and core concepts into one coherent system. In short, the ITIL Guiding Principles guide decisions → Service relationships enable co-creation → The ITIL Value System structures work → The value chain organizes activities → Management practices provide capabilities → The ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management ensure balance."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"Purpose, operating model, and value chain",n:["The organization’s purpose defines what it does and why. The operating model shows how that purpose is fulfilled, while value chain activities describe the end-to-end workflows that create value. These activities are not stand-alone; they are consistently enabled and strengthened by management practices, which provide the methods, resources, and guidance to carry the activities out effectively. Together, this structure ensures that digital products and services are managed in a reliable and value-driven way across their lifecycle."]},
{l:"m6b",h:"Value chain patterns",n:["Every digital product and service progresses through all stages of its lifecycle, although responsibility for managing these stages may belong to one organization or be shared across partners and suppliers. Organizations adopt different value chain patterns depending on their roles, responsibilities, and sourcing models. These patterns influence the management practices needed to support digital product and service activities. In most cases, the value chain provides a common framework within which multiple value streams operate, enabling consistent coordination, collaboration, and value creation."]},
{l:"m6c",h:"Management practices",n:["Management practices are the building blocks that enable organizations to perform work consistently and effectively. ITIL recognizes 34 practices, grouped into general practices, which apply broadly across organizations, and product and service management practices, which focus on digital products and services. These practices matter because they bring the value chain to life, ensuring activities like ‘design’, ‘build’, ‘transition’, and ‘operate’ are supported with the right people, processes, and tools."]},
{l:"m6c",h:"General management practices (12)",n:["ITIL defines 34 management practices that provide the capabilities, resources, and processes needed to manage digital products and services effectively.","• Product and service management practices directly support value chain activities and product and service management.","• General management practices apply across the wider organization and help align digital services with business goals.","Together, these practices create a consistent and integrated toolkit that supports the ITIL Value System, strengthens collaboration across teams, and enables organizations to deliver value in a reliable and repeatable manner."]},
{l:"m6c",h:"Practices in the value chain (1/2)",n:["The ITIL management practices enable and support the value chain activities rather than following a fixed sequence. A single practice may contribute to multiple activities, and each activity is typically supported by several practices. Different value streams may use the same practices in different ways, depending on context, objectives, and desired outcomes."]},
{l:"m6c",h:"Practices in the value chain (2/2)",n:["Each value chain activity performed by an organization is enabled and supported by several management practices. There are two levels of involvement of a management practice in a value chain activity. Activities of some practices are directly involved in the value chain activity; these practices can be categorized as enabling (E in the table). Other practices, though not directly involved in the value chain activity, support it with information and methods; these practices can be categorized as supporting (S in the table). For example, the ‘design’ value chain activity is enabled by 17 practices (including service design, architecture management, measurement and reporting, and others) and supported by seven practices (including IT asset management, knowledge management, and others).","When organizations include a value chain activity in their operating model, they need to develop the relevant management capabilities. Note that not all practices listed in this table are always involved in the respective value chain activities. For example, organizational change management practice may be very important for designing digital products and services when the design requires changes to the service provider’s or service consumer’s organization. At the same time, this practice is not included in many cases of product and service design. The mapping provided here should be considered when organizations develop their value chains and management practices, but it should always be adapted to the organization and product specifics. Refer to the Official Book for the complete Table B.1."]},
{l:"m6c",h:"Structure of ITIL Official Practice Guides",n:["Each ITIL Official Practice Guide follows a common structure, making them easier to apply consistently. They begin with general information, including purpose, scope, key terms, and success factors. The main sections describe how the practice contributes to value streams, roles and responsibilities involved, the information and technology it relies on, and any dependencies on suppliers or partners. Each guide also includes capability assessment criteria for self-evaluation. Finally, they provide recommendations for success linked to the ITIL Guiding Principles, ensuring practical and consistent application."]},
{l:"m6c",h:"Benefits of ITIL Official Practice Guides",n:["Each of the 34 guides follows the same structure, making it easier to compare and integrate practices. They go beyond processes to cover people, technology, and supplier dependencies. They include clear recommendations, success factors, roles, and metrics that organizations can apply immediately and support continual improvement, with built-in maturity assessments and alignment to ITIL guiding principles. This makes them not just reference materials, but tools for ongoing growth and optimization."]},
{l:"a1",h:"Discover: purpose and metrics of success",n:["‘Discover’ is not a one-time effort but an ongoing activity that adapts as strategies, consumer expectations, and market conditions change. Once important changes or trends in these factors are identified, product/service teams decide on the best way to address them. Success is reflected in how well the offerings fit the organization’s strategy, how relevant they are to the market, the level of stakeholder satisfaction, and, where appropriate, their commercial success."]},
{l:"a1",h:"Discover: overview",n:["The ‘discover’ activity helps organizations continually review their positioning, vision, and strategy to maintain an effective product and service portfolio. By analysing the business context and stakeholder needs, organizations can identify opportunities, define priorities, and ensure that product capabilities and service offerings remain aligned with organizational objectives. Discoveries made at organizational and portfolio levels influence product/service-level decisions and requirements. Effective collaboration between product and service teams, business analysts, and relationship managers supports informed decision-making and continual alignment with changing business and consumer needs."]},
{l:"a1",h:"High-level workflow of the 'discover' activity",n:["This figure shows the high-level workflow of the ‘discover’ activity. ‘Discover’ can be triggered by regular reviews, stakeholder requests, or external events. Each iteration follows four core steps: assessing the product context, agreeing direction and objectives, prioritizing actions and allocating resources, and communicating changes in the product roadmap. The outcome of each iteration is an updated and shared understanding of product direction reflected in roadmaps, backlogs, and improvement initiatives that guide subsequent lifecycle management activities."]},
{l:"a1",h:"Discover: roles and outputs",n:["The ‘discover’ activity may be carried out by product teams or, in organizations without multi-disciplinary teams, by service owners, business analysts, or relationship managers. The key output is an updated product roadmap, which reflects the latest insights and strategic direction. This output feeds into other product and service management activities and ensure that alignment with organizational strategy is maintained throughout the lifecycle."]},
{l:"a1",h:"Discover: management practices",n:["Key enabling practices that support the ‘discover’ activity include architecture management, risk management, relationship management, and continual improvement. A full list of the management practices and their role in the ‘discover’ activity can be found in the Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a1",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Discover",n:["In line with its strategy for innovation, the company decided to invest in driverless cars, partnering with one of the major automotive companies with a ready driverless car solution.","Due to regulatory constraints across European countries, major American cities have been selected for piloting this new offering."]},
{l:"a2",h:"Design: purpose and metrics of success",n:["The ‘design’ activity translates ideas and requirements into practical guidance for development and delivery. By defining how products and services should function and operate, it helps teams maintain consistency, usability, and alignment with business objectives. Organizations evaluate the effectiveness of design through measures such as stakeholder feedback, quality and completeness of outputs, adherence to standards and roadmaps, and the efficiency of design iterations."]},
{l:"a2",h:"Design: overview",n:["Effective design reduces rework and supports smoother transition into later lifecycle management activities. Digital product design should always include designing the future service interactions and service experience, so it is in fact ‘digital product and service design’. Service interactions may be limited to accessing a product interface or include joint service actions performed by users and service provider representatives."]},
{l:"a2",h:"Design: key terms",n:["The ‘design’ activity produces two key outputs: product/service specifications and prototypes. Specifications define what the solution must deliver, including functional and non-functional requirements, and guide downstream activities while reducing ambiguity. Prototypes are early representations used to test ideas, validate assumptions, and gather feedback from stakeholders. Together, they refine design decisions, reduce risk, prevent rework, and increase confidence that the final solution meets real needs and is operationally viable."]},
{l:"a2",h:"Human-centred design",n:["Human-centred design is a principle that prioritizes users’ needs, capabilities, constraints, and contexts when shaping products and services. Rather than starting from technology or internal preferences, design decisions are informed by how users behave, what they need to accomplish, and the contexts in which products are used. This approach supports inclusive, intuitive experiences that are more likely to be adopted and sustained over time. Human-centred design does not replace other design approaches, but works alongside them."]},
{l:"a2",h:"High-level workflow of the 'design' activity",n:["The ‘design’ workflow shows how validated needs are translated into service solutions. Rather than progressing linearly, ‘design’ allows teams to revisit earlier steps as constraints, risks, or insights emerge. This iterative approach helps reduce downstream risk by addressing issues early, when changes are less costly. The workflow ensures that design decisions remain aligned with service outcomes and lifecycle readiness."]},
{l:"a2",h:"Design: roles and outputs",n:["Design is most effective when carried out by multi-disciplinary product teams that combine diverse skills and perspectives. Product design is not complete without designing the related service(s); service design is difficult and ineffective if performed outside of the product design context. If an organization has separate teams for product development and service management, it is vital that both teams are involved in the product and service design. Some organizations use specialized design teams, which can bring consistency but also risk reducing flexibility and creativity.","The outputs of the ‘design’ activity, including specifications and prototypes, guide development, testing, operations, support, and resource allocation."]},
{l:"a2",h:"Design: management practices",n:["Key enabling practices that support the ‘design’ activity include business analysis and service design. A full list of the management practices and their role in the ‘design’ activity can be found in the Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a3",h:"Acquire: purpose and metrics of success",n:["The ‘acquire activity’ involves sourcing, procurement, and resource management to ensure scalability and sustainability. It may also involve identifying and reallocating technology resources within the organization. Success is gauged by the quality of resources and services, how quickly they are acquired, whether the activity aligns with roadmaps and policies, and how satisfied stakeholders are with both the timelines and the resources provided."]},
{l:"a3",h:"Acquire: overview",n:["Digital products rely on a combination of internal and external technology resources. These resources are required not only to build or modify products, but also to support ongoing operations, service delivery, and support activities, and are typically recorded and managed in a Configuration Management Database (CMDB). The ‘acquire’ activity ensures that these resources are available by sourcing them from external suppliers or reallocating existing internal resources. The activity may be triggered by the development of new or changed products, operational requirements, or regular contract and supplier review cycles."]},
{l:"a3",h:"High-level workflow of the 'acquire' activity",n:["The ‘acquire’ workflow structures how sourcing decisions are made and validated before services move into build and transition. It focuses on ensuring that technology, people, and supplier capabilities are available, suitable, and aligned with service needs. The workflow is iterative. As risks, constraints, or dependencies emerge, teams may return to earlier steps to reassess options or adjust decisions. This helps prevent late surprises and unsupported solutions."]},
{l:"a3",h:"Acquire: roles and outputs",n:["‘Acquire’ is often performed by specialized procurement teams, but budget responsibility may sit with product, project, business, or technology teams. This variety can create confusion, delays, and extra costs if responsibilities are unclear. Clear cost allocation to products helps assign budgets and streamline procurement. The main outputs are the acquired resources and services, which then feed into build, transition, and ongoing operations. Supporting practices include supplier management, financial management, and asset management."]},
{l:"a3",h:"Acquire: management practices",n:["Key enabling practices that support the ‘acquire’ activity include supplier management, service financial management, and IT asset management. A full list of the management practices and their role in the ‘acquire’ activity can be found in the Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a4",h:"Build: purpose and metrics of success",n:["The ‘build’ activity is performed as frequently as needed, triggered by approved solution designs. Success is measured by the quality of solutions, the efficiency of the build cycle, adherence to product roadmaps, and stakeholder satisfaction."]},
{l:"a4",h:"Build: overview",n:["When an iteration of a product design is complete, the product team assesses the design and plans the building and testing activities. When the resources’ availability is confirmed, the building and testing activities are performed as planned. These activities may include software development, system integration, infrastructure or platform configuration, and process development. The products that are built and tested (along with the supporting documentation) are then prepared for transition, either to the organization’s live environment or a marketplace, or to a service consumer."]},
{l:"a4",h:"Build: key terms",n:["In ‘build’, testing and continuous integration are key techniques to ensure reliable outputs.","Testing validates that the outputs meet quality criteria before they progress to ‘transition’. It provides assurance that what has been built works as intended and meets agreed requirements. Continuous integration means developers frequently merge code changes into a shared repository. Together, these practices ensure that the ‘build’ stage produces validated, stable outputs that can safely move into ‘transition’."]},
{l:"a4",h:"High-level workflow of the 'build' activity",n:["The ‘build’ activity’s workflow focuses on constructing, integrating, and validating components in a controlled manner before they are transitioned into live environments. The workflow is iterative. As defects, risks, or changes are identified, teams may return to earlier steps to adjust implementation or resolve issues. This helps ensure quality and readiness before transition."]},
{l:"a4",h:"Build: roles and outputs",n:["The ‘build’ activity can be carried out by multi-disciplinary product teams, specialized developer/infrastructure/testing teams, or temporary project teams. Multi-disciplinary teams are recommended to avoid long cycles and unclear responsibilities. Outputs include built and tested product and service solutions, with full supporting documentation, which are then handed over for transition to live environments, marketplaces, or service consumers."]},
{l:"a4",h:"Build: management practices",n:["Key enabling practices that support the ‘build’ activity include software development and management, infrastructure and platform management, and service validation and testing. A full list of the management practices and their role in the ‘build’ activity can be found in the Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a5",h:"Transition: purpose and metrics of success",n:["Depending on the product architecture, Transition may deploy products and services in an organization’s live environment, a service consumer’s live environment, or a marketplace. Transition also includes onboarding suppliers when they are involved in product operations, service delivery, or product and service support. Offboarding suppliers and decommissioning resources or products are also forms of transition.","Metrics of success include the timeliness and duration of the transition cycle, the impact on service availability and performance, the number and severity of transition errors, adherence to product roadmaps and guidelines, and stakeholder satisfaction with the process and results."]},
{l:"a5",h:"Transition: overview",n:["The transition activity results in new or updated products or services and components deployed to the live environment. Depending on the product and service architecture, it may also make the new or updated services being available to users."]},
{l:"a5",h:"Transition: key terms (1/2)",n:["Deployment, release and change are foundational ‘transition’ terms.","• Deployment moves the components into controlled environment.","• Release represents the packaged outcome of change, bringing together one or more updates in a controlled way.","• Changes range from small adjustments to major updates and must be assessed and authorized to manage risk.","Together, release and change highlight how organizations package and authorize updates before they move into production. They form the basis for understanding how delivery and deployment move updates into the live environment. In some cases, deployment and release are performed simultaneously. In others, services are made available to users later, as part of the ‘deliver’ activity."]},
{l:"a5",h:"Transition: key terms (2/2)",n:["Continuous delivery ensures that software is always in a release-ready state. Deployments are possible at any time, but organizations decide when to release, often to balance business needs and risk. Continuous deployment builds on this by automating the release process. This increases speed and responsiveness but also requires strong testing, monitoring, and governance.","Together, these approaches show how the ‘transition’ activity balances speed with control, ensuring that services remain stable while still evolving quickly."]},
{l:"a5",h:"High-level workflow of the 'transition' activity",n:["The ‘transition’ activity moves built solutions into live use while emphasizing readiness and control and minimizing disruption. It ensures operational readiness, observability, documentation, and ownership. The activity’s outputs feed directly into ‘operate’, ‘deliver’, and ‘support’."]},
{l:"a5",h:"Transition: roles and outputs",n:["Transition may sometimes be handled by product/service teams alone, but more often it requires specialized support from software development, infrastructure engineering, operations, and support teams. In larger or more complex cases, transition is organized as a project involving cross-functional groups. Clear definition of roles avoids confusion.","The outputs of the ‘transition’ activity serve as inputs to the ‘operate’, ‘deliver’, and ‘support’ activities, ensuring continuity across the lifecycle."]},
{l:"a5",h:"Transition: management practices",n:["Key enabling practices that support the ‘transition’ activity include software development and management, infrastructure and platform management, release management, and deployment management. A full list of the management practices and their role in the ‘transition’ activity can be found in the Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a5",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Transition",n:["Once tested and refined, the new services were ready to meet the real world. The ‘transition’ activity would ensure smooth rollout into live operations."]},
{l:"a6",h:"Operate: purpose and metrics of success",n:["The ‘operate’ activity ensures transitioned products, services and resources run as intended in the live environment. It maintains performance, availability, and compliance with organizational policies and guidelines. Key activities include backups, continuity and security testing, monitoring, and event management."]},
{l:"a6",h:"Operate: overview",n:["The exact scope of the ‘operate’ activity for a particular product or a service solution depends on the product/service architecture and design. Modern digital products/services are often designed for highly automated operation. Legacy systems may require manual maintenance. However, if a modern product includes hardware (servers, computers, tablets, payment terminals, IoT devices, and so on), the ‘operate’ activity is likely to involve manual or semi-automated checks, tests, minor updates, and maintenance."]},
{l:"a6",h:"Operate: practices for reliability",n:["Most operations are invisible to customers unless disruptions occur, but lack of proper operations quickly affects service reliability. To strengthen reliability, improve insights, and move from reactive to proactive operations, organizations adopt site reliability engineering and observability. Observability provides insight into system state through metrics, logs, and traces, helping teams identify risks and optimize performance."]},
{l:"a6",h:"High-level workflow of the 'operate' activity",n:["This figure shows the high-level workflow of the ‘operate’ activity. ‘Operate’ is triggered by deployed products, transitioned resources, or onboarded suppliers and runs continuously rather than as a one-time event. Each iteration assesses transitioned solutions and operational requirements, plans activities and confirms resource availability, then executes operational plans and reports status to stakeholders. The outcome is reliable, secure, and observable services, with operational data feeding back into other lifecycle management activities for continual improvement."]},
{l:"a6",h:"Operate: key terms",n:["Both events and incidents are reported as part of the ‘operate’ workflow and can trigger investigation or support actions. As products are run in the live environment, operational status is reported to stakeholders, including any outstanding events, incidents, and trends."]},
{l:"a6",h:"Operate: roles and outputs",n:["The outputs of the operate activity provide visibility into reliability and feed into the ‘deliver’ and ‘support’ activities. The operated products enable service delivery; reported deviations in product operation trigger the ‘support’ activity. Product operations data is a key input to many activities, including ‘discover’, ‘design’, ‘build’, and ‘transition’. Modern applications and platforms are designed to provide plenty of live performance data to inform product design, build, and transition."]},
{l:"a6",h:"Operate: management practices",n:["Several management practices contribute to the ‘operate’ activity. Key practices include monitoring and event management, and infrastructure and platform management. A full list of the involved management practices and their role in the ‘operate’ activity can be found in Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a6",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Operate",n:["Following successful transition, the new services are now live and operation procedures are updated. The team is now doing business as usual."]},
{l:"a7",h:"Deliver: purpose and metrics of success",n:["The ‘deliver’ activity differs from ‘operate’: ‘operate’ ensures products remain in working state, while ‘deliver’ focuses on providing agreed levels of service defined in SLAs. The exact scope of the ‘deliver’ activity for a particular service depends on the product architecture and design and on the service offering and agreement."]},
{l:"a7",h:"Deliver: overview",n:["The scope of the ‘deliver’ activity depends on the nature of the product and service relationship. Some digital products, such as mobile applications, require minimal service interaction beyond monitoring availability and user satisfaction. Other services, especially in corporate environments, involve ongoing activities such as onboarding, request fulfilment, support, and the transfer of equipment or supplies. Product architecture influences the complexity of service delivery and determines whether dedicated delivery teams and additional service actions are required to support effective service consumption."]},
{l:"a7",h:"Deliver: key terms",n:["Both service delivery and its triggers depend on clear agreements and requests. SLAs act as the baseline for measuring delivery success. Service requests are the formal way users initiate agreed service actions under those SLAs. Together, SLAs define the standards, and service requests operationalize them during delivery."]},
{l:"a7",h:"High-level workflow of the 'deliver' activity",n:["The ‘deliver’ activity converts commitments into consistent service experiences by operationalizing SLAs through structured workflows. It begins by assessing commitments and requests, confirming delivery plans and resources, and then executing while reporting progress and gathering feedback. Effective delivery combines automation with human judgment and clear communication. The output is delivered services, supported by performance reports and feedback that enable continual improvement."]},
{l:"a7",h:"Deliver: roles and outputs",n:["The ‘deliver’ activity involves multiple roles. Product teams provide the foundation, while service delivery teams ensure services are carried out in line with SLAs. User support teams and service desk teams handle direct interactions with users and manage service requests.","The outputs include delivered services as well as service performance and user feedback records and reports. These outputs provide visibility into service quality and feed into other lifecycle management activities, including support, discover, and design."]},
{l:"a7",h:"Deliver: management practices",n:["Several management practices contribute to the ‘deliver’ activity. Key practices include service request management and service level management. A full list of the involved management practices and their role in the ‘deliver’ activity can be found in Appendix B of the Official Book."]},
{l:"a8",h:"Support: purpose and metrics of success",n:["All types of incidents and disasters should be addressed: predicted, prevented from occurring, detected, mitigated, resolved, analysed, and prevented from recurring. Which of these actions the organization will be focusing on depends on the anticipated and actual impact of the incident or disaster as well as on some other internal and external factors. The ‘support’ activity minimizes their impact, restoring normal operations and service delivery. It also ensures that user feedback is captured. Success metrics focus on how quickly and effectively disruptions are resolved, while ensuring alignment with SLAs and strategic goals."]},
{l:"a8",h:"Support: overview",n:["The ‘support’ activity focuses on restoring service and minimizing disruption. Automation helps prevent and detect issues early, but some incidents only appear after they occur. Support may involve IT operations, SRE, product teams, and service desk staff, with additional specialists for major incidents or disasters. Collaboration is critical to ensure effective resolution."]},
{l:"a8",h:"Support: key terms",n:["Organizations experience issues that differ in scale, urgency, and impact, ranging from minor service interruptions to major disruptions affecting critical operations. Some issues are caused by underlying weaknesses that repeatedly affect services until their root cause is understood and formally managed. More severe situations may threaten organizational continuity and require emergency response and recovery procedures. Understanding how these situations relate to one another helps support teams restore services, investigate causes, prevent recurrence, and determine when standard processes are sufficient or when exceptional actions are required."]},
{l:"a8",h:"High-level workflow of the 'support' activity",n:["The ‘support’ activity is triggered by operational incidents or disasters affecting live services. Issues are first identified and assessed to determine impact and urgency, generating assessment records. Teams then act to restore normal operations using fixes, workarounds, or recovery procedures. Finally, actions are reviewed and documented in incident reports to capture lessons learned. The outcome is restored service and improved resilience across the lifecycle."]},
{l:"a8",h:"Support: roles and outputs",n:["Support involves cross-functional collaboration. Different teams address incidents depending on context. The outputs of the ‘support’ activity (restored normal product operation and service delivery) ensure service continuity while also contributing to continual improvement and prevention efforts. Post-mortem reports serve as an important input to the discover, design, build, transition, operate, and deliver activities."]},
{l:"a8",h:"Support: management practices",n:["Several management practices contribute to the ‘support’ activity. Key practices include service continuity management and incident management. A full list of the involved management practices and their role in the ‘deliver’ activity can be found in Appendix B of the Official Book.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m7a",h:"Module 7 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m7a",h:"Introduction to value streams",n:["Value streams reflect how work is actually performed within an organization, not just how processes are designed. ITIL emphasizes that workflows evolve according to organizational context, stakeholder needs, and complexity. Because every organization combines lifecycle management activities, practices, and resources differently, value streams must be continually identified, mapped, analysed, and improved to optimize value creation and reduce waste."]},
{l:"m7a",h:"Why value streams",n:["ITIL explains that value streams show how work is actually performed to create value. Mapping value streams helps organizations visualize workflows, identify improvement opportunities, and adapt processes to real-world conditions and stakeholder needs."]},
{l:"m7a",h:"Types of value streams: core and enabling",n:["Organizations identify value streams based on their operating model and scope. Core value streams directly enable value for consumers, while enabling value streams support internal activities such as procurement, onboarding, or operational support. A value stream may be core in one context and enabling in another."]},
{l:"m7b",h:"Value stream mapping",n:["Value stream mapping helps organizations visualize the flow of work, information, and value across activities. It supports shared understanding, identifies bottlenecks and waste, and enables continual improvement of value streams."]},
{l:"m7b",h:"Steps of value stream mapping",n:["Review Appendix E in the Official Book. It explains how value stream mapping moves from understanding the current state to designing and implementing improvements. The analysis step focuses on identifying value-adding activities, bottlenecks, and waste to optimize the flow of work and information."]},
{l:"m7b",h:"Value stream mapping vs. management",n:["Value stream mapping visualizes and analyses value streams at a point in time. Value stream management is an ongoing approach focused on continual improvement of how work flows across the organization.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"Module 8 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"What is continual improvement?",n:["Continual improvement is built into every part of the organization, from strategy to daily operations. Everyone involved in managing products and services should always look for opportunities to improve. Continual improvement applies across the entire ITIL Value System, including products, services, service components, practices, and relationships. This makes continual improvement a foundation for ensuring the organization adapts and continues to meet stakeholder expectations in a changing environment."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"Need for continual improvement",n:["Continual improvement is a core activity within the ITIL Value System. Its purpose is to keep the value system and all its components adaptable, effective, and aligned with organizational goals.","To support this, ITIL provides three enablers:","• The ITIL Continual Improvement Model: a structured approach to implementing improvements.","• The ITIL Continual Improvement Official Practice Guide: supporting day-to-day product and service enhancements.","• The ITIL Transformation Official Book: enabling improvements to the ITIL VS and its components.","Continual improvement is also reinforced by the ITIL Guiding Principles. These principles help shape an organizational culture of collaboration and cooperation, breaking down silos and embedding improvement as a natural way of working."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"ITIL Continual Improvement Model",n:["The ITIL Continual Improvement Model provides a structured, repeatable approach to improving products, services, and practices across the value system. It guides organizations through clarifying purpose, assessing the current state, defining a target state, and planning next steps. Designed for ordered and complex contexts, it promotes evidence-based decisions, experimentation, and regular progress review. Repeated use embeds continual improvement into everyday work and aligns change with business goals.","Slow incident resolution times","• Step 1: What is the vision? Deliver faster, more efficient incident resolution to improve customer satisfaction and reduce downtime.","• Step 2: Where are we now? Current average resolution time is 10 hours; many incidents are reassigned multiple times before closure.","• Step 3: Where do we want to be? Reduce average resolution time to under 6 hours within the next quarter.","• Step 4: How do we get there? Analyse incident data to identify recurring issues, improve knowledge base access, and provide refresher training for support staff.","• Step 5: Take action. Update the incident categorization process and implement a new escalation workflow.","• Step 6: Are we getting there? After four weeks, resolution time decreased to 7 hours, but reassignment rates remain high. Additional team training planned.","• Step 7: How do we keep the improvements relevant? Schedule monthly reviews of incident data to adjust categories and training needs, ensuring continual improvement in resolution times."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"What is the vision?",n:["This step (1) of the Continual Improvement Model defines the vision for the improvement initiative. The organization’s goals and objectives are translated into clear direction for the relevant team or unit. Stakeholders should agree the expected value of the improvement and understand who is responsible and authorized to move the initiative forward. Skipping this step can reduce alignment, visibility, and organizational support."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"Where are we now?",n:["This step (2) defines the current state, or Point A, before moving toward the target state. Assessments may include user perceptions of value, competencies and skills, workflows and processes, organizational culture, and technology capabilities. Where possible, objective measurements should establish a reliable baseline, supported by stakeholder feedback and stories to provide context. Without this baseline, it becomes difficult to measure progress or evaluate the impact of improvements."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"Where do we want to be?",n:["This step (3) defines the target state, or Point B, of the improvement journey. Sometimes the target can be described with SMART objectives, Critical Success Factors (CSFs), and Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). In more complex or uncertain situations, the best that can be defined is a direction of progress, supported by qualitative criteria. The target state is about progress towards the vision, not necessarily full achievement. If this step is skipped, the initiative lacks a clear destination or direction, making it difficult to plan improvements or demonstrate value to stakeholders."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"How do we get there?",n:["At this step (4), the organization creates a plan for moving from the current state towards the target state. When the path forward is clear, the plan can be direct and straightforward. When it is less certain, exploratory experiments may be required to identify the most promising approach. Regardless of the level of certainty, improvement work is best carried out iteratively. Each iteration provides an opportunity to check progress, re-evaluate, and change directions if appropriate. This ensures improvements remain adaptive and aligned to the vision."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"Take action",n:["This step (5) is where the agreed plan and any experiments are executed. Activities may be performed sequentially, simultaneously, or independently depending on the situation. Agile-style approaches often involve experimentation, iteration, and revisiting earlier steps where needed. The focus should remain on objectives rather than rigidly following a single plan. Progress should be measured continuously, with risks actively managed and visibility maintained. Once completed, the resulting state becomes the baseline for the next cycle of continual improvement."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"Are we getting there?",n:["This step (6) evaluates the new current state to determine if the improvement has met its objectives and whether those objectives are still relevant. Success cannot be assumed; it must be validated. Progress should be assessed against agreed objectives, CSFs, and KPIs where available. If results fall short or circumstances change, further actions or new iterations may be required. Where SMART objectives cannot be defined, the focus should be on direction of progress and whether the new state is demonstrably better than before. If this step is skipped, organizations risk assuming success where there is none, missing opportunities to course-correct, and losing valuable lessons from the improvement effort."]},
{l:"m8a",h:"How do we keep the improvements relevant?",n:["Step 7 ensures improvements are embedded and sustained within the organization. Organizational change management and knowledge management help integrate new ways of working and reduce the risk of reversion. If expected results are not achieved, lessons learned should be documented and shared. Transparency and learning from deviations support future improvement efforts and help embed continual improvement within the organizational culture. If this step is skipped, improvements may remain isolated, revert over time, or fail to secure support for future initiatives.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m9a",h:"Module 9 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m9a",h:"A holistic approach to service management",n:["The ITIL Four Dimensions represent perspectives which are relevant to all levels, including individual product design, management practices, and the organization’s value system as a whole."]},
{l:"m9a",h:"The ITIL Four Dimensions of Product and Service Management",n:["The ITIL Four Dimensions provide a holistic lens for understanding how services are designed, delivered, and governed. Each dimension influences how experience is created in practice: through organizational structures and capabilities, the technology and data that support interactions, the contributions and constraints of partners and suppliers, and the processes and workflows that guide work. All dimensions work together and none can be considered in isolation.","The Four Dimensions are constrained or influenced by several external factors that are often beyond the control of the organization. These factors are illustrated in line with the PESTLE model. The Four Dimensions and external factors should be considered holistically and collectively, in no specific order or hierarchy."]},
{l:"m9a",h:"Recap: Key learning points",n:["This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Organizations and people",n:["The effectiveness of an organization cannot be assured by a formally established structure or system of authority alone. The organization also needs a culture that supports its objectives and the right level of capacity and competency among its workforce. It is vital that the leaders of the organization champion and advocate values, which motivate people to work in desirable ways. Ultimately, however, it is the way in which an organization performs its work that creates shared values and attitudes. These values and attitudes over time are considered the organization’s culture."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Culture",n:["An organization’s success relies on a culture that supports its objectives and develops workforce capability and competency. In rapidly changing digital environments, organizations must continually improve while managing increasing economic, social, and political impacts. Building a strong safety culture encourages trust, openness, and shared responsibility for risk management. When employees feel valued rather than blamed, they are more likely to report risks and concerns early, helping prevent failures and supporting better outcomes for all stakeholders."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Leadership",n:["Leaders shape culture by acting with integrity, inspiring people, and aligning priorities with vision. They model values, foster learning, and build trust. This is leadership guided by a vision and driven by values that target not only the success of the organization but also the well-being of all stakeholders. Conscious leaders speak with integrity, lead with authenticity, and hold themselves accountable. They listen with the intent to understand and not just to respond, and they achieve this by being in tune with themselves and the world around them."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Organizational structure and design",n:["Organizational structure is a component of business architecture. It should align with the organization’s vision and strategic goals while enabling effective delivery of products and services. Categories describe how structures vary, while core elements are always present."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Conway's law and digital design",n:["Communication structures within an organization influence the design of its systems and services. This relationship is known as Conway’s Law, named after Melvin E. Conway, who introduced the concept in 1967. To address this, many organizations adopt the reverse Conway approach, intentionally shaping team structures to support the desired product and service architecture. Small, cross-functional teams with end-to-end responsibility help organizations create modular, flexible solutions that evolve independently. This improves collaboration, speeds up decision-making and delivery, and strengthens alignment between business objectives, technology design, and customer value."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Skills and competencies",n:["To create and manage products and services that meet evolving consumer needs and leverage technology, organizations must develop competencies in their people and teams.","• Creativity: the ability to think innovatively and develop novel solutions or ideas.","• Attention to detail: being thorough and accurate in carrying out tasks.","• Problem-solving: this includes the ability to analyse situations, apply logical reasoning, brainstorm solutions, make decisions, and think creatively to overcome obstacles.","• Critical thinking: this involves analysing information objectively, logical reasoning, observation, and questioning assumptions.","• Teamwork/collaboration: this involves working effectively with others, coordinating tasks, managing conflict within a group, sharing ideas, and respecting diverse opinions.","• Interpersonal skills: these relate to building relationships, empathy, networking, diplomacy, and effective social interaction.","• Work ethic: this covers traits like responsibility, discipline, commitment, punctuality, and professionalism.","• Emotional intelligence: often encompassing self-awareness, empathy, and managing relationships effectively.","• Communication: this encompasses verbal and written communication, active listening, presentation skills, negotiation, and providing constructive feedback.","• Adaptability/flexibility: this refers to the capacity to embrace and adjust to change, self-manage, and learn new things.","• Time management: this involves skills like prioritizing tasks, planning, meeting deadlines, and organizing work effectively.","• Leadership: this includes motivating others, delegating tasks effectively, mentoring colleagues, and making sound decisions.","• Stress management: the ability to remain calm and effective under pressure."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"Organizations, people and AI",n:["Data analytics helps organizations better understand stakeholder perceptions, evaluate the performance of teams, products, and services, and identify risks or areas requiring greater management attention. By analysing patterns, trends, and deviations, organizations can make more informed strategic decisions and respond proactively to changing conditions. AI increasingly acts as a collaborative partner by supporting repetitive and data-intensive activities, allowing people to focus on strategic, creative, and relationship-based work such as stakeholder engagement, decision-making, and continual improvement across the organization."]},
{l:"m9b",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Organization and People",n:["This scenario is used to demonstrate the organizations and people dimension in practice."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"Information and technology",n:["Information and technology are the core components of digital products and services. They also play a crucial role in effective product development and service management. The wide use of cloud, followed by rapid adoption of generative AI, provides many opportunities, but also creates challenges related to ethics, governance, and compliance. Organizations should ensure effective data governance to enable the use of AI technologies and support the effective and responsible use of information."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"Technology",n:["The technologies that support product and service management include, but are not limited to, workflow management systems, knowledge bases, inventory systems, communication systems, and analytical tools.","Increasingly, organizations benefit from advances in artificial intelligence, machine learning, and cognitive computing, which are applied at all levels of management, from strategic planning and portfolio optimization to development, testing, monitoring, and user support. Mobile platforms, cloud solutions, remote collaboration tools, and automated testing and deployment have also become common practice. The ITIL Official Practice Guides provide recommendations on automation and practical advice for its application across practices. Refer to Table D.1 of the Official Book for more information on the automation tools."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"Artificial Intelligence",n:["Artificial intelligence is now one of the most influential technologies in digital product and service management. It helps organizations manage complexity, automate repetitive tasks, and make smarter, faster decisions. AI and big data are reshaping how services are designed and managed, driving automation, adaptability, and real-time responsiveness. AI strengthens human judgment by providing insights and automating routine tasks, often using deep learning and natural language processing. Generative AI has already had a major impact, with vendors embedding it into tools for development, deployment, support, monitoring, and risk management. The ITIL AI Capability Model groups AI capabilities into six areas, though in practice, many real-world applications combine multiple functions."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"AI challenges and governance",n:["AI tools are only as good as the data they are trained on. Data quality challenges intensify with the use of AI, creating a new level of demand for quality and control. This has increased the importance of effective data governance and management. Generative AI is already embedded into tools for development, deployment, support, monitoring, and risk management. Emergence of GenAI and its fast commoditization have raised many concerns regarding governance, ethics, and compliance."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"Data, information, and knowledge",n:["The data-information-knowledge categorization does not fully reflect the non-linear and iterative nature of knowledge creation. The same content may be viewed as data, information, or knowledge depending on the context and the perspective of the person interpreting it. Although often presented as a progression, the boundaries between these categories are fluid and shaped by feedback, reinterpretation, and use. Information creates value only when it supports goals and stakeholder outcomes, while effective knowledge relies on strong governance to ensure data quality and availability."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"Data governance",n:["Data governance is critical because it ensures data remains trustworthy and usable as an organizational asset. It links governance directly to business strategy, clarifies who is responsible for data decisions, and provides standards that make practices consistent and auditable. A strong governance framework also promotes openness in decision-making, while remaining flexible enough to adapt to new technologies, regulations, and business needs. In practice, this means organizations can balance compliance, security, and innovation while maintaining confidence in the quality and availability of their data."]},
{l:"m9c",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Information and technology",n:["This scenario is used to demonstrate the information and technology dimension in practice."]},
{l:"m9d",h:"Partners and suppliers",n:["The third dimension of product and service management, and of all ITIL practices, is partners and suppliers. Unlike the organizations and people dimension, which focuses on internal resources, this dimension highlights the role of third parties and external providers in value creation."]},
{l:"m9d",h:"Working with partners and suppliers",n:["The partners and suppliers dimension examines how organizations work with external organizations to support value creation throughout the product and service lifecycle. Suppliers may provide specialized expertise, technology, resources, or additional capacity, while also helping organizations share responsibilities and risks. Their involvement can range from supporting specific activities to taking partial or full responsibility for managing products and services. These relationships vary in integration and formality and are governed through contracts, partnerships, and other agreements that define expectations and responsibilities."]},
{l:"m9d",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Partners and suppliers",n:["This scenario is used to demonstrate the partners and suppliers dimension in practice."]},
{l:"m9e",h:"Value streams and processes",n:["The ‘value streams and processes’ dimension focuses on how work actually flows through an organization to create value for service consumers and other stakeholders. It looks beyond individual roles or technologies and instead examines what activities are performed, how they are connected, and how efficiently they enable outcomes."]},
{l:"m9e",h:"Optimizing workflows for complexity",n:["Organizations do not operate in a single, uniform context. Different types of work exhibit different levels of predictability, and the relationship between actions and outcomes varies accordingly. Applying the same methods to every situation, regardless of context, often leads to failure rather than consistency. In ITIL, work contexts are described using four broad categories based on predictability and cause-and-effect relationships:","• Ordered contexts are predictable environments where cause-and-effect relationships are known or can be discovered through analysis. Established procedures, standards, and best practices are effective because following the correct steps reliably produces expected outcomes. For example, baking a cake is an ordered activity: if the recipe is followed correctly, the result is predictable. Many traditional business processes and repeatable projects operate in ordered contexts.","• Complex contexts are unpredictable, and cause-and-effect relationships can only be understood in hindsight. Outcomes emerge through interaction, experimentation, and adaptation rather than detailed upfront planning. Raising a child is a useful analogy: the same approach may lead to different results depending on the situation. Many organizational, social, and market environments are complex adaptive systems. However, not everything within a complex system is necessarily complex; for example, while an organization as a whole is complex, a well-planned, repeatable project within it may still operate in an ordered context.","• Chaotic contexts represent crisis situations where there is no clear relationship between cause and effect. Immediate action is required to stabilize the situation, and analysis and planning are secondary to decisive intervention. A fire in a building illustrates a chaotic context: the priority is immediate response to restore safety, not detailed analysis.","• Confused contexts occur when it is unclear which type of context applies. This is often the starting point when facing a new or unfamiliar situation. The priority is to assess the nature of the context and determine whether it is ordered, complex, or chaotic before selecting an appropriate approach. This distinction is critical, as methods that work well in one context may fail in another; for example, detailed planning that is effective in ordered contexts can cause paralysis in chaotic situations, while experimental approaches suitable for complex contexts may be unnecessary in ordered environments.","Recognizing these contexts is critical because methods that work well in one context often fail in another. Rigid processes and detailed plans may succeed in ordered situations but become ineffective or harmful in complex or chaotic environments. ITIL guidance and value chain workflows are therefore not intended to be copied directly into organizations. They must be adopted and adapted based on situational needs. Effective product and service management requires both structured guidance and the flexibility to adjust, experiment, and deviate when conditions demand it."]},
{l:"m9e",h:"ITIL Car Rental | Value streams and processes",n:["This scenario is used to demonstrate the value streams and processes dimension in practice."]},
{l:"m10a",h:"Module 10 | Syllabus",n:["This slide outlines the key syllabus topics that will be covered in this module.","This slide outlines the key learning points that will be covered in this module."]},
{l:"m10a",h:"Integrating ITIL with DevOps",n:["No single framework is sufficient in fast-moving digital environments. ITIL provides structure and governance for managing digital products and services across the ITIL Value System. DEVOPS INSTITUTE® certifications contribute to practical methods such as automation, collaboration, continuous development, and rapid feedback to enable continuous delivery and operation. DevOps techniques support lifecycle management activities including discover, design, acquire, build, transition, and operate. Together, they balance stability and speed to deliver resilient, adaptive, customer-focused services."]},
{l:"m10a",h:"Project management in ITIL",n:["Effective project management is essential because many changes to digital products and services are delivered through structured projects. ITIL provides foundational guidance on project and portfolio management, but more complex or large-scale initiatives often require the depth, structure, and governance provided by comprehensive project-management methodologies. Project management ensures change is delivered in a controlled, predictable, and strategically aligned manner, and project outputs must transition seamlessly into operational services within the ITIL Value System."]},
{l:"m10a",h:"Integrating ITIL with PRINCE2",n:["No single framework is sufficient in fast-moving digital environments. PRINCE2 provides structured governance for projects, programmes, and portfolios, enabling controlled delivery of change. ITIL focuses on digital product and service management across the value chain, ensuring value realization in live operation. PRINCE2 governs implementation and change, while ITIL manages, supports, and continually improves services. Together, they align strategy, transformation, and operations to deliver sustained value."]},
{l:"m10a",h:"Shared principles across frameworks",n:["ITIL, PRINCE2, and DevOps can work together in a transparent and collaborative way. PRINCE2® certifications support structured project, programme, and portfolio management, while DEVOPS INSTITUTE® certifications strengthen agile and continuous product development and operation. Together, they extend ITIL by enabling delivery of change at different scales and speeds. For ITIL professionals, these complementary domains enhance versatility and support end-to-end value creation.","This slide outlines the key learning points that were covered in this module."]},
{l:"m10b",h:"Exam information",n:["As part of the certification process, you will need to complete an exam.","The exam will test both your recall of information about ITIL Foundation (Version 5) and your understanding of the concepts.","Note that achievement of ITIL Foundation (Version 5) is a prerequisite for the ITIL® Practice Manager, ITIL® Managing Professional, and ITIL® Strategic Leader designations."]},
{l:"m10b",h:"Prepare for your exams!",n:["Your Official Training Materials include a Learner Workbook, a Quick Reference Guide, along with exam related assets (Syllabus, Sample papers, Glossary, questionnaires, and so on). You will use these throughout the training modules, as well as during exam preparation."]},
{l:"m10b",h:"What's next?",n:["Register for your exam. After successfully passing, claim your digital badge through PeopleCert Plus by logging into your PeopleCert account and activating your free subscription. Your digital badge demonstrates certification and can be shared on social media and email signatures. This training also qualifies for PeopleCert Plus Continuing Professional Development points, required to maintain your badge. Remember to log in and claim your points. Thank you for learning with us."]},
{l:"m10b",h:"Tell Us What You Think. We Are Listening.",n:["Here is your opportunity to give PeopleCert some feedback on the course materials."]}
];

